const DATA = [{"n": "01", "name": "Start here: the AI engineering map", "lessons": [{"id": "01.01", "t": "What AI engineering is", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/what-ai-engineering-is", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Chip Huyen: AI Engineering (O'Reilly, 2025)", "https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/ai-engineering/9781098166298/", "The book that defined the field: building apps on top of foundation models."], ["Latent Space: The Rise of the AI Engineer", "https://www.latent.space/p/ai-engineer", "swyx's essay that named the role and its split from ML engineering."], ["Anthropic: Building effective agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents", "What production LLM systems actually look like."]], "q": "How is an AI engineer's job different from an ML engineer's? Give two concrete differences.", "n": ["AI engineering means building products on top of pretrained foundation models instead of training models from scratch.", "The model is a dependency you call over an API or host yourself. Most of the work is around it: prompts, context, retrieval, tools, evaluation, cost and latency.", "ML engineering starts from data and a training loop. AI engineering starts from a product need and a model that already mostly works.", "Because outputs are open-ended text, evaluation is the hard part. You can't check a paragraph with accuracy on a test set.", "The core loop: prototype with a strong model, build an eval set, then optimise prompt, context, model choice and serving.", "You still need to understand internals (attention, KV cache, quantization) to reason about cost, latency and failure modes."], "ak": "", "a": "## A new kind of engineering\nUntil around 2022, using machine learning in a product meant collecting data, training a model and serving it. Foundation models changed the order. A single pretrained model can now summarise, classify, write code and answer questions with no task-specific training. Chip Huyen defines AI engineering as the process of building applications on top of these models. swyx's essay \"The Rise of the AI Engineer\" argued the role would become as common as the backend engineer, because the skills needed are closer to software engineering than to research.\n\n## What changes compared with ML engineering\n| Aspect | ML engineering | AI engineering |\n| Starting point | A dataset and a training objective | A product need and a pretrained model |\n| Main lever | Model architecture and training data | Prompts, context, retrieval, tools, model choice |\n| Output | Usually a label or a number | Open-ended text, code, images, actions |\n| Evaluation | Accuracy, F1 on a held-out set | Rubrics, LLM judges, human review, task success |\n| Cost driver | Training compute | Inference: tokens in and out, per request |\n\nThe model is now a dependency. You can call it through an API or host an open-weights model, but in both cases most of your engineering effort goes into everything around it.\n\n## The work around the model\n- **Prompt and context**: what instructions and information the model sees on each call.\n- **Retrieval**: finding the right documents to put in the context (embeddings, vector search).\n- **Tools and agents**: letting the model call functions, read results and decide what to do next.\n- **Evaluation**: deciding whether a change made the system better or worse.\n- **Serving and optimisation**: latency, throughput and cost, which is where internals like the KV cache and quantization matter.\n\n%%flow The usual order of work: start with the best model, measure, then optimise.\nProduct need -(prototype)-> Strong model + prompt -(measure)-> *Eval set -(iterate)-> Context, retrieval, tools -(optimise)-> Cheaper / faster model\n%%\n\n## Why internals still matter\nYou can ship a demo without knowing what attention is. You can't explain why a 100k-token prompt is slow and expensive, why a quantized model gets worse at maths, or why your agent forgets an instruction from early in the conversation. This course goes from the application layer (embeddings, retrieval) into the model (transformers, attention, KV cache), out to optimisation (quantization, distillation, speculative decoding) and back up to reasoning and agents.\n\n## What the job looks like day to day\nA useful way to see AI engineering is to follow one feature from idea to production. Say a support team wants a \"suggest a reply\" button.\n\n1. **Frame the task.** Write down the input (the ticket thread plus customer record), the output (a draft reply under 150 words, in the company tone) and what \"good\" means (correct facts, no promises the policy forbids, a human edits it less than half the time).\n2. **Build a baseline in an afternoon.** One prompt, one model call, ten real tickets pasted in by hand. Read every output. Most of what you learn about the problem comes from this step.\n3. **Collect an eval set.** 50 to 200 real tickets with notes on what a good reply contains. This is the test suite you will run after every change.\n4. **Add context.** The baseline invents refund rules, so you retrieve the relevant policy sections (RAG, module 3) and the last three orders.\n5. **Harden.** Structured output so the UI can parse it, a timeout and fallback model, logging of every prompt and response, and a check that blocks replies containing a refund amount.\n6. **Measure in production.** Track the edit distance between the draft and what the agent actually sent. That number tells you whether the next change helped.\n\nNone of these steps trains a model. Every one of them decides whether the feature works.\n\n## The failure modes are different\nTraditional software fails loudly: an exception, a 500, a failing test. An LLM feature fails quietly. It returns fluent, confident text that is wrong 8% of the time, and nobody notices until a customer complains. That changes how you build:\n\n- **Tests become evals.** You can't assert an exact string. You score outputs with rules, reference answers, or a second model acting as a judge, and you look at the pass rate across many cases.\n- **Changes are statistical.** A prompt edit that fixes three examples may break five others. You only know by re-running the whole set.\n- **Behavior drifts without a deploy.** A provider updates a model alias, or users start asking a new kind of question, and quality moves while your code is unchanged. Pin model versions and keep sampling production traffic into your evals.\n\n! Example: a prompt change that looked like a win\n! You add \"Always apologise for the inconvenience\" to the system prompt. On the five tickets you tried, replies feel warmer.\n! The 120-case eval shows the pass rate fell from 81% to 74%: replies to billing questions now open with an apology and run past the 150-word limit, and three replies apologised for an outage that never happened.\n! Without the eval set you would have shipped it.\n\n> Takeaway: AI engineering is building products around a pretrained model. The hard parts are context, evaluation and cost, and understanding the model's internals is what lets you reason about all three.", "s": "ML engineers mostly train and tune models from data; AI engineers start from a pretrained foundation model and adapt it with prompts, retrieval, tools and sometimes fine-tuning. Evaluation is also different: ML work measures accuracy on labelled data, while AI engineering has to judge open-ended outputs with rubrics, LLM judges or human review. A third difference is cost: inference per token dominates instead of training compute."}, {"id": "01.02", "t": "The AI engineering stack", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/the-ai-engineering-stack", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Chip Huyen: Building A Generative AI Platform", "https://huyenchip.com/2024/07/25/genai-platform.html", "Layer-by-layer walk through a production GenAI platform."], ["a16z: Emerging Architectures for LLM Applications", "https://a16z.com/emerging-architectures-for-llm-applications/", "Reference stack: data pipelines, embeddings, vector DBs, orchestration."], ["Chip Huyen: AI Engineering (O'Reilly, 2025)", "https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/ai-engineering/9781098166298/", "Chapter 1 splits the stack into application, model and infrastructure layers."]], "q": "Name the three layers of the AI stack and give one component you'd find in each.", "n": ["Chip Huyen splits the stack into three layers: application development, model development and infrastructure.", "Application layer: prompts, context construction, retrieval, tools, guardrails, evaluation, the user interface.", "Model layer: choosing, fine-tuning, distilling and quantizing models; datasets for adaptation.", "Infrastructure layer: inference serving, GPUs, vector databases, caching, monitoring.", "Most teams spend most of their time in the application layer and buy the other two as services.", "A production platform grows in steps: plain model call, then context, then guardrails, router, cache, and finally agent loops with write actions."], "ak": "", "a": "## Three layers\nChip Huyen's book describes the AI stack as three layers. Almost every tool you will hear about fits in one of them.\n\n%%layers The AI engineering stack, top to bottom. Most product work happens at the top.\n*Application | prompts, context and retrieval, tools, guardrails, evals, UI\nModel | model choice, fine-tuning, distillation, quantization, datasets\nInfrastructure | inference serving, GPUs, vector stores, caches, monitoring\n%%\n\nThe application layer changed the most when foundation models arrived. Before, a team needed its own model and infrastructure before it could build anything. Now a team can call a hosted model and spend all its time on the top layer.\n\n## Growing a platform step by step\nIn \"Building A Generative AI Platform\", Huyen describes how a system grows from a single API call:\n1. **Plain call**: user query goes to the model, response comes back.\n2. **Enhance context**: retrieve documents or call tools and put the results in the prompt (RAG).\n3. **Guardrails**: check inputs for prompt injection and personal data, check outputs for format errors, toxicity and hallucination.\n4. **Router and gateway**: send easy queries to a cheap model and hard ones to a strong model; one gateway handles keys, fallbacks and rate limits.\n5. **Cache**: reuse answers for repeated or similar queries, and reuse the processed prompt prefix (prompt caching).\n6. **Agent patterns**: loops, branching, and tools that write (send an email, change a database).\n\nObservability (logs, traces, metrics) and orchestration run across all of these steps.\n\n%%flow A typical request path once a few layers are in place.\nQuery -(check)-> Input guardrail -(route)-> *Router -(context)-> Retriever -(prompt)-> Model -(check)-> Output guardrail -(cache)-> Answer\n%%\n\n## Where the a16z reference fits\na16z's \"Emerging Architectures for LLM Applications\" draws the same picture from the data side: data pipelines feed an embedding model, embeddings go into a vector database, an orchestration layer assembles prompts, and an LLM API or hosted open model answers. Their main point is that in-context learning (putting the right data in the prompt) was the default pattern, with fine-tuning used much less often.\n\n## Buy or build at each layer\n- **Model**: closed API (fast to start, less control) vs open weights (control, privacy, more ops work).\n- **Vector store**: a Postgres extension is often enough; a dedicated vector DB pays off at large scale or with heavy filtering.\n- **Serving**: managed endpoints vs your own vLLM or TensorRT-LLM cluster.\n\n## Put numbers on the stack\nEach layer you add costs latency and money, and each one should earn its place. The figure below models a typical request mix. Move the cache hit rate and the share of traffic you can route to a small model, and watch which layer pays for itself.\n\n%%widget stack Monthly cost of a feature as you add a cache, a router, retrieval and a guardrail.\n\nThree patterns show up again and again:\n\n- **Caching wins first.** If 30% of questions repeat (common for FAQs and internal tools), a cache in front of the model removes 30% of the spend with almost no quality risk.\n- **Routing wins big, but needs an eval.** Sending easy requests to a model that costs a tenth as much can cut the bill in half. You need a classifier or rule for \"easy\", and an eval that proves the small model is good enough on that slice.\n- **Context is the silent cost.** Retrieval that stuffs 10 chunks of 500 tokens into every call adds 5,000 input tokens per request. At scale that often costs more than the model's answer.\n\n## What each layer owns\n| Layer | Owns | Typical failure if you skip it |\n| Application | Prompts, context assembly, evals, UX, guardrails | Plausible but wrong answers reach users unchecked |\n| Model | Choice of model, fine-tunes, adapters | Paying for a frontier model where a small one would do |\n| Infrastructure | Serving, batching, caching, rate limits, monitoring | Latency spikes and runaway bills under load |\n\nMost teams start at the top and move down only when a number forces them to. Self-hosting a model (the infrastructure layer) makes sense when you have steady high volume, strict data rules, or a fine-tuned open model; below a few million tokens a day an API is usually cheaper once you count engineer time.\n\n> Takeaway: think of the stack as application, model and infrastructure. Start with a plain model call and add context, guardrails, routing, caching and agents only when a requirement asks for them.", "s": "Application layer: prompts, retrieval/context construction, guardrails, evaluation (e.g. a RAG pipeline). Model layer: model selection, fine-tuning, distillation, quantization (e.g. a LoRA fine-tune). Infrastructure layer: inference serving, GPUs, vector databases, caching and monitoring (e.g. vLLM or a vector DB)."}, {"id": "01.03", "t": "One model call, end to end", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/one-model-call-end-to-end", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Hugging Face: LLM inference optimization", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/llm_optims", "Prefill vs decode, KV cache and static caches."], ["Anyscale: How continuous batching enables 23x throughput", "https://www.anyscale.com/blog/continuous-batching-llm-inference", "What happens on the server while your request waits."], ["OpenAI tiktoken", "https://github.com/openai/tiktoken", "A fast BPE tokenizer you can try on your own prompts."]], "q": "Your chat request has a 2,000-token prompt and gets a 300-token answer. Describe what the server does, and which part dominates time-to-first-token.", "n": ["Text is split into tokens by a tokenizer (e.g. BPE). Pricing, context limits and latency are all measured in tokens.", "Tokens become embedding vectors and flow through the transformer layers.", "Prefill: the whole prompt is processed in one parallel pass, filling the KV cache. This sets time-to-first-token (TTFT).", "Decode: one new token per forward pass, each reading the KV cache. This sets time-per-output-token and is memory-bandwidth bound.", "At every step the model outputs a probability distribution over the vocabulary; a sampler (temperature, top-p) picks the next token.", "Generation stops at an end-of-sequence token, a stop sequence or max_tokens. The server batches many users' requests together to keep GPUs busy."], "ak": "", "a": "## From text to tokens\nA model never sees characters. A tokenizer splits text into tokens using a fixed vocabulary, typically built with byte-pair encoding (BPE). Common words are one token; rare words split into pieces. In English a token averages around four characters, or about three quarters of a word. Everything is counted in tokens: the context window, the price, and the latency. Try OpenAI's tiktoken on your own prompt and you will see that code, numbers and non-English text often use more tokens than you expect.\n\n## The request path\n%%seq One chat completion from the client's point of view.\nClient > API server: POST prompt\nAPI server > Tokenizer: text\nTokenizer -> API server: token ids\nAPI server > GPU: prefill (whole prompt)\nGPU -> API server: first token\nAPI server > GPU: decode step, repeat\nGPU -> Client: stream tokens\n%%\n\n## Prefill and decode\nInference has two phases with very different costs.\n- **Prefill**: the model processes all prompt tokens at once. It is a big parallel matrix multiplication, so it is compute-bound and uses the GPU well. While doing this it stores each layer's keys and values in the **KV cache**. Prefill time is most of your **time to first token (TTFT)**.\n- **Decode**: the model generates one token per forward pass. Each pass reads all the weights and the whole KV cache from GPU memory but does little maths, so it is memory-bandwidth bound. Its speed is the **time per output token (TPOT)**.\n\nTotal latency is roughly TTFT + output tokens x TPOT. That's why long outputs are slow even when prompts are short, and why long prompts hurt TTFT.\n\n## Choosing the next token\nAfter each forward pass the model produces logits, one score per vocabulary entry. Softmax turns them into probabilities, and a sampler picks one:\n- **Greedy** takes the top token.\n- **Temperature** sharpens (below 1) or flattens (above 1) the distribution.\n- **Top-p** keeps the smallest set of tokens whose probability adds up to p and samples from that.\nThe chosen token is appended and the loop continues until an end-of-sequence token, a stop string, or the max_tokens limit.\n\n## What the server does around you\nYour request shares a GPU with others. Servers use **continuous batching**: new requests join the batch at every decode step instead of waiting for the whole batch to finish. Anyscale measured up to 23x throughput gains from this. The cost is that your TPOT depends on how busy the server is.\n\n! Example: 2,000-token prompt, 300-token answer\n! Prefill handles 2,000 tokens in one pass, maybe 100-300 ms on a modern GPU. That is your TTFT.\n! Decode then runs 300 sequential steps. At 30 ms per token that is 9 seconds, far longer than prefill.\n! So for this request, output length drives total latency; prompt length drives the wait before streaming starts.\n\n## Try it: tokens and time\nType into the box to see how text breaks into tokens. Notice that common English words are one token, while rare words, numbers, dates and non-English text split into several pieces. You pay per token and the model reads per token, so this split matters for both cost and behavior (it's why models used to miscount letters in \"strawberry\").\n\n%%widget tokenizer Approximate tokenization of any text you type.\n\nThe second figure splits one request into its phases. Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel and is fast per token. Decode generates one token at a time and is slow per token. Raise the prompt length and the time to first token grows; raise the output length and total time grows much faster.\n\n%%widget latency Where the time goes in one model call: network, queue, prefill, decode.\n\n## Worked numbers\nRules of thumb that hold for current hosted models:\n\n- English text averages about 4 characters, or 0.75 words, per token. A 1,000-word document is about 1,300 tokens.\n- Prefill runs at thousands of tokens per second on a GPU, because every prompt token is processed at once.\n- Decode runs at roughly 30 to 150 tokens per second per user, because each new token needs a full pass through the model.\n\n! Example: why the answer length dominates latency\n! A request with a 3,000-token prompt and a 500-token answer, on a server doing prefill at 5,000 tokens per second and decode at 60 tokens per second per user.\n! Prefill: 3,000 / 5,000 = 0.6 s. Decode: 500 / 60 = 8.3 s. Add 0.2 s of network and queueing.\n! Time to first token is about 0.8 s, total time about 9.1 s. Halving the prompt saves 0.3 s; halving the answer saves 4.2 s. Ask for shorter outputs before you optimize prompts, and stream tokens so users see progress after 0.8 s instead of 9.\n\n> Takeaway: a model call is tokenize, prefill the prompt into the KV cache, then decode one token at a time with a sampler. Prefill sets time to first token; output length sets total time.", "s": "The server tokenizes the prompt, then runs prefill: all 2,000 tokens go through the model in one parallel pass, filling the KV cache and producing the first token, so prefill dominates TTFT. Then decode runs 300 sequential forward passes, each sampling one token from the output distribution and appending to the KV cache, streaming tokens back until EOS or max_tokens. Decode dominates total latency."}]}, {"n": "02", "name": "Embeddings and retrieval", "lessons": [{"id": "02.01", "t": "From data to vectors", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/how-are-vectors-constructed", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Jay Alammar: The Illustrated Word2vec", "https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-word2vec/", "How training on context turns words into vectors."], ["Sentence Transformers documentation", "https://www.sbert.net/", "The standard library for sentence and document embeddings."], ["Reimers & Gurevych: Sentence-BERT (2019)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10084", "Siamese BERT networks that made sentence embeddings practical."]], "q": "Explain how a sentence becomes a single embedding vector in a modern embedding model, and why the same model must be used at index and query time.", "n": ["An embedding is a fixed-length list of numbers (e.g. 384, 768, 1536 dimensions) that represents meaning.", "Early methods: one-hot and TF-IDF vectors are sparse and know nothing about meaning. Word2vec learned dense word vectors by predicting neighbouring words.", "Modern embedding models are transformer encoders. Each token gets a contextual vector; pooling (mean or [CLS]) turns them into one vector.", "They are trained with contrastive learning: pull matching pairs (question, answer) together, push unrelated pairs apart.", "Images, audio and code can be embedded the same way. CLIP puts images and text into one shared space.", "Vectors from different models live in different spaces. Index and query with the same model and version, or re-embed everything."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why vectors\nComputers compare numbers easily and meaning hardly at all. An embedding turns a piece of data (a word, a sentence, an image) into a fixed-length vector so that similar things end up close together. Once data is vectors, \"find related documents\" becomes \"find nearby points\".\n\n## From sparse to dense\n- **One-hot**: one dimension per vocabulary word. Huge, sparse, and every word is equally far from every other.\n- **TF-IDF / BM25**: weights words by how distinctive they are. Good for keyword match, blind to synonyms (\"car\" vs \"automobile\").\n- **Word2vec (2013)**: Jay Alammar's illustrated guide shows the trick. Train a small network to predict a word's neighbours; the learned weights become dense word vectors where king - man + woman lands near queen.\n- **Contextual embeddings**: transformers give each token a vector that depends on the sentence, so \"bank\" in \"river bank\" and \"bank account\" differ.\n\n## How a sentence embedding is built\n%%flow A sentence embedding model in one line.\nText -(tokenize)-> Token ids -(encoder)-> Token vectors -(mean pool)-> *Sentence vector -(normalize)-> Unit vector\n%%\n\nSentence-BERT (Reimers and Gurevych, 2019) showed that plain BERT vectors are poor for similarity, and fixed it by fine-tuning BERT in a siamese setup so that pooled vectors of similar sentences have high cosine similarity. Modern embedding models keep this recipe and train contrastively on huge sets of pairs: a query and its relevant passage are pulled together while other passages in the batch are pushed apart.\n\n## Choosing dimensions and models\n| Model size | Typical dimensions | Notes |\n| Small (MiniLM class) | 384 | Fast, runs on CPU, fine for many apps |\n| Base | 768 | Good default |\n| Large / API models | 1024-3072 | Better quality, more storage and slower search |\n\nSome newer models support Matryoshka training, where the first k dimensions are already a usable embedding, so you can truncate vectors to save space.\n\n## Rules that save you later\n- Use the **same model and version** for documents and queries. Vectors from two models are in unrelated spaces.\n- Store which model produced each vector; a model upgrade means re-embedding the corpus.\n- Embed chunks of a sensible size (a few hundred tokens). A whole book in one vector averages its meaning away.\n\n## See meaning become arithmetic\nOnce text is a vector, relationships between meanings become directions in space. The classic demonstration from word2vec (Mikolov et al., 2013) is that the vector for \"king\" minus \"man\" plus \"woman\" lands closest to \"queen\". The figure below uses small hand-made vectors to show the idea: pick a word, subtract one concept, add another, and see which word is nearest to the result.\n\n%%widget analogy Vector arithmetic on a toy word space.\n\nModern sentence embedding models don't guarantee clean analogies like this, but the principle carries over: similar meanings end up near each other, and consistent differences (formal versus casual, question versus answer, English versus French) tend to line up as directions.\n\n## What an embedding call looks like\n```\nPOST /v1/embeddings\n{\"model\": \"text-embedding-3-small\", \"input\": [\"How do I reset my password?\"]}\n\n-> {\"data\": [{\"embedding\": [0.0123, -0.0442, 0.0871, ... 1536 numbers]}]}\n```\n\nThat one call gives you a fixed-length vector no matter how long the input was (up to the model's limit, often 512 to 8,192 tokens). You store it next to the original text and an id.\n\n## Where people go wrong\n- **Mixing models.** Vectors from two different models live in different spaces. A query embedded with model B against documents embedded with model A gives garbage similarities, not an error. Store the model name and version with every vector.\n- **Embedding too much at once.** A 20-page document squashed into one vector averages away the detail. Chunk first (lesson 03.05), then embed each chunk.\n- **Forgetting the query side.** Some models (E5, BGE, Nomic and others) expect a prefix such as \"query: \" or \"search_query: \" on questions and a different one on documents. Skipping it quietly costs recall.\n\n! Example: cost of embedding a knowledge base\n! 200,000 support articles, averaging 600 tokens each, is 120 million tokens.\n! At an illustrative $0.02 per million tokens, the one-off embedding run costs about $2.40. Storage at 1,536 float32 dimensions is 200,000 × 1,536 × 4 bytes ≈ 1.2 GB.\n! Embedding is cheap; the expensive parts come later, in storage, indexing and re-embedding when you change models.\n\n> Takeaway: an embedding model is an encoder plus pooling, trained so that related inputs land near each other. Pick one model and keep it consistent for indexing and querying.", "s": "The text is tokenized, a transformer encoder produces a contextual vector per token, and a pooling step (usually mean pooling or the [CLS] token) collapses them into one fixed-length vector, often L2-normalized. The model was trained contrastively so related texts are close. Each model defines its own vector space, so query vectors are only comparable with document vectors from the same model and version."}, {"id": "02.02", "t": "Vector embeddings and semantic space", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/vector-embeddings-semantic-space", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Pinecone: What are vector embeddings", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/vector-embeddings/", "Intro to embeddings and the geometry of meaning."], ["Google ML Crash Course: Embeddings", "https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/embeddings", "Embedding spaces, distance and visualisation."], ["Pinecone: Vector similarity explained", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/vector-similarity/", "Euclidean, cosine and dot product compared."]], "q": "When is cosine similarity equivalent to dot product, and why do most embedding pipelines normalize vectors?", "n": ["An embedding space is a geometry where distance stands for difference in meaning.", "Directions can carry meaning (gender, tense, topic) but individual dimensions are usually not interpretable.", "Three common metrics: Euclidean (L2) distance, cosine similarity (angle only) and dot product (angle and length).", "If all vectors are normalized to length 1, cosine and dot product give identical rankings, and L2 gives the same order too.", "Use the metric the model was trained with. Most text embedding models expect cosine or normalized dot product.", "Semantic search finds paraphrases that keyword search misses, but can miss exact terms like product codes. That is why hybrid search exists."], "ak": "", "a": "## Meaning as geometry\nIn an embedding space, every item is a point. Items with similar meaning sit near each other, and some directions line up with concepts: moving from \"walk\" to \"walked\" is roughly the same step as from \"swim\" to \"swam\". Google's crash course shows this with projections of word vectors. The individual dimensions usually have no human meaning; only distances and directions do.\n\n## Measuring closeness\n| Metric | What it measures | Range |\n| Euclidean (L2) | Straight-line distance | 0 to infinity, smaller is closer |\n| Cosine similarity | Angle between vectors, ignores length | -1 to 1, larger is closer |\n| Dot product | Angle and length together | Unbounded, larger is closer |\n\nPinecone's guide gives the rule of thumb: use the metric the embedding model was trained with.\n\n## Why normalize\nIf every vector has length 1, then:\n- cosine(a, b) = a . b, so cosine and dot product are identical;\n- ||a - b||^2 = 2 - 2 (a . b), so L2 ranking is the same too.\nNormalizing once at write time lets the index use the fastest operation (a dot product) with no change in results. Leaving vectors unnormalized under dot product means longer vectors win more often, which can bias results toward certain documents.\n\n## What semantic space is good and bad at\n%%flow Same query, two kinds of search.\n[Keyword] \"reset my password\" -(BM25)-> Docs containing \"reset\" + \"password\"\n[Semantic] \"reset my password\" -(embed)-> *Nearby vectors -> \"Recover account access\" too\n%%\n\nSemantic search wins on paraphrases and synonyms. It loses on exact identifiers (error code E1234, SKU numbers, names), where a keyword index is sharper. Production systems often run both and merge results (hybrid search), covered later in the module.\n\n## Drag a query through the space\nThe figure is a 2D slice of a semantic space with a handful of documents. Drag the query point and watch the ranking change. Switch the metric between cosine, dot product and Euclidean distance, and toggle normalization.\n\n%%widget simspace Ranking documents by cosine, dot product or Euclidean distance.\n\nTwo things to notice:\n\n- With **normalized vectors**, all three metrics give the same order. Cosine similarity, dot product and Euclidean distance are then just different scales of the same angle.\n- Without normalization, **dot product favors long vectors**. A document whose vector happens to be long can outrank a closer-in-meaning document. Most embedding APIs return unit-length vectors for exactly this reason; check yours before choosing a metric.\n\n## How close is close?\nCosine scores are not probabilities, and their range depends on the model. With many modern models, unrelated texts still score 0.6 to 0.7, and near-duplicates score above 0.9. That makes a fixed cut-off like \"only use results above 0.8\" fragile:\n\n| Pair (illustrative, one model) | Cosine |\n| \"reset my password\" vs \"I forgot my password\" | 0.91 |\n| \"reset my password\" vs \"change account email\" | 0.78 |\n| \"reset my password\" vs \"pasta recipes\" | 0.62 |\n\nThe numbers shift when you switch models, so calibrate thresholds on your own labelled pairs and re-check them after any model change.\n\n## Where geometry fails\nEmbeddings capture topical similarity well and logical structure poorly. \"Flights from Delhi to Paris\" and \"flights from Paris to Delhi\" are almost identical vectors. So are \"the drug is safe in pregnancy\" and \"the drug is not safe in pregnancy\". Exact identifiers (error code E-4012, SKU 88-1203) also embed badly, because the model has never learned what they mean. That is why production search combines vectors with keyword search and filters, covered in 02.06.\n\n> Takeaway: embedding spaces turn meaning into distance. Normalize vectors and use cosine or dot product as the model expects, and remember that semantic similarity is not exact match.", "s": "Cosine similarity equals dot product when both vectors have unit length, since cosine is the dot product divided by the product of norms. Pipelines normalize so the cheapest operation (dot product) gives cosine rankings, L2 ordering also matches, and vector length no longer biases results."}, {"id": "02.03", "t": "Matching storage to your workload", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/choosing-the-right-db", "k": [], "x": ["pgvector"], "r": [["pgvector on GitHub", "https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector", "Vector search inside Postgres: HNSW and IVFFlat indexes."], ["Pinecone: What is a vector database", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/vector-database/", "What a dedicated vector DB adds over a library or extension."], ["Faiss on GitHub", "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss", "The in-process library many vector databases build on."]], "q": "You have 2 million product descriptions in Postgres with price and category filters. Would you add pgvector or a dedicated vector DB? Justify with workload numbers.", "n": ["Three options: an in-process library (Faiss, hnswlib), a vector extension on your existing DB (pgvector), or a dedicated vector database (Milvus, Qdrant, Weaviate, Pinecone).", "Decide with workload facts: number of vectors, dimensions, QPS, update rate, filter complexity, latency target, and whether data already lives in a relational DB.", "Memory estimate: vectors x dims x 4 bytes. 10M x 768 float32 is about 30 GB before index overhead.", "pgvector is enough for up to a few million vectors with moderate QPS, and keeps transactions and joins with your other data.", "A dedicated vector DB pays off with hundreds of millions of vectors, high QPS, heavy metadata filtering, multi-tenancy or tiered storage.", "A library has no persistence or updates out of the box; fine for batch jobs and prototypes."], "ak": "", "a": "## Three ways to store vectors\n%%layers From least to most infrastructure.\nLibrary | Faiss, hnswlib, Annoy: in-process, you handle persistence and updates\n*Extension | pgvector, Elasticsearch / OpenSearch kNN: vectors next to existing data\nDedicated vector DB | Milvus, Qdrant, Weaviate, Pinecone: distributed, filtering, tenancy\n%%\n\nPinecone's explainer lists what a database adds over a raw index: CRUD on vectors, metadata filtering, horizontal scaling, backups, access control and real-time updates. Faiss, by contrast, is a library: very fast, but you manage persistence, sharding and updates yourself.\n\n## Questions that decide it\n- **How many vectors, how many dimensions?** Raw size = N x d x 4 bytes for float32. 1M x 768 is about 3 GB. 100M x 768 is about 300 GB, which will not fit one machine's RAM without compression.\n- **QPS and latency?** Tens of QPS at 50 ms is easy for anything. Thousands of QPS at p99 under 20 ms needs a tuned, sharded system.\n- **Update rate?** Bulk nightly rebuilds suit a library. Continuous inserts and deletes need a DB that handles them.\n- **Filters?** \"Nearest vectors where category = shoes and price < 50\" is hard; how well the store combines filters with the ANN index matters a lot.\n- **Where does the rest of the data live?** If it's already in Postgres, keeping vectors there avoids a second system and keeps everything in one transaction.\n\n## pgvector in practice\npgvector adds a vector column type, distance operators and two index types: IVFFlat and HNSW. You can join vector results with normal tables and filter with SQL. Limits show up at larger scale: index builds on tens of millions of rows take a long time and a lot of memory, and filtered search can return too few rows when the filter is very selective, because the index finds neighbours first and filters after.\n\n! Example: 2 million product descriptions\n! 2M x 768 dims x 4 bytes is about 6 GB raw, plus HNSW overhead: fits comfortably on one Postgres server.\n! Filters (price, category) are ordinary columns; queries join with stock and pricing tables.\n! Choose pgvector. Revisit a dedicated DB only if the catalogue grows past tens of millions or QPS reaches thousands.\n\n## Size it before you choose\nMost storage decisions come down to one number: how many bytes your vectors take and whether that fits in memory. Set your vector count, dimensions and format below.\n\n%%widget vecmem RAM needed for raw vectors plus index, and how many machines it takes.\n\nThe arithmetic is simple and worth doing by hand once:\n\n- Raw size = vectors × dimensions × bytes per number. float32 is 4 bytes, float16 is 2, int8 is 1, binary is 1/8.\n- An HNSW index adds the graph: roughly M × 2 neighbor ids of 4 bytes each per vector at the bottom layer. With M = 16 that is about 128 bytes per vector, small next to a 1,536-dim float32 vector (6,144 bytes).\n- Leave headroom. Index builds, updates and the operating system need memory too; plan to fill no more than 60 to 70% of RAM.\n\n! Example: 50 million product embeddings\n! 50 M × 1,024 dims × 4 bytes = 205 GB of raw float32 vectors. With HNSW overhead and headroom you need about 300 GB of RAM.\n! That is three or four large machines, or one if you store int8 vectors (51 GB raw) and keep float32 copies on disk for rescoring.\n! The same data at 10 M vectors fits in Postgres with pgvector on one server, which is often the right call.\n\n## A decision rule\n- Under about 5 to 10 million vectors, and you already run Postgres: start with pgvector. One database, transactions, joins with your other tables, one backup story.\n- Tens to hundreds of millions, high query rates, or heavy filtering: a dedicated vector database (Qdrant, Milvus, Weaviate, Pinecone and others) with sharding and quantization built in.\n- Billions, mostly cold data: disk-based indexes such as DiskANN, or object storage with a small hot index in memory.\n\nPick on measured recall, latency and cost at your size, not on benchmarks run on someone else's data.\n\n> Takeaway: size the workload first (vectors x dimensions x 4 bytes, QPS, updates, filters). Use the database you already have until those numbers say otherwise.", "s": "2M x 768 floats is about 6 GB, which fits on one Postgres node with an HNSW index. Data and filters (price, category) already live in Postgres, so pgvector keeps joins, transactions and one system to operate. A dedicated vector DB is only justified at hundreds of millions of vectors, thousands of QPS, heavy filtered search or multi-tenant isolation."}, {"id": "02.04", "t": "Vector compression", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/vector-compression", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Pinecone: Product Quantization", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/faiss/product-quantization/", "Subvectors, codebooks and the memory math."], ["Jegou et al.: Product Quantization for Nearest Neighbor Search (2011)", "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5432202", "The original PQ paper."], ["Hugging Face: Embedding Quantization", "https://huggingface.co/blog/embedding-quantization", "Binary and int8 embeddings with rescoring."]], "q": "A 768-dim float32 vector is compressed with PQ into 96 subvectors with 256 centroids each. What is the new size and compression ratio?", "n": ["Vectors are big: a 768-dim float32 vector is 3 KB. At 100M vectors that is 300 GB, too much for RAM.", "Compression trades a little recall for much less memory and faster distance maths.", "Dimensionality reduction (PCA, Matryoshka truncation) keeps fewer dimensions.", "Product quantization (PQ) splits a vector into m subvectors and replaces each with the id of its nearest centroid. With 256 centroids, each id is one byte.", "Distances are computed from precomputed lookup tables (asymmetric distance), so search on codes is fast.", "Typical pattern: search compressed vectors to get candidates, then rescore the top few hundred with full-precision vectors."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why compress\nMemory is the main cost of vector search. A 768-dimension float32 vector is 768 x 4 = 3,072 bytes. A billion of them is 3 TB. Even 100 million is 300 GB, more RAM than most single servers have. Compression shrinks each vector so the index fits in memory, and as a bonus, smaller vectors mean faster distance calculations because less data moves through the CPU cache.\n\n## Ways to make vectors smaller\n- **Fewer dimensions**: PCA, or Matryoshka-trained models where you can keep the first 256 of 1024 dimensions.\n- **Scalar quantization**: store each number in fewer bits (int8 or even 1 bit). Covered in the next lesson.\n- **Product quantization (PQ)**: replace chunks of the vector with codebook ids.\n\n## How product quantization works\nPinecone's PQ guide walks through it:\n1. Split each D-dim vector into m equal subvectors (768 dims into 96 pieces of 8).\n2. For each subvector position, run k-means on the training data to learn k centroids (usually 256).\n3. Encode each subvector as the id of its nearest centroid. With k = 256 the id fits in one byte.\n4. The vector is now m bytes instead of D x 4 bytes.\n\n%%flow Product quantization of one vector.\n768 floats (3 KB) -(split)-> 96 subvectors of 8 -(nearest centroid)-> *96 one-byte codes -(store)-> 96 bytes\n%%\n\nAt query time the query is not compressed. For each subvector position the system precomputes the distance from the query piece to all 256 centroids, a small table. The distance to any stored vector is then the sum of m table lookups. This is called asymmetric distance computation, and it's very fast.\n\n## The cost: recall\nEach vector is now approximated by a mix of centroids, so distances are approximate and some true neighbours get ranked lower. Common fixes:\n- **Rescoring**: fetch the top 100-1,000 candidates with PQ, then recompute exact distances with the original vectors (stored on disk or SSD).\n- **More subvectors** (larger m): better accuracy, less compression.\n- **Combine with an index**: Faiss's IVF-PQ clusters vectors first and stores PQ codes per cluster.\n\n## Product quantization, interactively\nThe figure compresses one vector with product quantization (PQ). Change the number of sub-vectors and bits per code and watch the size and the reconstruction error.\n\n%%widget pq Splitting a vector into sub-vectors and replacing each with a codebook id.\n\nStep by step, for a 768-dim vector with m = 96 sub-vectors and 8-bit codes:\n\n1. Split the vector into 96 pieces of 8 dimensions each.\n2. For each of the 96 positions, run k-means on a training sample to learn 256 centroids (8 bits gives 2^8 = 256). That is the codebook.\n3. Replace each piece with the id of its nearest centroid: one byte.\n4. The vector is now 96 bytes instead of 768 × 4 = 3,072 bytes, a 32× reduction.\n\n## Why search still works on codes\nAt query time you don't decompress every vector. For each of the 96 positions you compute the distance from the query piece to all 256 centroids once, giving a 96 × 256 lookup table. The distance to any stored vector is then 96 table lookups and additions, with no multiplications. This is called asymmetric distance computation, because the query stays exact and only the database is compressed. It is why PQ indexes can scan millions of vectors per second on a CPU.\n\n## Choosing parameters\n| Setting | Higher means | Cost |\n| m (sub-vectors) | Finer pieces, better accuracy | More bytes per vector |\n| Bits per code | More centroids per piece | Bigger lookup tables, slower training |\n| Training sample | Better codebooks | Longer build |\n\nA common starting point is 8 bits per code and m chosen so that each piece has 4 to 16 dimensions. Measure recall@10 against exact search on a few hundred real queries; if it drops below what your product needs, raise m or rescore the top candidates with full vectors.\n\n> Takeaway: compression trades a small amount of recall for large memory savings. PQ turns a vector into a handful of bytes, and rescoring the top candidates with full vectors wins most of the recall back.", "s": "Each of the 96 subvectors is stored as one byte (256 centroids = 8 bits), so the vector becomes 96 bytes. The original is 768 x 4 = 3,072 bytes, so the compression ratio is 32x (plus a small shared codebook)."}, {"id": "02.05", "t": "Compression and quantization", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/compression-quantization---scaling-vectors-efficiently", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Hugging Face: Embedding Quantization", "https://huggingface.co/blog/embedding-quantization", "int8 and binary embeddings, speedups and retained accuracy."], ["Qdrant: Binary Quantization", "https://qdrant.tech/articles/binary-quantization/", "1-bit vectors with oversampling and rescoring."], ["Weaviate: Vector quantization", "https://docs.weaviate.io/weaviate/concepts/vector-quantization", "PQ, BQ and SQ in one vector DB."]], "q": "Compare int8 scalar quantization and binary quantization on size, speed and accuracy for 1024-dim vectors.", "n": ["Scalar quantization (SQ): map each float32 value to int8 using the value range per dimension. 4x smaller, small accuracy loss.", "Binary quantization (BQ): keep only the sign of each value, 1 bit per dimension. 32x smaller, distance becomes Hamming distance (XOR + popcount), very fast.", "Binary loses more accuracy; works best with high-dimensional models and is usually paired with oversampling and rescoring.", "Hugging Face reports binary retrieval keeping around 92-96% of float32 quality after rescoring, with large speedups.", "Pattern: search the small vectors, oversample (e.g. 4x top-k), rescore with int8 or float32 vectors.", "Quantize vectors (storage) is different from quantizing model weights (module 7), but the trade-off is the same: bits for accuracy."], "ak": "", "a": "## Fewer bits per number\nProduct quantization (previous lesson) compresses chunks of a vector. Scalar and binary quantization compress each number on its own. They are simpler, need little or no training, and are built into most vector databases.\n\n## Scalar quantization: float32 to int8\nFor each dimension, find the range of values in your data (min and max, or a percentile to ignore outliers). Map that range linearly onto 256 integer buckets. Each number now takes 1 byte instead of 4.\n- **Size**: 4x smaller.\n- **Speed**: integer SIMD maths is faster than float maths.\n- **Accuracy**: usually a small loss, often below one percent of recall for good embedding models.\n\n## Binary quantization: 1 bit per dimension\nKeep only whether each value is positive or negative. A 1024-dim vector becomes 128 bytes.\n- **Size**: 32x smaller than float32.\n- **Speed**: distance becomes Hamming distance: XOR the two bit strings and count the ones. CPUs do this in a couple of instructions.\n- **Accuracy**: larger loss. Qdrant notes that it works best with high-dimensional embeddings (1,000+ dims), where enough information survives.\n\n| Method | Bytes for 1024 dims | Distance | Typical accuracy |\n| float32 | 4,096 | Dot product | Baseline |\n| int8 (SQ) | 1,024 | Integer dot product | Very close to baseline |\n| Binary (BQ) | 128 | Hamming | Lower; good after rescoring |\n\n## Oversample, then rescore\n%%flow The standard recipe for binary vectors.\nQuery -(binary search)-> *Top 4k candidates -(rescore with int8 / float32)-> Top k results\n%%\n\nThe Hugging Face embedding quantization post shows this: binary search to get many candidates fast, then rescore them with higher-precision vectors kept on disk. They report retrieval quality around 92-96% of float32 with big speed and memory gains. Qdrant exposes the same as an \"oversampling\" factor plus a rescore flag.\n\n## Picking a level\n- Under a few million vectors: float32 is often fine; memory is cheap at that scale.\n- Tens of millions: int8 is the easy default.\n- Hundreds of millions or more, or cost-sensitive: binary or PQ with rescoring.\n- Always measure recall@k on your own queries before and after.\n\n## Measure it live\nThe figure below runs a real experiment in your browser: 3,000 clustered 64-dim vectors, 40 queries, and recall@10 measured against exact float32 search. Switch between int8 and binary, change the oversampling factor and toggle rescoring.\n\n%%widget binq Recall of int8 and binary quantization, with and without oversample-and-rescore.\n\nWhat to look for:\n\n- **int8** keeps recall close to 100% at 4× less memory. The error from rounding each number to one of 256 levels is small compared with the gaps between neighbors.\n- **Binary** alone loses a lot of recall at 32× less memory. One bit per dimension keeps only the sign.\n- **Binary plus rescoring** wins most of it back: fetch 4× or 10× more candidates using cheap binary distances, then re-rank only those with full-precision vectors.\n\n## How int8 scaling works\nFor each dimension (or for the whole vector set), find the range of values seen in a sample, for example -0.21 to 0.19. Map that range linearly to -128 to 127. A value of 0.05 becomes round((0.05 - (-0.21)) / 0.40 × 255) - 128 = 38. Values outside the sampled range get clipped, which is why you calibrate on representative data, not on a handful of vectors.\n\n## Binary in practice\nBinary distance is Hamming distance: XOR the two bit strings and count the ones. CPUs do this with a single popcount instruction per 64 bits, so a 1,024-dim binary vector is compared in 16 instructions. Binary works best on high-dimensional embeddings (1,024 dims and up) from models trained with quantization in mind; on small or low-dimensional models the recall loss can be too large even with rescoring.\n\n! Example: the memory plan for 100 M vectors at 1,024 dims\n! float32 everywhere: 410 GB in RAM.\n! Binary index in RAM (12.8 GB) plus int8 vectors for rescoring on fast SSD (102 GB): fetch the top 100 by Hamming distance, rescore with int8, return the top 10.\n! RAM need drops by about 30×, and recall@10 typically stays in the mid to high 90s. Check on your own data before relying on that figure.\n\n> Takeaway: int8 gives 4x savings almost for free; binary gives 32x but needs oversampling and rescoring to keep quality.", "s": "int8 SQ stores 1024 dims in 1,024 bytes (4x smaller than float32), uses fast integer dot products, and loses very little accuracy. Binary stores 1 bit per dim, 128 bytes (32x smaller), uses Hamming distance via XOR and popcount (fastest), but loses more accuracy, so you oversample candidates and rescore them with higher-precision vectors."}, {"id": "02.06", "t": "Vector search", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/vector-search", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Pinecone: k-NN vs ANN", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/faiss/vector-indexes/", "Flat, LSH, HNSW and IVF indexes compared on speed and recall."], ["ann-benchmarks", "https://ann-benchmarks.com/", "Recall vs queries-per-second for real ANN libraries."], ["Elastic: Hybrid search with reciprocal rank fusion", "https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/rrf.html", "Merging keyword and vector results."]], "q": "Why can't you just do exact k-NN over 50 million vectors per query, and what metric do you use to judge an approximate index?", "n": ["Vector search returns the k items nearest to a query vector.", "Exact (brute-force, flat) search compares the query with every vector: perfect recall, cost grows linearly with N.", "Approximate nearest neighbour (ANN) search skips most vectors with an index, trading a little recall for orders of magnitude less work.", "Measure with recall@k (share of true top-k returned) against latency or QPS. ann-benchmarks plots exactly this.", "Most ANN indexes have a knob (efSearch, nprobe) that moves along the recall/speed curve at query time.", "Hybrid search merges vector results with keyword (BM25) results, often with reciprocal rank fusion."], "ak": "", "a": "## The problem\nGiven a query vector q and a set of N stored vectors, return the k closest. That's it. The difficulty is doing it in milliseconds when N is in the millions or billions.\n\n## Exact search\nA flat index computes the distance from q to every vector and keeps the best k. Recall is perfect. Cost is N x d multiply-adds per query.\n\n! Example: 50 million vectors, 768 dims\n! One query = 50M x 768, about 38 billion multiply-adds, and 150 GB of float32 data to read.\n! Even at high memory bandwidth that is a large fraction of a second per query on one machine, so a few QPS at best.\n! Exact search is fine for a few hundred thousand vectors, or as a ground truth to test an ANN index.\n\n## Approximate search\nANN indexes avoid looking at most vectors. Pinecone's index guide covers the main families:\n- **Clustering (IVF)**: group vectors into clusters, only scan the few clusters nearest the query.\n- **Graphs (HNSW)**: hop through a graph of neighbours toward the query.\n- **Hashing (LSH)**: hash so that nearby vectors collide.\n- **Quantization (PQ)**: often combined with the above to cut memory.\n\nThe next lesson goes inside IVF and HNSW.\n\n## Judging an index\n| Metric | Meaning |\n| recall@k | Of the true top k, how many did the index return |\n| Latency (p50/p99) | Time per query |\n| QPS | Queries per second per machine |\n| Memory | Bytes per vector including index overhead |\n| Build time | How long indexing takes, and whether updates are cheap |\n\nann-benchmarks.com plots recall against QPS for many libraries. Every index has a curve, and the query-time knob (HNSW's efSearch, IVF's nprobe) picks a point on it.\n\n## Hybrid search\n%%flow Combining keyword and vector results.\n[Keyword] Query -(BM25)-> Ranked list A\n[Vector] Query -(ANN)-> Ranked list B\n[Fusion] List A + List B -(RRF)-> *Final ranking\n%%\n\nReciprocal rank fusion (RRF), as in Elasticsearch, scores each document as the sum of 1 / (k + rank) over the lists it appears in. It needs no score calibration and handles the case where vector search misses exact terms.\n\n## Why exact search stops scaling\nExact search computes the distance from the query to every stored vector. It is perfectly accurate and simple, and its cost grows linearly. The figure estimates how long one exact query takes, limited by how fast the machine can stream vectors from memory.\n\n%%widget flatcost Time for one brute-force query as the collection grows.\n\nUp to about a million vectors, brute force on a modern CPU or GPU is often fast enough, and it never misses. Past that, approximate indexes trade a little recall for orders of magnitude less work.\n\n## Fusing keyword and vector results\nHybrid search runs a keyword search (BM25) and a vector search, then merges the two ranked lists. The scores are on different scales, so you can't just add them. Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) ignores scores and uses ranks:\n\n```\nscore(doc) = sum over lists of 1 / (k + rank_in_that_list)      with k = 60 by default\n```\n\nA document ranked 1st by keywords and 4th by vectors scores 1/61 + 1/64 = 0.0320. One ranked 2nd in only one list scores 1/62 = 0.0161. Agreement between the two lists beats a single high rank. Change k in the figure to see how a smaller k rewards top ranks more strongly.\n\n%%widget rrf Merging a keyword ranking and a vector ranking with Reciprocal Rank Fusion.\n\n## Measuring an index\n- **Recall@k**: of the true top k neighbors (from exact search), how many did the index return? Aim for 0.95 or higher for most RAG use.\n- **Latency at p50 and p99**: the tail matters, because one slow shard slows every query that touches it.\n- **Queries per second at that recall**: an index tuned for 0.99 recall may serve half the traffic of one tuned for 0.95.\n\nAlways report the three together. A latency number without the recall it was measured at means nothing.\n\n> Takeaway: exact search is linear and too slow at scale. ANN indexes trade a few points of recall for huge speedups; always judge them by recall@k against latency, on your own data.", "s": "Exact k-NN must compute distances to all 50M vectors per query (tens of billions of operations and reading ~150 GB of floats), giving high latency and very low QPS. ANN indexes skip most vectors. They're judged by recall@k (fraction of the true top-k found) versus latency/QPS and memory, measured against exact search as ground truth."}, {"id": "02.07", "t": "Indexing techniques that make vector search scale", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/indexing-techniques---making-vector-search-scale", "k": [], "x": ["HNSW", "IVF"], "r": [["Malkov & Yashunin: HNSW (2016)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09320", "Hierarchical navigable small world graphs."], ["Pinecone: HNSW explained", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/faiss/hnsw/", "Layers, M, efConstruction and efSearch with diagrams."], ["Faiss wiki: Guidelines to choose an index", "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Guidelines-to-choose-an-index", "Which index for which size and memory budget."]], "q": "Explain how HNSW finds a nearest neighbour, and name the parameters that trade recall for speed and memory.", "n": ["IVF (inverted file): k-means splits vectors into nlist clusters. A query scans only the nprobe closest clusters.", "IVF is memory-efficient and pairs well with PQ (IVF-PQ), but needs training and recall drops at cluster borders.", "HNSW builds a layered graph: few nodes and long links on top, all nodes and short links at the bottom.", "Search starts at the top layer, greedily moves to the closest neighbour, drops down a layer, and repeats.", "HNSW knobs: M (links per node: recall and memory), efConstruction (build quality), efSearch (query-time recall vs speed).", "HNSW is the common default for in-memory search; disk-based graphs like DiskANN handle billion-scale on SSDs."], "ak": "", "a": "## Two families dominate\nMost production vector search uses one of two index types, or a combination with quantization.\n\n## IVF: search a few clusters\n1. **Train**: run k-means on a sample to get nlist centroids (for example 4,096).\n2. **Assign**: put every vector in the list of its nearest centroid.\n3. **Query**: find the nprobe centroids closest to the query (say 16) and scan only those lists.\n\n%%flow IVF scans a small fraction of the data.\nQuery -(nearest centroids)-> *nprobe of nlist clusters -(scan)-> Candidates -(sort)-> Top k\n%%\n\nWith nlist = 4,096 and nprobe = 16 you scan about 0.4% of vectors. Raising nprobe increases recall and cost. The weak spot: a true neighbour sitting just across a cluster boundary is missed unless you probe more clusters. IVF combines naturally with PQ (IVF-PQ in Faiss), which is how billion-scale indexes fit in RAM.\n\n## HNSW: navigate a graph\nMalkov and Yashunin's HNSW builds a graph where each vector links to its nearby vectors, organised in layers like a skip list. The top layers are sparse with long-range links; layer 0 has every vector.\n\n%%layers Search drops through the layers, getting more local each time.\nLayer 2 | a handful of nodes, long jumps across the space\nLayer 1 | more nodes, medium jumps\n*Layer 0 | every vector, short links to close neighbours\n%%\n\nSearch starts at an entry point on the top layer, greedily moves to whichever neighbour is closest to the query, and when no neighbour is closer it drops a layer. At layer 0 it keeps a candidate list of size efSearch and returns the best k.\n\n| Parameter | Higher value means |\n| M | More links per node: better recall, more memory, slower build |\n| efConstruction | Better-quality graph, slower build |\n| efSearch | Better recall, slower queries (tunable per query) |\n\nPinecone's HNSW guide shows the trade-off curves. HNSW gives excellent recall and latency but stores the graph in memory, adding roughly M x 2 x 4 bytes per vector on top of the vectors themselves, and deletes are awkward.\n\n## Choosing\nThe Faiss wiki's guidelines boil down to:\n- Under ~1M vectors: flat or HNSW.\n- Millions, memory available: HNSW.\n- Hundreds of millions, memory tight: IVF with PQ.\n- Billions on SSD: disk-based graph indexes (DiskANN).\n\n## IVF: click to move the query\nIn the IVF figure, points are grouped into clusters around centroids (the squares). A query only scans the clusters whose centroids are closest. Raise nprobe to scan more clusters: recall goes up and so does work.\n\n%%widget ivf IVF search: the query scans only the nprobe nearest clusters.\n\nThe failure case is easy to see: put the query near the border between two clusters. Its true nearest neighbor may sit just across the border, in a cluster you didn't scan. That is the recall IVF gives up.\n\n## HNSW: watch the greedy walk\nHNSW stores a graph where each point links to its near neighbors, with sparse upper layers for long jumps. Search starts at the top, greedily moves to whichever neighbor is closer to the query, drops a layer when no neighbor is closer, and repeats until the bottom layer.\n\n%%widget hnsw HNSW search: greedy hops through the layers toward the query.\n\n## Tuning knobs\n| Index | Knob | Effect of raising it |\n| IVF | nlist (clusters) | Smaller lists, faster scans, but needs higher nprobe for the same recall |\n| IVF | nprobe | Higher recall, linearly more work per query |\n| HNSW | M (links per node) | Better recall and robustness, more memory, slower build |\n| HNSW | efConstruction | Better graph quality, slower build |\n| HNSW | efSearch | Higher recall, slower queries; can be set per query |\n\nStarting points that work for many workloads: IVF with nlist about the square root of the vector count and nprobe 1 to 5% of nlist; HNSW with M = 16, efConstruction = 200, then raise efSearch until recall hits your target.\n\n! Example: picking between them for 20 M vectors\n! HNSW at M = 16 gives sub-10 ms queries at 0.98 recall but needs all vectors plus about 2.5 GB of graph in RAM, and deletes are awkward.\n! IVF-PQ with nlist = 4,096 fits in a fraction of the memory and rebuilds quickly, but needs rescoring to get above about 0.9 recall.\n! If RAM is available and the data changes slowly, HNSW. If memory is tight or you rebuild often, IVF-PQ with rescoring.\n\n> Takeaway: IVF narrows the search to a few clusters; HNSW walks a layered graph from coarse to fine. Both have one query-time knob (nprobe, efSearch) that trades recall for speed.", "s": "HNSW stores vectors in a multi-layer proximity graph. A query enters at the sparse top layer, greedily hops to the neighbour closest to the query until no improvement, drops to the next layer and repeats; at layer 0 it explores a candidate list of size efSearch and returns the top k. M controls links per node (recall vs memory), efConstruction controls build quality, and efSearch controls query-time recall vs latency."}, {"id": "02.08", "t": "Search execution from query to result", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/search-execution-flow---from-query-to-result", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Pinecone: Rerankers and two-stage retrieval", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/series/rag/rerankers/", "Why a cross-encoder after ANN improves results."], ["Qdrant: Filtering", "https://qdrant.tech/documentation/concepts/filtering/", "Payload filters combined with vector search."], ["Anthropic: Contextual Retrieval", "https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval", "Chunk context, hybrid search and reranking measured end to end."]], "q": "Walk through a query in a RAG system from user text to the chunks placed in the prompt. Where would you add a reranker, and why there?", "n": ["1. Parse the request: text, filters (tenant, date, category), k.", "2. Embed the query with the same model used at indexing.", "3. Apply filters: pre-filter (restrict candidates first), post-filter (search then drop) or filtered graph traversal.", "4. ANN search across shards or segments; each returns its local top-k and results are merged.", "5. Optional rescoring with full-precision vectors, then reranking the top 50-100 with a cross-encoder.", "6. Fetch the stored text and metadata for the final top few and return them (in RAG, place them in the prompt)."], "ak": "", "a": "## The full path\n%%flow One search request through a typical vector store.\nUser query -(embed)-> Query vector -(filter + ANN)-> *Candidates per shard -(merge)-> Top 100 -(rerank)-> Top 5 -(fetch text)-> Prompt context\n%%\n\n## 1. Embed the query\nThe query is embedded with the same model and version as the documents. Some models expect a prefix such as \"query: \" for queries and \"passage: \" for documents; skipping it lowers quality.\n\n## 2. Filters\nReal queries include constraints: this user's documents only, from the last year, in English. There are three ways to apply them:\n- **Pre-filtering**: find matching ids first, then search only among them. Exact, but slow if many items match and the index can't use the filter.\n- **Post-filtering**: run ANN, then drop non-matching results. Fast, but with a selective filter you can end up with fewer than k results.\n- **Filtered traversal**: the index checks the filter while walking the graph or clusters. Qdrant and other vector DBs do this, and it handles both cases well.\n\n## 3. Search shards and merge\nLarge collections are split across shards or segments. Each one returns its local top k; a coordinator merges them into a global top k. This is why per-shard k is often set a bit higher than the final k.\n\n## 4. Rescore and rerank\nANN on compressed vectors is approximate, and embedding similarity is a rough signal of relevance. Two fixes stack:\n- **Rescore** candidates with full-precision vectors.\n- **Rerank** the top 50-100 with a cross-encoder, a model that reads the query and the passage together and outputs a relevance score. Pinecone's reranker guide explains why this is more accurate: a bi-encoder squeezes each document into one vector before seeing the query; a cross-encoder sees both.\n\nCross-encoders are too slow to run over the whole corpus, so they go at the end, on a short list.\n\n## 5. Return and use\nFetch the text and metadata of the final hits. In RAG they go into the prompt, usually with source ids so the model can cite them. Anthropic's Contextual Retrieval work measured how much each stage helps: adding context to chunks, combining embeddings with BM25, and reranking each cut retrieval failures, and together they reduced failed retrievals by 67% in their tests.\n\n## Budget the whole path\nEvery step on the query path takes time, and they add up. Set the cost of each stage below and see whether the total fits your latency budget.\n\n%%widget funnel Latency of one search: embed, search shards, rerank, fetch.\n\nThree observations from playing with it:\n\n- **Shards run in parallel, so the slowest one sets the time.** One overloaded shard turns a 10 ms search into a 60 ms one.\n- **Reranking is often the largest cost.** A cross-encoder scores each (query, document) pair with a full model pass. Reranking 100 candidates at 3 ms each is 300 ms on one GPU unless you batch them.\n- **The embedding call is a network hop.** If it goes to a hosted API, 50 to 150 ms is common. Caching embeddings for repeated queries removes it.\n\n## Filters: before, during or after\nMost real queries carry filters: tenant id, language, date range, permissions. Where you apply them changes both speed and correctness.\n\n| Strategy | How | Risk |\n| Post-filter | Search top 100, then drop the ones that fail the filter | If only 2% of data matches, you may end with zero results |\n| Pre-filter | Find matching ids first, then search only those | Slow if the filter matches a lot; breaks HNSW graph navigation |\n| Filtered search | The index checks the filter while it walks | Needs engine support; best of both when available |\n\nFor multi-tenant apps, the safest pattern is a separate collection or partition per large tenant. That makes leaking one tenant's data into another's results structurally impossible, instead of depending on a filter being set on every query.\n\n! Example: why a filtered query returned nothing\n! A user searches their own 300 documents in a 30 M document index. The engine retrieves the global top 100 and then filters by owner.\n! The chance that any of the user's documents is in the global top 100 is tiny, so they get zero results.\n! Fix: filtered search, or a per-user partition, or raise k only for highly selective filters.\n\n> Takeaway: query, embed, filter, ANN per shard, merge, rescore, rerank, fetch. Put the expensive, accurate step (the reranker) last, on the shortest list.", "s": "The query text is embedded with the same model as the corpus; filters (tenant, date) are applied via pre-filtering or filtered index traversal; ANN runs on each shard and results are merged into a global top 50-100; candidates are optionally rescored with full vectors, then a cross-encoder reranks them; the top few chunks' text is fetched and inserted into the prompt with citations. The reranker goes after ANN because it is accurate but too slow to run on the whole corpus."}, {"id": "02.09", "t": "Inside a vector database", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/milvus-db", "k": [], "x": ["Milvus"], "r": [["Milvus: Architecture overview", "https://milvus.io/docs/architecture_overview.md", "Access, coordinator, worker and storage layers."], ["Wang et al.: Milvus, a purpose-built vector data management system (SIGMOD 2021)", "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3448016.3457550", "The design paper."], ["Milvus on GitHub", "https://github.com/milvus-io/milvus", "Source and docs for the open-source vector DB."]], "q": "In Milvus, what happens between an insert and the moment the new vector is searchable with an index? Name the components involved.", "n": ["Milvus separates storage and compute, and splits the system into access, coordination, worker and storage layers.", "Proxies (access layer) receive requests; coordinators manage metadata, data placement and index building.", "Workers: query nodes serve searches, data nodes persist inserts, index nodes build indexes.", "Writes go to a log (message queue) first, which is the source of truth; data nodes turn it into segments in object storage.", "Growing segments are searched with brute force; sealed segments get an ANN index built in the background.", "Because compute is stateless, query nodes scale out independently of storage."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why look inside\nEvery dedicated vector database faces the same problems: accept writes continuously, keep indexes fresh, search across many machines, and survive failures. Milvus is open source and its architecture docs are detailed, so it is a good one to study.\n\n## Four layers\n%%layers Milvus 2.x splits responsibilities so each layer scales on its own.\nAccess layer | stateless proxies: validate requests, route, merge results\nCoordinators | metadata, data placement, load balancing, index jobs\n*Worker nodes | query nodes (search), data nodes (persist), index nodes (build)\nStorage | metadata store (etcd), log broker (Pulsar or Kafka), object storage (S3/MinIO)\n%%\n\nThe key idea, from the Milvus architecture overview: **storage and compute are separated**, and the log is the backbone. Workers are stateless and can be added or replaced without moving data around.\n\n## The write path\n1. A client inserts vectors through a proxy.\n2. The proxy writes them to the **log broker**. Once logged, the write is durable.\n3. Query nodes subscribe to the log so new data becomes searchable quickly.\n4. Data nodes batch log entries into **segments** and flush them to object storage.\n5. When a segment reaches its size limit it is **sealed**, and an index node builds an ANN index (HNSW, IVF, DiskANN...) for it.\n\n## The read path\n%%seq A search in Milvus.\nClient > Proxy: search(vector, filter, k)\nProxy > Query node: search assigned segments\nQuery node > Query node: indexed search on sealed, brute force on growing\nQuery node -> Proxy: local top k\nProxy -> Client: merged top k\n%%\n\nNewly inserted data sits in **growing segments**, which have no index yet and are searched by brute force. That's fine because they are small. Sealed segments use their indexes. The proxy merges the partial results.\n\n## Consistency choices\nBecause writes flow through a log, a search might not see a write from a moment ago. Milvus lets you choose a consistency level per request (strong, bounded staleness, session, eventually). Stronger means the query waits until the query nodes have caught up with the log.\n\n## What this teaches about any vector DB\n- Fresh writes and ANN indexes don't mix well, so systems keep a small unindexed buffer and index in the background.\n- Segment or shard results are always merged at the end.\n- Separating stateless compute from durable storage is what makes scaling and recovery simple.\n\n## Why deletes and updates are hard\nGraph and cluster indexes are built for reads. Changing them in place is expensive, so most vector databases borrow the log-structured design used by Cassandra, RocksDB and Lucene: write to small new segments, and merge them in the background.\n\n%%widget segments Writes create small segments; background compaction merges them and drops deleted rows.\n\nWalk through it in the figure:\n\n1. **Insert**: new vectors go to a write-ahead log for durability, then into a small in-memory segment. They are searchable within a second or so.\n2. **Seal**: when the segment reaches a size limit, it is frozen and indexed (HNSW or IVF built on just that segment).\n3. **Delete**: nothing is removed. The id is added to a deletion bitmap, and searches skip it.\n4. **Compact**: in the background, several small segments plus their deletion bitmaps are rewritten into one large, clean segment.\n\nA query searches every live segment and merges the results, which is why a database with too many small segments gets slow, and why compaction schedules matter.\n\n## Consequences for your app\n- **Updates are delete plus insert.** Re-embedding a document leaves the old vector in place until compaction. Space usage can temporarily double during a full re-embed.\n- **Freshness has a lag.** A document written now may not appear in search for a second or more. If users upload a file and immediately ask about it, either wait for the write to be visible or answer from the file directly.\n- **Count and recall can drift.** Heavily deleted segments waste search effort. If recall falls over weeks without a code change, check the deleted-row ratio and force a compaction.\n\n> Takeaway: a vector database is a log plus segments plus background index builds, with stateless search workers on top. New data is searchable by brute force first and indexed once its segment is sealed.", "s": "The proxy receives the insert and writes it to the log broker (durable at that point). Query nodes consume the log so the data is searchable in a growing segment via brute force. Data nodes batch log entries into segments and flush to object storage; once a segment is sealed, the index coordinator schedules an index node to build the ANN index, after which query nodes load the indexed sealed segment."}]}, {"n": "03", "name": "LLM foundations", "lessons": [{"id": "03.01", "t": "What a large language model does", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/llm-intro", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Andrej Karpathy: Intro to Large Language Models (video)", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zjkBMFhNj_g", "One-hour talk: LLMs as next-token predictors and what that implies."], ["Stephen Wolfram: What Is ChatGPT Doing ... and Why Does It Work?", "https://writings.stephenwolfram.com/2023/02/what-is-chatgpt-doing-and-why-does-it-work/", "Long, clear essay on next-token probabilities."], ["3Blue1Brown: Large Language Models explained briefly", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LPZh9BOjkQs", "Eight-minute visual overview."]], "q": "An LLM is 'just predicting the next token'. Explain what that means and give one capability and one failure that follow from it.", "n": ["An LLM takes a sequence of tokens and outputs a probability for every possible next token.", "Generating text is that step in a loop: pick a token, append it, predict again.", "Training on trillions of tokens of text forces the model to learn grammar, facts, style and some reasoning, because all of these help predict the next token.", "The model is a fixed set of weights (billions of parameters). It has no memory between calls except what is in the context window.", "Capabilities follow from prediction: translation, summarising, coding, following instructions after fine-tuning.", "Failures follow too: confident but false text (hallucination), knowledge cut-off, sensitivity to prompt wording."], "ak": "", "a": "## One job: the next token\nA large language model is a function. Its input is a sequence of tokens; its output is a probability distribution over the whole vocabulary for the next token. Karpathy's intro talk frames it as a compressed model of the internet's text: a few hundred gigabytes of weights distilled from many terabytes of text.\n\nTo generate, you sample a token from that distribution, append it, and run the model again.\n\n%%flow Generation is next-token prediction in a loop.\n\"The capital of France is\" -(model)-> *P(next token) -(sample)-> \"Paris\" -(append, repeat)-> \"Paris.\"\n%%\n\n## Why prediction leads to ability\nWolfram's essay makes the point at length: to predict text well, the model has to capture the structure that produced the text. Predicting the next line of a proof needs some logic. Predicting a translation needs both languages. Predicting the reply in a dialogue needs a model of the speakers. None of these were trained directly; they emerge because they reduce prediction error.\n\n## What a model is and isn't\n- **Weights**: billions of numbers fixed after training. They store patterns and facts, imperfectly.\n- **Context window**: the only working memory. The model knows about your conversation only because the whole conversation is sent on each call.\n- **No lookup**: unless you give it tools or retrieved documents, it can't check a fact. It produces the most plausible continuation.\n\n## Base model vs assistant\nA base model trained only on raw text will continue a question with more questions, because that's plausible on the web. Assistant models are further trained on instruction-following examples and human feedback so that the most likely continuation of a question is a helpful answer. Lesson 03.04 and module 8 cover this.\n\n## Consequences you will design around\n| Property | Consequence for apps |\n| Predicts plausible text | Can state false things fluently (hallucination) |\n| Fixed training data | Knowledge cut-off; needs retrieval for fresh or private data |\n| Context is the only memory | You must manage what goes in each prompt |\n| Sampling is random | Same prompt can give different answers |\n\n## Run the loop yourself\nA language model does one thing: given the tokens so far, output a probability for every token in its vocabulary. Everything else is a loop around that. Click a candidate to append it and see the model run again on the longer text.\n\n%%widget nexttoken The generation loop with toy next-token probabilities.\n\nThe probabilities in the figure are hand-written, but the shape is realistic. After \"The capital of France is\", a real model puts almost all its probability on \" Paris\". After \"My favourite food is\", the probability is spread across hundreds of tokens. The first case looks like knowledge, the second like choice; to the model they are the same operation.\n\n## Why one objective produces so much\nTo predict the next token well across trillions of tokens of text, the model has to compress the regularities behind the text: grammar, facts, the style of a legal contract, the way a Python function usually ends, the steps of a proof. Nobody programs these in. They are what lowers the prediction loss.\n\nThat also explains the failure modes you will design around:\n\n- **Fluent errors.** The model outputs what is likely, not what is true. A plausible citation with the wrong year is a likely string.\n- **No built-in \"I don't know\".** The loop always produces a next token. Abstaining has to be trained in or prompted for.\n- **No memory between calls.** The model only sees what is in the context window this time. Chat history, user preferences and facts must be sent again on every call.\n- **Knowledge cut-off.** Anything after the training data ends is unknown unless you put it in the prompt.\n\n! Example: one token at a time, with real counts\n! A 200-token answer means the model runs 200 forward passes, each reading the prompt plus everything generated so far (with a KV cache, module 4, it only computes the new token's part).\n! If one in fifty tokens is a slightly risky choice, a 200-token answer has about four. Most are harmless word choices; one wrong number in a table is not. That is why long, factual outputs need checks.\n\n> Takeaway: an LLM predicts the next token, over and over. Its abilities and its failures both come from that one objective, so plan for plausible-but-wrong output and give it the context it needs.", "s": "Given the tokens so far, the model outputs a probability distribution over its vocabulary for the next token; generation samples one, appends it, and repeats. Capability: because predicting text well requires modelling its structure, it can translate, summarise or write code without task-specific training. Failure: it produces the most plausible continuation, not a verified one, so it can hallucinate confident false facts (and knows nothing after its training cut-off unless given context)."}, {"id": "03.02", "t": "How LLMs learn and generate text", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/how-llms-work", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Andrej Karpathy: Let's build GPT from scratch (video)", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCc8FmEb1nY", "Code a small GPT and its training loop."], ["Kaplan et al.: Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models (2020)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361", "Loss falls predictably with parameters, data and compute."], ["Hoffmann et al.: Training Compute-Optimal LLMs (Chinchilla, 2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556", "About 20 tokens per parameter for compute-optimal training."]], "q": "What loss does pretraining minimise, and what did the Chinchilla paper change about how big models are trained?", "n": ["Pretraining: show the model text, ask it to predict each next token, measure cross-entropy loss, and update weights by gradient descent.", "Training is parallel: in one pass the model predicts every position in a sequence at once (with causal masking).", "Scaling laws (Kaplan 2020): loss falls as a smooth power law with more parameters, data and compute.", "Chinchilla (2022): for a fixed compute budget, models were too big and undertrained. About 20 training tokens per parameter is compute-optimal.", "Modern models train far beyond 20 tokens per parameter because inference cost matters more than training cost.", "After pretraining come supervised fine-tuning and preference training, which turn a text predictor into an assistant."], "ak": "", "a": "## The training objective\nPretraining is simple to state. Take a huge corpus, cut it into sequences, and for every position ask the model to predict the next token. The loss is **cross-entropy**: the negative log probability the model gave to the token that actually came next. Average it over all positions and minimise with gradient descent.\n\n%%flow One training step.\nBatch of text -(forward pass)-> Predicted distributions -(compare with real next tokens)-> *Cross-entropy loss -(backprop)-> Weight update\n%%\n\nA key efficiency: the transformer predicts every position in a sequence in one forward pass. A causal mask stops each position from seeing future tokens, so one 4,000-token sequence gives 4,000 training examples at once. Karpathy's GPT-from-scratch video builds exactly this loop.\n\n## Training vs generation\n| Aspect | Training | Generation |\n| Input | Real text | Prompt plus generated tokens |\n| Positions processed | All in parallel | One new token per step |\n| Output used | Loss on every position | Only the last position's distribution |\n| Weights | Updated | Frozen |\n\nAt inference the model only ever uses the distribution at the final position. Errors can compound: the model conditions on its own earlier tokens, which may be wrong.\n\n## Scaling laws\nKaplan et al. (2020) found that loss falls as a smooth power law as you increase parameters, data or compute, over many orders of magnitude. That made it possible to predict a big model's loss from small runs and plan budgets in advance.\n\nHoffmann et al. (Chinchilla, 2022) revisited the balance. For a fixed compute budget, they found earlier large models were undertrained: you should scale data as fast as parameters. Their 70B Chinchilla trained on 1.4T tokens beat the 280B Gopher. The rule of thumb that came out of it is **about 20 tokens per parameter**.\n\n## Why modern models overtrain\nChinchilla optimises training compute only. Once a model is deployed, every user request costs inference compute, which grows with parameter count. So labs now train smaller models on far more tokens than Chinchilla suggests (Llama 3 8B saw about 15T tokens, close to 2,000 per parameter). It costs more to train but less to serve.\n\n## After pretraining\nPretraining gives a base model that continues text. Two more stages make it useful:\n1. **Supervised fine-tuning (SFT)** on instruction and response examples.\n2. **Preference training** (RLHF, DPO and related methods) to prefer helpful, honest, harmless answers.\n\n## The compute-optimal trade-off\nTraining compute is roughly 6 × N × D floating point operations, where N is parameters and D is training tokens. For a fixed budget you can train a big model on few tokens or a small model on many. Hoffmann et al. (2022, \"Chinchilla\") found that loss is lowest when tokens grow in proportion to parameters, about 20 tokens per parameter.\n\n%%widget chinchilla For a fixed compute budget, how loss changes as you trade model size against training tokens.\n\nTheir 70B model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens beat the 280B Gopher, which used the same compute on fewer tokens. The lesson for the field was that earlier large models were undertrained.\n\n## Why everyone now trains past \"optimal\"\nChinchilla-optimal minimizes training cost for a given quality. It ignores inference. A model you serve to millions of users runs inference far more than it ever trained, and inference cost grows with N (about 2 × N operations per generated token). So it pays to train a smaller model for much longer:\n\n| Model | Parameters | Training tokens | Tokens per parameter |\n| Chinchilla (2022) | 70B | 1.4T | 20 |\n| Llama 2 7B (2023) | 7B | 2T | about 290 |\n| Llama 3 8B (2024) | 8B | over 15T | about 1,900 |\n\nLoss keeps falling slowly past the 20:1 point, so these models are better than a Chinchilla-optimal model of the same size, and far cheaper to serve than a Chinchilla-optimal model of the same quality.\n\n## What pretraining does and doesn't give you\nAfter pretraining, the model is a strong next-token predictor over internet-like text. Ask it \"What is the capital of France?\" and it may continue with three more quiz questions, because that is a likely continuation of a list of quiz questions. Turning it into an assistant is the job of post-training, covered in 03.04.\n\n! Example: estimating training cost\n! An 8B model on 15T tokens needs 6 × 8×10^9 × 15×10^12 = 7.2×10^23 FLOPs.\n! An H100 sustains maybe 400 TFLOP/s in practice on bf16 training, so that is 7.2×10^23 / 4×10^14 = 1.8×10^9 GPU-seconds, or about 500,000 GPU-hours.\n! At a few dollars per GPU-hour, that is a seven-figure training run for a \"small\" model, before any failed experiments.\n\n> Takeaway: LLMs learn by minimising next-token cross-entropy over huge corpora, in parallel over every position. Scaling laws make loss predictable, and inference cost pushes labs to train smaller models on more data.", "s": "Pretraining minimises cross-entropy loss on next-token prediction: the negative log-probability of the actual next token at each position, averaged over the corpus. Chinchilla showed that for a fixed compute budget, earlier models were too large and trained on too little data; compute-optimal training scales data with parameters, roughly 20 tokens per parameter (70B params on 1.4T tokens beat a 280B model)."}, {"id": "03.03", "t": "LLM text generation", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/llm-text-generation", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Hugging Face: How to generate text", "https://huggingface.co/blog/how-to-generate", "Greedy, beam search, top-k and top-p sampling with examples."], ["Holtzman et al.: The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration (2019)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09751", "The nucleus (top-p) sampling paper."], ["Hugging Face: Generation strategies", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/generation_strategies", "Reference for every decoding option in transformers."]], "q": "You need reliable JSON extraction for one feature and varied marketing copy for another. What decoding settings would you use for each and why?", "n": ["The model outputs logits; softmax turns them into probabilities over the vocabulary.", "Greedy decoding always picks the most likely token: deterministic, but tends to repeat itself in long text.", "Beam search keeps several candidate sequences; good for translation, poor for open-ended text.", "Temperature divides logits before softmax: below 1 is sharper and safer, above 1 is flatter and more varied.", "Top-k keeps the k most likely tokens; top-p (nucleus) keeps the smallest set with total probability p.", "For structured output use low temperature plus constrained decoding (JSON schema / grammar); for creative text use temperature 0.8-1.0 with top-p around 0.9."], "ak": "", "a": "## From logits to a token\nEach forward pass ends with a vector of logits, one per vocabulary entry (often 100k+). Softmax converts them to probabilities. A **decoding strategy** decides which token to emit. This choice changes output quality a lot, with no change to the model.\n\n## Deterministic strategies\n- **Greedy**: take the argmax every step. Simple and repeatable, but Hugging Face's generation guide shows it falls into loops (\"I'm not sure. I'm not sure.\") on long open-ended text.\n- **Beam search**: keep the b highest-probability partial sequences and extend all of them. Works for tasks with one right answer (translation), but Holtzman et al. showed that maximising probability produces bland, repetitive text, because human text is not the most probable text.\n\n## Sampling strategies\n| Knob | What it does | Typical values |\n| Temperature T | Divide logits by T before softmax. T < 1 sharpens, T > 1 flattens | 0 to 1.2 |\n| Top-k | Sample only from the k most likely tokens | 40-100 |\n| Top-p (nucleus) | Sample from the smallest set whose probabilities add to p | 0.9-0.95 |\n| Min-p | Drop tokens below a fraction of the top token's probability | 0.05-0.1 |\n| Repetition / frequency penalty | Lower logits of tokens already used | Small values |\n\n%%flow The usual sampling pipeline, applied every step.\nLogits -(/ temperature)-> Scaled logits -(top-k / top-p filter)-> *Truncated distribution -(sample)-> Next token\n%%\n\nTop-p adapts to the situation: when the model is confident, the nucleus holds a few tokens; when many continuations are plausible, it holds many. That is why Holtzman et al. proposed it over a fixed top-k.\n\n## Stopping\nGeneration stops at the end-of-sequence token, a stop sequence you provide, or max_tokens. Hitting max_tokens mid-sentence is a common cause of truncated JSON.\n\n## Structured output\nFor machine-read output, sampling settings are not enough. **Constrained decoding** masks out any token that would break a JSON schema or grammar, so output is always valid. Most APIs and servers (vLLM, llama.cpp, provider \"structured output\" modes) support it.\n\n! Example: two features, two settings\n! JSON extraction: temperature 0 (or close), schema-constrained decoding, generous max_tokens. You want the single most likely, valid answer every time.\n! Marketing copy: temperature around 0.9, top-p 0.9-0.95, maybe a small frequency penalty. You want variety without nonsense, and you may sample several drafts.\n\n## Shape the distribution\nEvery decoding setting reshapes the same next-token distribution before one token is drawn. Change temperature, top-k, top-p and min-p below and watch which tokens stay eligible and how probability moves between them.\n\n%%widget sampling Temperature, top-k, top-p and min-p applied to one next-token distribution.\n\nWhat each knob does, mechanically:\n\n- **Temperature T** divides the logits before softmax. T below 1 sharpens the distribution (the top token gets more), T above 1 flattens it, T near 0 is greedy decoding.\n- **Top-k** keeps the k most likely tokens and renormalizes. Simple, but the right k differs by context: after \"The capital of France is\" only one token makes sense, after \"Once upon a\" dozens do.\n- **Top-p (nucleus)** keeps the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p, so the set shrinks when the model is confident and grows when it isn't. This is why it replaced top-k as the usual default.\n- **Min-p** keeps tokens with at least min_p times the top token's probability. It adapts in the same direction as top-p and handles high temperatures more gracefully.\n\n## Settings by task\n| Task | Typical settings | Why |\n| Extraction, classification, code fixes | T = 0 to 0.2 | You want the single most likely answer |\n| General chat | T = 0.7, top-p = 0.9 to 0.95 | Natural variety without nonsense |\n| Brainstorming, fiction | T = 0.9 to 1.1, top-p = 0.95, or min-p = 0.05 | More diverse options |\n| Self-consistency voting (08.02) | T = 0.7 to 1.0 | You need varied reasoning paths |\n\nTemperature 0 is not fully deterministic on most hosted APIs: batching and floating-point order on GPUs can change near-ties between runs. If you need reproducibility, cache outputs.\n\n## Structured output in more detail\nConstrained decoding enforces a format by masking tokens. At each step, a grammar (often compiled from a JSON schema) tells the server which tokens could legally come next, and every other token's probability is set to zero before sampling. The model can't produce invalid JSON, but it can still produce valid JSON with wrong values, so validate the content too.\n\n> Takeaway: decoding is a product decision. Use low temperature and constrained decoding where correctness matters, and temperature plus top-p sampling where variety matters.", "s": "JSON extraction: temperature 0 or near it (greedy-like, repeatable), plus schema/grammar-constrained decoding so every token keeps the output valid, and enough max_tokens to avoid truncation. Marketing copy: temperature ~0.8-1.0 with top-p ~0.9 (optionally a small repetition penalty) to sample diverse but coherent text, possibly generating several candidates. Greedy/beam search makes copy bland and repetitive; high temperature makes JSON unreliable."}, {"id": "03.04", "t": "Making a model useful", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/llm-improvements", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Ouyang et al.: InstructGPT (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155", "SFT plus RLHF: a 1.3B instruct model preferred over 175B GPT-3."], ["Hu et al.: LoRA (2021)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685", "Low-rank adapters for cheap fine-tuning."], ["OpenAI: Optimizing LLM accuracy", "https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/optimizing-llm-accuracy", "When to use prompting, RAG or fine-tuning."]], "q": "A support bot answers in the wrong tone and also doesn't know your product's latest pricing. Which technique fixes each problem, and why not the other way round?", "n": ["A base model only continues text. Several techniques make it useful, at very different costs.", "Prompting and few-shot examples: cheapest, change behaviour per call with no training.", "Retrieval (RAG): add fresh or private knowledge at query time. Best for facts that change.", "Supervised fine-tuning (SFT): train on example inputs and outputs. Best for format, tone, style and narrow skills.", "Preference training (RLHF, DPO): train on which of two answers is better, to make outputs helpful and safe.", "Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (LoRA) trains small adapter matrices instead of all weights, cutting memory and cost."], "ak": "", "a": "## From text predictor to assistant\nA pretrained model has broad knowledge but no idea it is supposed to help you. InstructGPT (Ouyang et al., 2022) showed how much the next steps matter: their 1.3B model after fine-tuning and RLHF was preferred by human raters over the raw 175B GPT-3.\n\n## The ladder of techniques\n%%layers Try the cheaper rungs first. Each one up costs more and changes the model more.\nPrompting | instructions, few-shot examples, system prompt\n*Retrieval (RAG) | inject relevant, current documents at query time\nFine-tuning (SFT / LoRA) | train on input/output examples for style, format, narrow skills\nPreference training | RLHF / DPO on pairs of better and worse answers\nPretraining | new base model: only for labs\n%%\n\nOpenAI's accuracy guide frames the choice as two axes: **context optimisation** (what the model needs to know: RAG) and **LLM optimisation** (how the model needs to act: fine-tuning). Many systems need both.\n\n## Supervised fine-tuning\nCollect examples of the input and the ideal output, and continue training on them. It's good for:\n- consistent tone and format;\n- a narrow task the model does poorly with prompting alone;\n- shortening prompts (behaviour learned instead of described every call).\nIt's bad at adding facts that change: the knowledge goes stale and the model can blend it wrongly.\n\n## LoRA\nFull fine-tuning updates every weight, which needs as much GPU memory as training. LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) freezes the original weights and learns two small matrices per layer whose product is the weight update. Trainable parameters drop by orders of magnitude, adapters are megabytes, and you can swap adapters per customer on one base model. QLoRA goes further by keeping the frozen base in 4-bit.\n\n## Preference training\nSFT teaches what a good answer looks like. Preference training teaches which of two answers is better. RLHF trains a reward model on human comparisons and optimises the LLM against it with reinforcement learning. DPO skips the reward model and trains directly on the preference pairs. Module 8 returns to this for reasoning models.\n\n! Example: wrong tone, stale pricing\n! Pricing changes every month, so it belongs in retrieval: fetch the current price page into the prompt. Fine-tuning would bake in prices that go stale.\n! Tone is a stable behaviour. Start with a system prompt and examples; if it is still inconsistent, a small SFT or LoRA run on a few hundred good replies fixes it.\n\n## How cheap is LoRA, really?\nLoRA freezes the original weight matrix W and learns a low-rank update B × A next to it. Only A and B train. Set a model shape and a rank below and compare trainable parameters and memory with full fine-tuning.\n\n%%widget lora Trainable parameters and training memory: full fine-tuning versus LoRA.\n\nThe reason it works: the change that fine-tuning makes to a weight matrix tends to have low \"intrinsic rank\". A 4,096 × 4,096 matrix has 16.8 M entries, but the useful adjustment for a narrow task can be captured by B (4,096 × 16) times A (16 × 4,096), which is 131k entries, about 0.8% of the size. Hu et al. (2021) reported LoRA on GPT-3 175B with 10,000× fewer trainable parameters and about 3× less GPU memory than full fine-tuning, at similar quality.\n\n## When to fine-tune at all\nFine-tuning changes how the model behaves. It is a poor way to add facts.\n\n| You want | Better tool |\n| The model to know your product docs | RAG (03.05): facts stay current and citable |\n| A consistent output format or tone | Prompting with examples first; fine-tune if that's not enough |\n| A small model to match a big one on one task | Fine-tune or distil (07.03) on the big model's outputs |\n| Lower latency and cost on a high-volume task | Fine-tune a small model |\n| New behavior the base model can't do from prompts | Fine-tune, with a few thousand good examples |\n\nA useful rule: get the best result you can with prompting and retrieval, build an eval set, and only fine-tune when the eval shows a gap that more prompting doesn't close. The eval set is also what tells you whether the fine-tune helped.\n\n! Example: a classification fine-tune that paid off\n! A ticket router uses a large model with a long prompt: 2,500 input tokens per ticket, 400 ms, 92% accuracy on the eval set.\n! 3,000 labelled tickets train a LoRA on a 3B open model. The prompt shrinks to 150 tokens because the instructions are now in the weights. Accuracy on the same eval: 93%, latency 60 ms, cost per ticket down by over 90%.\n! The fine-tune didn't make the model smarter. It made a narrow, well-defined job cheap.\n\n> Takeaway: prompt first, retrieve for knowledge, fine-tune for behaviour, and use preference training to shape what \"good\" means. Each rung costs more than the one before.", "s": "Stale pricing is a knowledge problem: use retrieval (RAG) to put current pricing into the prompt at query time; fine-tuning would freeze facts that change and can't be updated without retraining. Wrong tone is a behaviour problem: fix it with a better system prompt and examples, and if that's not enough, supervised fine-tuning (e.g. LoRA) on example replies in the right tone. RAG doesn't reliably change style."}, {"id": "03.05", "t": "Retrieval-augmented generation", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/llms-and-rag", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Lewis et al.: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (2020)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401", "The paper that named RAG."], ["Anthropic: Contextual Retrieval", "https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval", "Chunk context, hybrid search and reranking, with measured gains."], ["Liu et al.: Lost in the Middle (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172", "Models use information at the start and end of long contexts best."]], "q": "Your RAG bot gives wrong answers. List the steps you'd check, in order, to find whether retrieval or generation is at fault.", "n": ["RAG = retrieve relevant documents for a query, then generate an answer grounded in them.", "Indexing: load documents, split into chunks, embed, store with metadata.", "Query time: embed the question, retrieve top chunks (often hybrid + rerank), put them in the prompt with instructions to cite and to say when the answer isn't there.", "Chunking matters: too small loses context, too large dilutes the embedding. Adding a short document-level context to each chunk helps.", "Evaluate the two halves separately: retrieval (recall@k, did the right chunk come back?) and generation (faithfulness to the chunks, answer quality).", "Long contexts don't remove the need for RAG: cost, latency and 'lost in the middle' effects all favour sending only what's relevant."], "ak": "", "a": "## The idea\nLewis et al. (2020) introduced retrieval-augmented generation: before generating, fetch documents relevant to the question and condition the answer on them. It fixes two core LLM limits from lesson 03.01: knowledge cut-off and no access to private data. It also makes answers checkable, because you can show the sources.\n\n## Two pipelines\n%%flow Offline indexing and online answering.\n[Indexing] Documents -(split)-> Chunks -(embed)-> *Vector store + BM25 index\n[Query] Question -(retrieve, rerank)-> Top chunks -(prompt)-> LLM -(cite)-> Answer\n%%\n\n**Indexing decisions**\n- **Chunking**: a few hundred tokens with some overlap is a common start. Split on structure (headings, paragraphs) rather than fixed character counts where you can.\n- **Context per chunk**: a chunk saying \"revenue grew 3%\" means nothing alone. Anthropic's Contextual Retrieval prepends a short LLM-written summary of where the chunk sits in its document before embedding. With BM25 and reranking added, they cut retrieval failures by 67%.\n- **Metadata**: source, date, access permissions, for filtering and citations.\n\n**Query-time decisions**\n- Retrieve with hybrid search, rerank, and keep the top few chunks.\n- Prompt the model to answer only from the provided sources, cite them, and say when the answer isn't present.\n- Order matters: Liu et al.'s \"Lost in the Middle\" found models use information at the start and end of a long context better than in the middle. Put the best chunks first.\n\n## Debugging RAG\nMost RAG failures are retrieval failures. Check in order:\n1. **Is the answer in the corpus at all?** If not, no prompt will fix it.\n2. **Was it chunked sensibly?** Look at the chunk that should answer the question.\n3. **Was it retrieved?** Check recall@k for that query. If it's at rank 30 and you pass 5, you lose.\n4. **Was it in the prompt?** Truncation and token limits silently drop context.\n5. **Did the model use it faithfully?** Only now is it a generation problem: tighten instructions, use a stronger model, or check for conflicting chunks.\n\n## RAG vs long context\nModels with 200k-1M token windows can sometimes take the whole corpus. That costs more per query, is slower (prefill grows with context), and still suffers from middle-of-context loss. RAG remains the default for large or changing corpora; long context helps when the relevant set is small but scattered.\n\n## Chunking decides what can be found\nBefore retrieval can find the answer, the answer has to sit inside one chunk. Change the chunk size and overlap below and see which chunk the question retrieves, and whether the answer survives intact.\n\n%%widget chunker How chunk size and overlap change what a retriever can find.\n\nThree regimes:\n\n- **Too small** (a sentence or two): precise matches, but the chunk lacks context. \"It was raised to 30 days\" is useless without knowing what \"it\" is.\n- **Too large** (several pages): the chunk is about many things, so its vector is a blurry average and matches nothing strongly. It also eats the context window.\n- **Middle** (200 to 500 tokens, split on headings and paragraphs, 10 to 20% overlap): the common starting point. Tune it on your own questions.\n\n## Techniques that move the needle\n- **Hybrid retrieval**: BM25 plus vectors with RRF (02.06). Catches exact terms like product codes that embeddings miss.\n- **Reranking**: retrieve 50 candidates cheaply, re-score with a cross-encoder, keep the best 5. Often the biggest single quality gain.\n- **Contextual chunks**: prepend a one-line summary of the document and section to each chunk before embedding (\"From the 2025 refund policy, section 3: ...\"). Anthropic reported this cut retrieval failures by about a third, and by about two thirds when combined with reranking, on their tests.\n- **Metadata filters**: product, version, date. Many \"wrong answers\" are right answers for last year's version.\n- **Cite and check**: ask the model to quote the passage it used, and verify the quote appears in the retrieved text.\n\n! Example: debugging one bad answer\n! Question: \"How long do customers have to return opened items?\" The bot answers \"14 days\". The policy says 30.\n! Step 1, retrieval: the top 5 chunks include the 2023 policy (14 days) and not the 2025 one. Retrieval failed, not generation.\n! Step 2, cause: both versions are indexed with no date field. Fix: add a version filter, or remove superseded documents.\n! Always check retrieval first. If the right chunk wasn't in the context, no prompt change can fix the answer.\n\n> Takeaway: RAG is retrieve then generate. Get chunking and retrieval right first, measure them separately from generation, and put the best evidence where the model will use it.", "s": "1) Check the answer exists in the source corpus. 2) Inspect how the relevant document was chunked (is the needed fact in one coherent chunk with enough context?). 3) Check retrieval: is that chunk in the top-k for this query (recall@k, try hybrid/rerank)? 4) Check the chunk actually reached the prompt (no truncation). 5) Only then check generation: did the model stay faithful to the retrieved text, or ignore/contradict it? Steps 1-4 failing means retrieval; only 5 is generation."}]}, {"n": "04", "name": "LLM internals", "lessons": [{"id": "04.01", "t": "Positional embeddings", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/positional-embeddings", "k": [], "x": ["RoPE"], "r": [["Vaswani et al.: Attention Is All You Need (2017)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762", "Section 3.5: sinusoidal positional encodings."], ["Su et al.: RoFormer / RoPE (2021)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09864", "Rotary position embeddings, used by most modern LLMs."], ["EleutherAI: Rotary Embeddings, a relative revolution", "https://blog.eleuther.ai/rotary-embeddings/", "Intuitive explanation with code."]], "q": "Why does a transformer need positional information at all, and what is the main advantage of RoPE over learned absolute position embeddings?", "n": ["Attention compares every token with every other token and ignores order. Without positions, 'dog bites man' and 'man bites dog' look the same.", "Original transformer: add fixed sinusoidal vectors (different frequencies per dimension) to token embeddings.", "GPT-2 style: learned absolute position embeddings, one vector per position up to a maximum length.", "RoPE: rotate query and key vectors by an angle proportional to position. Their dot product then depends only on relative distance.", "RoPE is used by Llama, Mistral, Qwen and most open models. ALiBi adds a distance penalty to attention scores instead.", "Context extension tricks (position interpolation, NTK scaling, YaRN) rescale RoPE frequencies so models work beyond their training length."], "ak": "", "a": "## Attention has no sense of order\nSelf-attention computes a weighted mix of all tokens, where weights come from dot products between query and key vectors. Shuffle the input tokens and each token gets the same set of scores, just in a different order. On its own, attention treats a sentence as a bag of words. Position has to be injected.\n\n## Absolute positions\n**Sinusoidal (Vaswani et al., 2017)**: add to each token embedding a fixed vector made of sines and cosines at different frequencies. Low dimensions change quickly with position, high dimensions slowly, like the hands of a clock. No parameters, and in principle it works for any length.\n\n**Learned (GPT-2, BERT)**: a trainable vector per position, up to a maximum (1,024 for GPT-2). Simple, but the model has never seen position 1,025, so it can't go beyond the training length.\n\n## Rotary position embeddings (RoPE)\nSu et al.'s RoPE changes where position goes. Instead of adding to the input, it **rotates** each query and key vector by an angle proportional to its position. Dimensions are paired, and each pair rotates at its own frequency.\n\n%%flow RoPE: rotation turns absolute positions into relative distance.\nQuery at position m -(rotate by m x theta)-> q'\nKey at position n -(rotate by n x theta)-> k'\nq' . k' -(depends only on)-> *m - n\n%%\n\nBecause rotating both vectors and taking a dot product depends only on the angle between them, the attention score depends on the **relative distance** m - n, not on absolute positions. EleutherAI's write-up gives the geometry with code. RoPE has no learned parameters, works with the KV cache, and is used by Llama, Mistral, Qwen and most open models.\n\n| Method | Where applied | Relative? | Used in |\n| Sinusoidal | Added to input | Implicitly | Original transformer |\n| Learned absolute | Added to input | No | GPT-2, BERT |\n| RoPE | Rotates Q and K | Yes | Llama, Mistral, Qwen |\n| ALiBi | Bias on attention scores | Yes | BLOOM, MPT |\n\n## Extending context\nA model trained on 8k tokens sees unfamiliar rotation angles beyond 8k and degrades. Position interpolation squeezes new positions into the trained range; NTK-aware scaling and YaRN rescale frequencies unevenly so local detail survives. With a short fine-tune these push context to 32k-128k and beyond. This is how many \"long context\" versions of open models are made.\n\n## Rotate and compare\nRoPE encodes position by rotating each query and key vector by an angle proportional to its position. The figure shows one pair of dimensions. Move the query position m and key position n, and watch the attention score.\n\n%%widget rope Rotating a query and a key by their positions; the score depends only on the offset.\n\nThe key property: if you rotate q by angle mθ and k by angle nθ, their dot product depends only on (m − n)θ. Move m and n together and the score stays fixed. Attention therefore sees relative distance for free, without any learned position table.\n\n## The full recipe\nA head of dimension d is split into d/2 pairs. Pair i rotates at its own frequency:\n\n```\ntheta_i = base^(-2i / d)        base = 10,000 in the original paper, 500,000 in Llama 3\nangle for pair i at position m = m * theta_i\n```\n\nThe first pairs rotate fast (they distinguish neighbouring tokens), the last pairs rotate very slowly (they distinguish positions thousands of tokens apart). It is the same idea as the hands of a clock: seconds, minutes and hours together pin down a time.\n\n## Why context extension is possible\nA model trained on 8k tokens has never seen the slow pairs reach the angles that 128k positions produce. Run it at 128k directly and attention breaks down. The fixes all change the angles, not the weights:\n\n| Method | Idea |\n| Position interpolation | Divide positions by the stretch factor, so 128k positions map onto the trained 0 to 8k range |\n| NTK-aware scaling | Raise the base instead, stretching slow pairs more than fast ones |\n| YaRN | Treat each frequency band differently and adjust attention temperature; needs only a short fine-tune |\n\nAll of them work best with a short fine-tune on long documents. Advertised context lengths also don't guarantee the model uses the whole window well; test retrieval of facts placed at different depths before relying on it.\n\n> Takeaway: attention is order-blind, so position must be added. Modern LLMs use RoPE, which rotates queries and keys so attention depends on relative distance and can be stretched to longer contexts.", "s": "Self-attention is permutation-invariant: it compares tokens by content only, so without position information word order is lost. RoPE rotates query and key vectors by angles proportional to their positions, so their dot product depends only on relative distance. Unlike learned absolute embeddings it adds no parameters, generalises better to relative structure, and can be rescaled (interpolation, NTK, YaRN) to extend context beyond the training length."}, {"id": "04.02", "t": "Attention", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/attention", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Jay Alammar: The Illustrated Transformer", "https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/", "Step-by-step pictures of Q, K, V and attention scores."], ["Vaswani et al.: Attention Is All You Need (2017)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762", "Scaled dot-product attention, equation 1."], ["3Blue1Brown: Attention in transformers, visually explained", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eMlx5fFNoYc", "Visual intuition for queries, keys and values."]], "q": "Write the scaled dot-product attention formula and explain what Q, K and V are and why the scores are divided by sqrt(d_k).", "n": ["Attention lets each token build its new representation from a weighted mix of other tokens.", "Each token's vector is projected three ways: query (what I'm looking for), key (what I offer), value (what I pass on).", "Score = Q . K for every pair of tokens; divide by sqrt(d_k); softmax over each row; multiply by V.", "Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(Q K^T / sqrt(d_k)) V.", "Dividing by sqrt(d_k) keeps dot products from growing with dimension, which would push softmax into near one-hot outputs with tiny gradients.", "Cost is quadratic in sequence length: n tokens give an n x n score matrix. This drives most long-context optimisations."], "ak": "", "a": "## The job attention does\nTake \"The animal didn't cross the street because it was too tired.\" To represent \"it\", the model needs information from \"animal\". Attention is the mechanism that lets each token pull in information from the other tokens it needs. Jay Alammar's Illustrated Transformer uses this exact sentence.\n\n## Queries, keys and values\nEach token's vector x is multiplied by three learned matrices:\n- **Query** q = x W_Q: what this token is looking for.\n- **Key** k = x W_K: what this token can be matched on.\n- **Value** v = x W_V: the information it hands over if selected.\n\nThe analogy is a soft dictionary lookup. The query is compared against every key; instead of returning one match, attention returns a weighted average of all values, weighted by how well each key matched.\n\n## The formula\n```\nAttention(Q, K, V) = softmax( Q K^T / sqrt(d_k) ) V\n```\n\n%%flow Scaled dot-product attention for a sequence of n tokens.\nQ, K -(Q K^T)-> n x n scores -(/ sqrt(d_k))-> Scaled -(softmax per row)-> *Attention weights -(x V)-> New token vectors\n%%\n\n1. **Scores**: dot product of each query with every key, an n x n matrix.\n2. **Scale**: divide by sqrt(d_k), the key dimension.\n3. **Softmax** each row so the weights for one token add to 1.\n4. **Mix**: multiply by V, so each output is a weighted sum of value vectors.\n\n## Why divide by sqrt(d_k)\nIf entries of q and k have unit variance, their dot product has variance d_k. With d_k = 128, scores spread over a range of roughly plus or minus 30, and softmax becomes almost one-hot: one weight near 1, the rest near 0, with vanishing gradients. Dividing by sqrt(d_k) brings the variance back to about 1, as the original paper explains.\n\n## The cost\nThe score matrix has n^2 entries per head per layer. Double the context and attention compute and memory quadruple. Prefill on a 100k-token prompt is dominated by this. FlashAttention (module 6) reduces the memory traffic; sparse attention (module 7) reduces the number of pairs.\n\n! Example: three tokens, tiny numbers\n! Query for \"it\" scores [animal: 4.0, street: 1.0, tired: 2.0] after scaling.\n! Softmax gives roughly [0.84, 0.04, 0.11].\n! The output for \"it\" is about 84% the value vector of \"animal\", plus small parts of the others.\n\n## Click a token, see its attention\nEach row of the grid is one query token, each column a key token, and the colour is the attention weight after softmax. Click \"it\" to see it attend mostly to \"animal\". Turn on the causal mask to see what a decoder is allowed to look at.\n\n%%widget attention Attention weights for one sentence, with an optional causal mask.\n\nScores in the figure are hand-set to show the pattern, but the mechanism is real: each output is a weighted average of value vectors, with weights from softmax(q · k / √d_k).\n\n## A computation small enough to do by hand\nTake three tokens with 2-dim queries, keys and values:\n\n```\nq(it)      = [1, 0]\nk(animal)  = [2, 0]   v(animal) = [10, 0]\nk(street)  = [0, 2]   v(street) = [0, 10]\nk(it)      = [1, 1]   v(it)     = [5, 5]\n```\n\n1. Scores: q · k = 2, 0, 1. Divide by √2: 1.41, 0, 0.71.\n2. Softmax: e^1.41 = 4.1, e^0 = 1, e^0.71 = 2.0, total 7.1, so weights 0.58, 0.14, 0.28.\n3. Output: 0.58 × [10, 0] + 0.14 × [0, 10] + 0.28 × [5, 5] = [7.2, 2.8].\n\nThe output for \"it\" is now mostly \"animal\" information. In a real model, d_k is 64 or 128, there are dozens of heads, and the projections that produce q, k and v are learned.\n\n## Why the √d_k matters\nDot products of random vectors grow with dimension: with unit-variance entries, the standard deviation of q · k is √d_k. Without scaling, a 128-dim head produces scores spread over a range of about ±30, and softmax turns that into a one-hot vector. Gradients for every other key then vanish. Toggle the scaling below to see it.\n\n%%widget scaling Softmax over random scores with and without dividing by √d_k.\n\n> Takeaway: attention is softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d_k))V, a learned soft lookup where each token mixes in values from the tokens whose keys match its query. It is powerful and quadratic in length.", "s": "Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d_k)) V. Q, K and V are linear projections of the token vectors: queries say what a token looks for, keys what each token can be matched on, values the information passed on. Scores are query-key dot products, softmaxed into weights that mix the values. Dividing by sqrt(d_k) keeps score variance near 1; otherwise large dot products make softmax nearly one-hot and gradients vanish."}, {"id": "04.03", "t": "Transformer architecture", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/transformer-architecture", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Jay Alammar: The Illustrated GPT-2", "https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-gpt2/", "Decoder-only transformer, block by block."], ["Andrej Karpathy: nanoGPT", "https://github.com/karpathy/nanoGPT", "About 300 lines of GPT model code you can read in one sitting."], ["Sebastian Raschka: The Big LLM Architecture Comparison", "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/the-big-llm-architecture-comparison", "How DeepSeek, Llama, Qwen, Gemma and others differ."]], "q": "Draw (in words) one decoder block of a modern LLM like Llama, naming each sub-layer in order and where the residual connections go.", "n": ["Modern LLMs are decoder-only transformers: a stack of identical blocks between an embedding layer and an output layer.", "Each block: normalisation, self-attention (causal, multi-head), residual add, normalisation, feed-forward network (MLP), residual add.", "Residual connections let each block add a small update to a running 'residual stream' and make deep stacks trainable.", "Pre-norm (normalise before each sub-layer) is now standard; Llama uses RMSNorm.", "The MLP is usually about 2/3 of parameters, often with a SwiGLU activation. It's where much factual knowledge seems to live.", "The final layer projects the last hidden state to vocabulary logits (often sharing weights with the input embedding)."], "ak": "", "a": "## The overall shape\nThe 2017 transformer had an encoder and a decoder for translation. GPT-style LLMs keep only the decoder: a stack of N identical blocks with causal self-attention, so each token can only see earlier tokens. Llama 3 8B has 32 blocks; the 405B model has 126.\n\n%%flow A decoder-only LLM end to end.\nToken ids -(embedding)-> Vectors -(N decoder blocks)-> *Final hidden states -(norm + LM head)-> Logits over vocabulary\n%%\n\n## Inside one block\n%%layers One pre-norm decoder block (Llama-style), bottom to top.\nResidual stream in | token vectors of size d_model (4,096 in Llama 3 8B)\nRMSNorm | normalise before attention\n*Causal multi-head self-attention | with RoPE on Q and K; output added back to the stream\nRMSNorm | normalise before the MLP\nFeed-forward (SwiGLU MLP) | expand to ~3.5x d_model, then project back; added to the stream\nResidual stream out | passed to the next block\n%%\n\n**Residual stream**\nEach sub-layer reads the stream, computes an update, and **adds** it back: x = x + Attention(Norm(x)), then x = x + MLP(Norm(x)). Gradients flow straight through the additions, which is why 100+ layer stacks train at all.\n\n**Attention vs MLP**\n- **Attention** moves information between positions (token to token).\n- **MLP** processes each position on its own. It holds most of the parameters (about two thirds), and interpretability work suggests much factual recall happens here.\n\n**Normalisation**\nThe original transformer normalised after each sub-layer (post-norm). Modern models normalise before (pre-norm), which trains more stably. Llama uses RMSNorm, a cheaper LayerNorm without mean-centering.\n\n## Where the parameters are\nFor a model with hidden size d and L layers, each block has about 4d^2 attention weights (Q, K, V, output) and about 8d^2 in the MLP. So total parameters are roughly 12 L d^2, plus embeddings. For d = 4,096 and L = 32 that's about 6.4B, close to Llama's 8B once embeddings and a wider MLP are counted.\n\n## How modern models differ\nRaschka's architecture comparison shows the 2017 skeleton survives, with tweaks:\n- RoPE instead of absolute positions.\n- Grouped-query attention (fewer K/V heads) to shrink the KV cache.\n- SwiGLU activations.\n- Mixture-of-experts MLPs (module 6) in DeepSeek, Mixtral, Qwen MoE.\n- Sliding-window or mixed local/global attention (Gemma, Mistral).\n\n## Count the parameters yourself\nThe figure computes parameter counts from the shape of a model. The defaults are close to Llama 3 8B. Change the hidden size, layers, heads or MLP width and see where the weights live.\n\n%%widget params Where a transformer's parameters are: attention, MLP and embeddings.\n\nHere is the arithmetic for the default, per layer:\n\n- **Attention**: W_q is 4,096 × 4,096 (16.8 M). With 8 KV heads of dim 128, W_k and W_v are 4,096 × 1,024 each (4.2 M each). W_o is 4,096 × 4,096 (16.8 M). Total about 42 M.\n- **MLP (SwiGLU)**: three matrices of 4,096 × 14,336: gate, up and down. Total about 176 M.\n- **Per layer**: about 218 M. Times 32 layers: about 7.0 B.\n- **Embeddings**: 128,256 × 4,096 = 525 M for the input table and another 525 M for the untied output head.\n- **Grand total**: about 8.0 B.\n\nThe MLP holds about 80% of the per-layer weights. That is why mixture-of-experts (06.02) puts its experts in the MLP, and why quantization papers focus on MLP matrices.\n\n## Pre-norm and residuals, concretely\nEach block computes:\n\n```\nh = x + Attention(RMSNorm(x))\ny = h + MLP(RMSNorm(h))\n```\n\nThe residual \"+ x\" means each block only adds a correction to a running vector (the residual stream). A 32-layer model is 32 small edits to the same vector, not 32 rewrites. Normalizing before each sub-layer (pre-norm) keeps those edits at a stable scale, which is what lets very deep stacks train without careful learning-rate tricks.\n\n! Example: memory needed just to load the weights\n! 8 B parameters × 2 bytes (bf16) = 16 GB. At int4 (07.01) that drops to about 4.5 GB including scales.\n! A 70 B model in bf16 is 140 GB: two 80 GB GPUs before any KV cache. Parameter count tells you the minimum hardware before you run a single request.\n\n> Takeaway: an LLM is embeddings, then N blocks of (norm, attention, add, norm, MLP, add), then a projection to logits. Attention mixes across tokens; the MLP transforms each token; residual adds hold it together.", "s": "Input from the residual stream -> RMSNorm -> causal multi-head self-attention (with RoPE on queries/keys) -> add the output back to the residual stream -> RMSNorm -> feed-forward MLP (SwiGLU) -> add back to the residual stream -> next block. Residual connections wrap both the attention and the MLP sub-layers; after the last block a final norm and the LM head produce logits."}, {"id": "04.04", "t": "KV Cache", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/kv-cache", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Hugging Face: KV cache strategies", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/kv_cache", "Dynamic, static, offloaded and quantized caches."], ["Pope et al.: Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.05102", "Memory and compute analysis of inference, including the KV cache."], ["Ainslie et al.: GQA (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13245", "Grouped-query attention, fewer KV heads for a smaller cache."]], "q": "Compute the KV cache size for one 8,000-token sequence on a model with 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128, in fp16.", "n": ["During decode, each new token needs the keys and values of all previous tokens at every layer.", "Recomputing them every step would make generation quadratic. The KV cache stores them once and appends one entry per new token.", "Size per token = 2 (K and V) x layers x KV heads x head dim x bytes per value.", "For long contexts and big batches, the KV cache can use more GPU memory than the weights. It limits batch size and context length.", "Ways to shrink it: grouped-query or multi-query attention (fewer KV heads), quantized caches (fp8/int8), sliding windows, multi-head latent attention.", "Prompt caching reuses the KV cache of a shared prefix (system prompt, documents) across requests."], "ak": "", "a": "## The repeated work\nIn decoding, the model produces one new token per step. Attention for that token needs a query for the new token, and keys and values for **every** previous token, at every layer. The previous tokens' K and V don't change. Without a cache, step t recomputes K and V for all t tokens, so generating n tokens costs O(n^2) extra work.\n\n## The cache\nStore each layer's K and V for every token once. At each decode step, compute q, k, v only for the new token, append k and v to the cache, and attend over the cached keys and values.\n\n%%flow One decode step with a KV cache.\nNew token -(project)-> q, k, v -(append k, v)-> *KV cache (all previous tokens) -(attention with q)-> Output -(next layer)-> ...\n%%\n\nPrefill fills the cache for the whole prompt in one pass; decode then reads it every step. This is why decode is memory-bandwidth bound: each step reads all weights and the whole cache for very little compute.\n\n## How big it gets\n```\nbytes per token = 2 x layers x kv_heads x head_dim x bytes_per_value\n```\n\n! Example: Llama-3-8B-like model, 8,000 tokens\n! 2 x 32 layers x 8 KV heads x 128 dims x 2 bytes (fp16) = 131,072 bytes, about 128 KB per token.\n! 8,000 tokens x 128 KB is about 1 GB for one sequence.\n! A batch of 32 such sequences needs about 32 GB of cache, two times the 16 GB of fp16 weights.\n\nPope et al.'s inference analysis shows this pattern: at large batch and long context, the KV cache, not the weights, sets how many requests fit on a GPU.\n\n## Making it smaller\n| Technique | How it shrinks the cache |\n| Multi-query attention (MQA) | All query heads share one K/V head |\n| Grouped-query attention (GQA) | Groups of query heads share K/V heads (Llama 3: 32 Q heads, 8 KV heads, 4x smaller) |\n| KV quantization | Store K/V in fp8 or int8 instead of fp16 |\n| Sliding-window attention | Keep only the last W tokens for some layers |\n| Multi-head latent attention (DeepSeek) | Store a compressed latent and expand it on the fly |\n| Paged attention (module 6) | Doesn't shrink entries, but removes wasted, fragmented space |\n\n## Prompt caching\nMany requests share a prefix: the same system prompt, the same long document. Servers and APIs can keep the KV cache for that prefix and reuse it, skipping its prefill. That cuts time to first token and cost, which is why API providers price cached input tokens much lower.\n\n## Size the cache for your deployment\nThe KV cache stores the keys and values of every past token, in every layer, so decode doesn't recompute them. Its size is easy to compute and often surprising. Set a model and a workload below.\n\n%%widget kvcache KV cache memory per sequence and for a batch, next to the model weights.\n\nThe formula:\n\n```\nbytes per token = 2 (K and V) × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes per value\n```\n\nFor Llama 3 8B in bf16: 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes, which is 128 KiB per token.\n\n- One 8,192-token conversation: 1 GiB.\n- 32 such conversations at once: 32 GiB, twice the size of the model weights.\n- With full multi-head attention (32 KV heads instead of 8) it would be 4× larger. That is why GQA (05.02) became standard.\n\n## Why the cache sets your throughput\nServing cost is mostly decided by how many sequences you can decode at once (the batch). Each decode step reads all the weights once regardless of batch size, so a bigger batch spreads that cost over more users. The batch size is capped by how many KV caches fit in the memory left after the weights. Shrinking the cache is therefore one of the most direct ways to cut cost per token:\n\n| Technique | Cache reduction | Cost |\n| GQA (8 KV heads instead of 32) | 4× | Built into the model; slight quality change |\n| FP8 KV cache | 2× versus bf16 | Small accuracy loss on some tasks |\n| Sliding window (07.02) | Bounded by window size | Can't attend beyond the window directly |\n| Paged allocation (06.01) | Removes wasted reservation | Engine support (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM) |\n\n## Prompt caching from the outside\nHosted APIs reuse the cache across requests that share a prefix. A 10,000-token system prompt plus tool definitions, sent on every call, is processed once and then read from cache. Providers typically bill cached input tokens at a fraction of the normal price. To benefit, keep the stable part of the prompt first and byte-identical; put anything that changes per request (the date, the user's name) after it.\n\n> Takeaway: the KV cache trades memory for compute by storing past keys and values. At long context and high batch it dominates GPU memory, which is what GQA, KV quantization and paging attack.", "s": "Per token: 2 (K and V) x 32 layers x 8 KV heads x 128 head dim x 2 bytes = 131,072 bytes = 128 KB. For 8,000 tokens: 8,000 x 128 KB = 1,024,000 KB, about 1 GB (0.98 GiB) per sequence."}, {"id": "04.05", "t": "What attention changes", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/what-is-attention-and-why-does-it-matter", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Bahdanau et al.: Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate (2014)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0473", "Attention added to RNNs, before transformers."], ["Lilian Weng: Attention? Attention!", "https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2018-06-24-attention/", "History and variants of attention."], ["Anthropic: In-context learning and induction heads", "https://transformer-circuits.pub/2022/in-context-learning-and-induction-heads/index.html", "Attention heads that copy patterns, behind in-context learning."]], "q": "Name two limits of RNNs that attention removed, and one new cost that attention introduced.", "n": ["Before transformers, RNNs read text one token at a time and squeezed everything into a fixed-size hidden state.", "That made long-range dependencies hard (information fades) and training slow (no parallelism across positions).", "Bahdanau et al. (2014) added attention to RNN translation so the decoder could look back at any input word.", "Transformers (2017) dropped recurrence entirely: every token attends directly to every other, in one parallel step.", "This enabled training on GPUs at huge scale, and behaviours like in-context learning via 'induction heads'.", "The price: compute and memory grow quadratically with context length, and a KV cache that grows with every token."], "ak": "", "a": "## Before attention\nRecurrent neural networks (RNNs, LSTMs) read a sequence one step at a time, updating a hidden state vector. For translation, an encoder RNN read the whole source sentence into one final vector, and a decoder RNN generated the translation from it.\n\nTwo problems followed:\n- **Bottleneck**: a 50-word sentence had to fit in one fixed-size vector. Long sentences translated badly.\n- **Sequential training**: step t needs step t-1, so you can't parallelise over positions. GPUs sat mostly idle.\n\n## Attention as a fix (2014)\nBahdanau et al. let the decoder look back at **all** encoder states at each output step and take a weighted mix of them. Translation of long sentences improved sharply, and the attention weights showed sensible word alignments. Lilian Weng's post traces this history and the variants that followed.\n\n## Attention as the whole model (2017)\n\"Attention Is All You Need\" removed recurrence. Every token attends to every other token directly in each layer.\n\n| Aspect | RNN / LSTM | Transformer |\n| Path between distant tokens | O(n) steps | 1 step |\n| Training parallelism | Sequential over positions | Fully parallel |\n| Memory of the past | Fixed-size hidden state | All previous tokens (KV cache) |\n| Cost per layer | O(n) | O(n^2) |\n| Inference state | Constant size | Grows with context |\n\n## What it enabled\n- **Scale**: parallel training made it practical to train on trillions of tokens, which is what scaling laws needed.\n- **Long-range use of context**: any earlier token is one hop away.\n- **In-context learning**: Anthropic's interpretability work found \"induction heads\", attention circuits that spot a pattern earlier in the context ([A][B] ... [A]) and predict what followed it ([B]). These appear at the same point in training as in-context learning ability.\n\n%%flow An induction head completes a pattern seen earlier in the context.\n\"Mr Dursley ... Mr\" -(attend to token after earlier \"Mr\")-> *\"Dursley\" -(copy)-> Predict \"Dursley\"\n%%\n\n## What it costs\nAttention's direct access is also its weakness. Every token compares with every other, so cost grows with n^2, and the KV cache grows with every token generated. Most of modules 4 to 7 is about paying this bill: KV caches, GQA, paged attention, FlashAttention, sparse attention. Newer recurrent-style models (state space models like Mamba) try to bring back constant-size state, usually mixed with some attention layers.\n\n## Sequential versus parallel, in numbers\nAn RNN processes a sequence one step at a time: step 500 can't start until step 499 is done. Attention computes every pair of positions at once. The figure plots both as sequence length grows.\n\n%%widget complexity Sequential steps for an RNN versus token pairs for attention, by sequence length.\n\nThis is the whole trade in one picture:\n\n- **RNN**: n sequential steps, little total work, but the steps can't be spread across thousands of GPU cores. Information from token 1 has to survive n − 1 updates to reach token n.\n- **Attention**: about n²/2 pairs for causal attention, a lot of total work, but all of it runs in parallel, and token n reads token 1 directly in one step.\n\nGPUs are built for exactly the second kind of work. That is why the 2017 Transformer trained in 3.5 days on 8 GPUs to beat recurrent models that took far longer, and why training scaled to trillions of tokens afterwards.\n\n## The quadratic bill\nn² is fine at 2,000 tokens (4 million pairs per head per layer) and painful at 128,000 (16 billion). Three lines of work came out of that bill, all covered later in the course:\n\n1. **Do the same math with less memory traffic**: FlashAttention (06.03). Still n² operations, far faster in practice.\n2. **Skip most pairs**: sparse and sliding-window attention (07.02).\n3. **Replace attention in some layers**: state-space models such as Mamba and hybrid models mix linear-time layers with a few attention layers. They are an active research area; most frontier models still rely mainly on attention.\n\n! Example: why long prompts are slow to start\n! Doubling a prompt from 32k to 64k tokens doubles the MLP work in prefill but quadruples the attention-score work.\n! At short lengths the MLP dominates, so prefill time grows roughly linearly. Past tens of thousands of tokens, attention starts to dominate and time to first token grows faster than the prompt.\n\n> Takeaway: attention replaced a fixed-size memory with direct access to every token, unlocking parallel training and in-context learning. The cost is quadratic compute and a cache that grows with context.", "s": "Attention removed the RNN's fixed-size hidden-state bottleneck (any token is one step from any other, so long-range dependencies don't fade) and the sequential training constraint (all positions are processed in parallel, enabling large-scale GPU training). The new cost: compute and memory quadratic in sequence length, plus a KV cache that grows with every token at inference."}]}, {"n": "05", "name": "Transformer walkthrough", "lessons": [{"id": "05.01", "t": "Masked attention", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/masked-attention", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Jay Alammar: The Illustrated GPT-2", "https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-gpt2/", "Masked self-attention with diagrams."], ["Andrej Karpathy: Let's build GPT from scratch (video)", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kCc8FmEb1nY", "Implements the causal mask with a lower-triangular matrix."], ["PyTorch: scaled_dot_product_attention", "https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention.html", "is_causal flag and attention masks in practice."]], "q": "Write the 4x4 causal mask for a 4-token sequence and explain how it is applied and why it makes parallel training possible.", "n": ["In a decoder, token i must not see tokens after i, or training would let it cheat by reading the answer.", "Causal masking sets scores for future positions to minus infinity before softmax, so their weights become 0.", "The mask is lower-triangular: row i allows columns 0..i.", "With the mask, one forward pass trains next-token prediction at every position in parallel.", "Padding masks are separate: they hide padding tokens in batched sequences of different lengths.", "At inference with a KV cache, the new token only attends to past tokens anyway, so no explicit mask is needed for decode."], "ak": "", "a": "## The cheating problem\nIn training, the model sees a whole sequence and must predict token i+1 from tokens 0..i. Plain self-attention lets every token look at every other, including future ones. Token 2 could simply attend to token 3 and copy it. Loss would drop, and the model would learn nothing usable for generation, where the future doesn't exist yet.\n\n## The causal mask\nBefore softmax, add a mask to the n x n score matrix: 0 where attention is allowed, minus infinity where it isn't. Softmax of minus infinity is 0, so future tokens get zero weight.\n\n```\n          k0    k1    k2    k3\nq0   [    0   -inf  -inf  -inf ]\nq1   [    0     0   -inf  -inf ]\nq2   [    0     0     0   -inf ]\nq3   [    0     0     0     0  ]\n```\n\n%%flow Where the mask goes inside attention.\nQ K^T / sqrt(d) -(+ causal mask)-> *Future scores = -inf -(softmax)-> Weights with zeros above diagonal -(x V)-> Output\n%%\n\nKarpathy's GPT video builds this with a lower-triangular matrix of ones (torch.tril) and masked_fill. In PyTorch today you just pass is_causal=True to scaled_dot_product_attention.\n\n## Why this gives parallel training\nWith the mask, row i of the output depends only on tokens 0..i. So the output at every position is a valid prediction of its next token, and one forward pass yields n training targets. This is the main reason transformers train so much faster than RNNs on the same hardware.\n\n## Other masks\n- **Padding mask**: batches hold sequences of different lengths padded to the same size; the mask hides pad tokens.\n- **Document masks**: when several short documents are packed into one training sequence, a block-diagonal mask stops attention across documents.\n- **Encoder models (BERT)**: no causal mask; every token sees both directions, which is why they're good for embeddings but not for generation.\n- **Sliding-window mask**: allow only the last W positions (Mistral, Gemma local layers).\n\n## At inference\nDuring decode with a KV cache, there is only one new query, and the cache only holds the past. There is nothing in the future to hide, so decode needs no explicit causal mask. Prefill of a prompt still uses it.\n\n## Compare the masks\nA mask is a matrix of allowed (query, key) pairs. Disallowed pairs get −∞ added to their score before softmax, which makes their weight exactly zero. Switch between the common masks below.\n\n%%widget mask Causal, bidirectional, sliding-window, packed-document and padding masks.\n\nWhat each is for:\n\n- **Causal**: decoders (GPT, Llama, Claude). Token i sees tokens 0 to i. This is what lets one forward pass train on every position at once without leaking the answer.\n- **Bidirectional**: encoders (BERT, embedding models). Every token sees every token, so the model can use both sides of a word. It can't generate left to right, which is why embedding models and chat models are different models.\n- **Sliding window**: each token sees only the last w tokens. Bounded cost per token (07.02).\n- **Packed documents**: training batches concatenate short documents into one long sequence to avoid padding waste. A block-diagonal causal mask stops document 2 from attending to document 1.\n- **Padding**: in a batch of different-length sequences, the shorter ones are padded. Pad positions are masked so no real token attends to them.\n\n## Why causal masking gives \"free\" training examples\nA 4,096-token training sequence with a causal mask is 4,096 next-token prediction problems solved in one pass: predict token 2 from token 1, token 3 from tokens 1 and 2, and so on, all in parallel. The loss is the average over every position. Without the mask, position 3 could simply read token 4 and the model would learn to copy instead of predict.\n\n! Example: the off-by-one that ruins a training run\n! You build inputs and labels from the same token array and forget to shift labels by one. Each position is now asked to predict its own token, which it can see.\n! Training loss drops to near zero in a few hundred steps, and the model generates nonsense. A loss that looks too good is a masking or shifting bug until proven otherwise.\n\n> Takeaway: the causal mask sets future scores to minus infinity before softmax, so each position sees only its past. That keeps training honest and lets one pass train every position at once.", "s": "Mask (0 = allowed, -inf = blocked): row0 [0,-inf,-inf,-inf], row1 [0,0,-inf,-inf], row2 [0,0,0,-inf], row3 [0,0,0,0]. It is added to the scaled QK^T scores before softmax, so future positions get weight 0. Since each output position only depends on earlier tokens, every position is a valid next-token prediction, so one forward pass trains all n positions in parallel without leaking the answer."}, {"id": "05.02", "t": "Multi-head attention", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/multihead-attention", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Jay Alammar: The Illustrated Transformer", "https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/", "The multi-head section shows heads attending to different words."], ["Vaswani et al.: Attention Is All You Need (2017)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762", "Section 3.2.2: multi-head attention."], ["Shazeer: Fast Transformer Decoding, multi-query attention (2019)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02150", "One shared K/V head to speed up decoding."]], "q": "A model has d_model = 4096 and 32 heads. What is each head's dimension, and how do multi-head outputs get back to size 4096?", "n": ["Instead of one big attention, split Q, K and V into h smaller heads that attend independently.", "Each head has dimension d_model / h (4096 / 32 = 128). Total compute is about the same as one full-size head.", "Different heads learn different patterns: previous token, syntax, coreference, copying.", "Outputs of all heads are concatenated back to d_model and passed through an output projection W_O.", "Multi-query (MQA) and grouped-query attention (GQA) share K/V across heads to shrink the KV cache with little quality loss.", "Tensor shapes: (batch, seq, d_model) -> reshape to (batch, heads, seq, head_dim) -> attention -> back."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why more than one head\nA single attention head computes one set of weights per token: one way of deciding what matters. But a token often needs several kinds of information at once: the previous word, the subject of the verb, the noun a pronoun refers to. Multi-head attention runs several smaller attentions in parallel, each with its own Q, K and V projections, so each can specialise.\n\n## How it's computed\nWith d_model = 4,096 and h = 32 heads, each head works in 128 dimensions.\n\n%%flow Multi-head attention for one layer.\nX (seq x 4096) -(W_Q, W_K, W_V)-> Q, K, V -(split into 32 heads)-> 32 x (seq x 128) -(attention per head)-> *32 head outputs -(concat)-> seq x 4096 -(W_O)-> Output\n%%\n\n```\nx: (batch, seq, 4096)\nq = x @ W_Q            -> (batch, seq, 4096)\nq = q.view(batch, seq, 32, 128).transpose(1, 2)  -> (batch, 32, seq, 128)\n# same for k, v\nout = softmax(q @ k.T / sqrt(128)) @ v            -> (batch, 32, seq, 128)\nout = out.transpose(1, 2).reshape(batch, seq, 4096)\nout = out @ W_O\n```\n\nBecause the heads split the dimension rather than add to it, total compute is about the same as one full-width head. You get h different attention patterns for free.\n\n## What heads learn\nAlammar's Illustrated Transformer shows two heads for the word \"it\": one focused on \"animal\", another on \"tired\". Interpretability research has found heads that track the previous token, heads that match syntax, and induction heads that copy patterns (lesson 04.05). Many heads are redundant; some can be pruned with little loss.\n\n## Sharing keys and values\nFor decoding speed, the KV cache matters more than compute. Variants:\n| Variant | Query heads | K/V heads | KV cache vs MHA |\n| Multi-head (MHA) | h | h | 1x |\n| Grouped-query (GQA) | h | g (e.g. 8) | g / h (e.g. 1/4) |\n| Multi-query (MQA) | h | 1 | 1 / h |\n\nShazeer's multi-query attention paper showed one shared K/V head makes decoding much faster. GQA (Llama 2 70B, Llama 3, Mistral) is the middle ground that keeps quality close to MHA.\n\n## Heads, groups and memory\nMulti-head attention splits d_model into h heads that each attend independently. Variants differ in how many key/value heads they keep. Pick a variant and see the parameter count and KV cache change.\n\n%%widget heads Multi-head, grouped-query and multi-query attention: weights and KV cache per token.\n\n| Variant | Query heads | KV heads | KV cache | Used in |\n| MHA | h | h | 1× | Original Transformer, GPT-2, Llama 1 |\n| GQA | h | g (for example 8) | g / h | Llama 2 70B, Llama 3, Mistral, most current open models |\n| MQA | h | 1 | 1 / h | PaLM, Falcon |\n\nGQA (Ainslie et al., 2023) is the compromise that won: with 8 KV groups for 32 or 64 query heads, quality is close to full MHA and the KV cache is 4 to 8× smaller. MQA saves more memory but tends to lose a bit more quality and can be less stable to train.\n\n## The reshape, step by step\nFor d_model = 4,096 and 32 heads:\n\n1. x has shape [batch, seq, 4,096].\n2. Q = x · W_q gives [batch, seq, 4,096]. Reshape to [batch, seq, 32, 128], then transpose to [batch, 32, seq, 128].\n3. Each of the 32 heads computes its own [seq, seq] attention matrix and a [seq, 128] output.\n4. Transpose back and reshape to [batch, seq, 4,096], then multiply by W_o.\n\nNo extra parameters come from having more heads: 32 heads of 128 and 1 head of 4,096 use the same W_q, W_k, W_v and W_o. Heads only change how the same projections are split, so the model can attend to several things at once.\n\n## Do heads specialise?\nSome do, measurably. Researchers have found heads that attend to the previous token, heads that copy from an earlier occurrence of the current token (\"induction heads\", Olsson et al., 2022) and heads that track syntax. Many heads can also be pruned with little loss, which suggests a lot of redundancy. Treat \"head 7 does grammar\" stories as partial descriptions, not a design you can rely on.\n\n> Takeaway: multi-head attention splits d_model into h heads of size d_model/h, runs attention in each, concatenates, and projects with W_O. Sharing K/V across heads (GQA, MQA) keeps the benefit while shrinking the cache.", "s": "Head dimension = 4096 / 32 = 128. Q, K and V are reshaped into 32 heads of 128 dims; each head computes attention independently; the 32 outputs (each seq x 128) are concatenated back to seq x 4096 and multiplied by the output projection W_O (4096 x 4096) to mix information across heads."}, {"id": "05.03", "t": "Trace the shapes through a transformer", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/summary-pdf--transformer-architecture", "k": ["Exercise"], "x": [], "r": [["Andrej Karpathy: nanoGPT model.py", "https://github.com/karpathy/nanoGPT/blob/master/model.py", "Read the shapes in real code."], ["Jay Alammar: The Illustrated GPT-2", "https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-gpt2/", "Matrix shapes at each stage, with pictures."], ["Hugging Face: Llama model code", "https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/main/src/transformers/models/llama/modeling_llama.py", "A production decoder with GQA and RoPE."]], "q": "For batch 2, sequence 10, d_model 512, 8 heads, vocab 32,000: give the tensor shape after embedding, after splitting into heads, the attention score matrix, and the final logits.", "n": ["Input token ids: (B, T).", "Embedding lookup: (B, T, d_model).", "Q, K, V after projection and head split: (B, h, T, d_head), with d_head = d_model / h.", "Scores Q K^T: (B, h, T, T). Softmax and multiply by V: (B, h, T, d_head).", "Merge heads and project: (B, T, d_model). MLP expands to (B, T, d_ff) and back. Shape is unchanged between blocks.", "LM head: (B, T, vocab). At inference only the last position's row is sampled."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why trace shapes\nMost bugs in model code are shape bugs, and most performance reasoning starts from shapes: which matrix is T x T, which grows with batch, which grows with vocabulary. This exercise follows one batch through a decoder.\n\nWe use: batch B = 2, sequence T = 10, d_model = 512, heads h = 8 (so d_head = 64), MLP size d_ff = 2,048, vocabulary V = 32,000.\n\n## The trace\n| Stage | Shape | Example |\n| Token ids | (B, T) | (2, 10) |\n| Token embedding | (B, T, d_model) | (2, 10, 512) |\n| Q, K, V projections | (B, T, d_model) each | (2, 10, 512) |\n| Split into heads | (B, h, T, d_head) | (2, 8, 10, 64) |\n| Scores Q K^T | (B, h, T, T) | (2, 8, 10, 10) |\n| Softmax(scores) x V | (B, h, T, d_head) | (2, 8, 10, 64) |\n| Merge heads + W_O | (B, T, d_model) | (2, 10, 512) |\n| MLP up | (B, T, d_ff) | (2, 10, 2048) |\n| MLP down | (B, T, d_model) | (2, 10, 512) |\n| After N blocks + final norm | (B, T, d_model) | (2, 10, 512) |\n| LM head | (B, T, V) | (2, 10, 32000) |\n\n%%flow The shape stays (B, T, d_model) between blocks; only attention scores and logits are different.\n(B, T) -(embed)-> (B, T, 512) -(heads)-> (B, 8, T, 64) -(QK^T)-> *(B, 8, T, T) -(x V, merge)-> (B, T, 512) -(LM head)-> (B, T, 32000)\n%%\n\n## What the shapes tell you\n- **Every block keeps (B, T, d_model).** That's what makes blocks stackable and residual adds possible.\n- **The score matrix is (B, h, T, T).** It's the only tensor quadratic in T. At T = 100,000 one head's scores alone are 10^10 entries, which is why FlashAttention never materialises it.\n- **Logits are (B, T, V).** With a 128k vocabulary, logits for a long sequence are huge. In training they are often computed in chunks; at inference only the last position is needed.\n- **Parameter count** comes from weight shapes, not activations: W_Q, W_K, W_V, W_O are each 512 x 512; MLP weights are 512 x 2,048 and 2,048 x 512.\n\n## With GQA and a KV cache\nIn a Llama-style model with 2 KV heads instead of 8, K and V become (B, 2, T, 64) and are repeated across 4 query heads each. During decode, Q is (B, h, 1, 64) while the cached K and V are (B, kv_heads, T_past, 64), so scores are (B, h, 1, T_past): linear in context, not quadratic.\n\n## Trace a batch through the model\nSet the batch size, sequence length and model width, and follow the tensor shape through every stage of a forward pass.\n\n%%widget shapes Tensor shapes through embedding, attention, MLP and the output head.\n\nReading the trace, three facts stand out:\n\n- **The residual stream never changes shape.** [B, T, d_model] goes in to every block and comes out of every block. That is what makes blocks stackable.\n- **The attention score tensor is the only one that grows with T².** [B, heads, T, T] is the memory problem FlashAttention (06.03) avoids by never storing it.\n- **The output head is huge at the end.** [B, T, vocab] logits with a 128k vocabulary is 128,000 numbers per position. During training this tensor alone can take more memory than the activations of a whole layer; libraries compute the loss in chunks to avoid materialising it.\n\n## Shapes during generation\nIn decode with a KV cache, the new token has T = 1:\n\n```\nx          [B, 1, d_model]\nQ          [B, h, 1, d_head]\nK, V cache [B, g, T_past + 1, d_head]   (g = KV heads, shared by h / g query heads)\nscores     [B, h, 1, T_past + 1]\noutput     [B, 1, d_model] -> logits [B, 1, vocab]\n```\n\nThe work per step is now a thin matrix-vector product rather than a matrix-matrix product. That is why decode is bound by memory bandwidth (reading weights and cache) rather than by compute, and why batching many users together is so effective.\n\n! Example: a shape bug caught early\n! You implement GQA and forget to repeat the 8 KV heads to match 32 query heads. The matmul of [B, 32, T, 128] by [B, 8, 128, T] fails with a shape error, or worse, broadcasts silently in a library that allows it.\n! Writing the expected shape next to every line, as in the trace above, catches this before any training run.\n\n> Takeaway: (B, T) to (B, T, d) to (B, h, T, d_head) to (B, h, T, T) and back to (B, T, d), then (B, T, vocab). Knowing where T x T and vocab appear tells you where memory goes.", "s": "After embedding: (2, 10, 512). After splitting into heads: (2, 8, 10, 64) for each of Q, K, V (d_head = 512/8 = 64). Attention scores QK^T: (2, 8, 10, 10). Final logits: (2, 10, 32000)."}]}, {"n": "06", "name": "LLM optimizations", "lessons": [{"id": "06.01", "t": "Paged attention", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/paged-attention", "k": [], "x": ["vLLM"], "r": [["Kwon et al.: Efficient Memory Management for LLM Serving with PagedAttention (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180", "The vLLM paper."], ["vLLM blog: Easy, Fast, and Cheap LLM Serving with PagedAttention", "https://blog.vllm.ai/2023/06/20/vllm.html", "Plain-language intro with animations."], ["vLLM documentation", "https://docs.vllm.ai/", "The open-source serving engine built on it."]], "q": "Why did pre-vLLM servers waste most of their KV cache memory, and how does paging fix it?", "n": ["Older servers reserved one contiguous KV cache block per request, sized for the maximum possible length.", "Most requests are shorter than the maximum, so most of that memory sat unused (internal fragmentation) and gaps between requests couldn't be reused (external fragmentation).", "The vLLM paper measured only 20-38% of KV cache memory actually holding tokens in existing systems.", "PagedAttention borrows virtual memory from operating systems: the cache is split into fixed-size blocks (e.g. 16 tokens), allocated on demand.", "A per-request block table maps logical blocks to physical blocks, so a sequence's cache need not be contiguous.", "Blocks can be shared across sequences (copy-on-write) for parallel sampling, beam search and shared prompt prefixes. Waste drops below 4%, and throughput rose 2-4x."], "ak": "", "a": "## The memory problem\nLesson 04.04 showed that the KV cache can take more GPU memory than the weights. The more requests whose caches fit at once, the larger the batch and the higher the throughput. So wasted cache memory directly means lost throughput.\n\nBefore vLLM, serving systems allocated each request a **contiguous** chunk of KV memory big enough for its maximum length (say 2,048 tokens), because they didn't know how long the output would be. Kwon et al. found three kinds of waste:\n- **Reserved**: space held for tokens not generated yet.\n- **Internal fragmentation**: space never used because the request finished early.\n- **External fragmentation**: gaps between allocations too small to use.\nOnly 20-38% of KV cache memory held actual token states in the systems they measured.\n\n## The OS idea\nOperating systems solved the same problem decades ago with virtual memory and paging: give each process a contiguous **logical** address space, backed by fixed-size **physical** pages placed anywhere.\n\n%%flow PagedAttention maps each sequence's logical KV blocks onto any free physical blocks.\nSequence tokens -(every 16 tokens)-> Logical blocks 0, 1, 2 -(block table)-> *Physical blocks 7, 1, 3 -(attention kernel reads)-> Output\n%%\n\nPagedAttention:\n1. Splits the KV cache into blocks of a fixed number of tokens (16 by default).\n2. Allocates a new block only when a sequence fills its current one.\n3. Keeps a **block table** per sequence mapping logical to physical blocks.\n4. Uses an attention kernel that gathers K and V from non-contiguous blocks.\n\nThe only waste is the unfilled part of each sequence's last block: under 4% in the paper.\n\n## Sharing blocks\nBecause blocks are indirect, two sequences can point at the same physical block:\n- **Parallel sampling** (n completions for one prompt): all share the prompt's blocks.\n- **Beam search**: beams share their common history.\n- **Shared prefixes**: many requests with the same system prompt share its blocks (prefix caching).\nA reference count tracks sharing; when a shared block must change, it is copied first (copy-on-write).\n\n## Results\nvLLM reported 2-4x higher throughput than prior systems at the same latency, with bigger gains for long sequences and parallel sampling. The block idea is now standard in serving engines (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM, TGI).\n\n## Watch the allocator\nRequests arrive with different lengths and finish at different times. A contiguous allocator reserves the maximum length up front for each request; a paged allocator hands out fixed-size blocks as tokens are generated. Step through the simulation and compare wasted memory.\n\n%%widget paging Contiguous reservation versus paged blocks for a stream of requests.\n\nThe Kwon et al. (2023) vLLM paper measured that earlier serving systems wasted 60 to 80% of KV cache memory on reservation and fragmentation, and that paging cut waste to under 4%. More free memory means a bigger batch, which is where the reported 2 to 4× throughput gain came from.\n\n## How the block table works\nEach sequence has a block table, like a page table in an operating system:\n\n```\nsequence 17: logical block 0 -> physical block 812\n             logical block 1 -> physical block 45\n             logical block 2 -> physical block 1,203   (partly filled)\n```\n\nThe attention kernel reads keys and values through this table, so the blocks can be anywhere in GPU memory. When a sequence finishes, its blocks go straight back to a free list. With 16-token blocks, the only waste per sequence is the unused part of its last block: at most 15 tokens.\n\n## Sharing is the second win\nTwo requests with the same system prompt can point their first blocks at the same physical blocks. A copy is only made when one of them writes to a shared block (copy-on-write). This enables:\n\n- **Parallel sampling**: n candidate answers from one prompt share the prompt's blocks.\n- **Beam search**: beams share their common prefix.\n- **Prefix caching across requests**: a long system prompt is stored once for every user who sends it. SGLang's RadixAttention generalises this with a tree of shared prefixes.\n\n> Takeaway: PagedAttention stores the KV cache in small fixed-size blocks mapped through a block table, like OS pages. Near-zero fragmentation means bigger batches, and shared blocks make prefix reuse cheap.", "s": "They pre-allocated one contiguous KV region per request sized to the maximum sequence length, so space reserved for tokens never generated, early finishes (internal fragmentation) and unusable gaps between allocations (external fragmentation) left only ~20-38% of memory holding real tokens. PagedAttention splits the cache into fixed-size blocks allocated on demand and tracked by a per-sequence block table, so memory needn't be contiguous, waste is limited to the last partial block (<4%), and blocks can be shared copy-on-write across sequences."}, {"id": "06.02", "t": "Mixture of experts", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/mixture-of-experts", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Hugging Face: Mixture of Experts Explained", "https://huggingface.co/blog/moe", "Routers, load balancing, and serving trade-offs."], ["Fedus et al.: Switch Transformers (2021)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.03961", "Top-1 routing to scale to a trillion parameters."], ["Jiang et al.: Mixtral of Experts (2024)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04088", "8 experts, top-2 routing, 47B total and 13B active parameters."]], "q": "Mixtral 8x7B has about 47B parameters but uses about 13B per token. Explain why, and what that means for GPU memory versus compute.", "n": ["A mixture-of-experts (MoE) layer replaces one big MLP with many expert MLPs plus a small router.", "For each token the router picks the top-k experts (often k = 1 or 2); only those run.", "Total parameters (capacity) grow with the number of experts; active parameters (compute per token) stay small.", "Training needs a load-balancing loss so tokens don't all go to a few experts.", "Serving cost: all experts must sit in memory even though each token uses a few, so MoE is memory-heavy and compute-light.", "Expert parallelism spreads experts across GPUs; tokens are sent to the GPU holding their expert (all-to-all communication)."], "ak": "", "a": "## Capacity without the compute\nIn a dense model every parameter is used for every token. Making the model bigger makes every token more expensive. Mixture of experts breaks that link: the model has many more parameters, but each token only passes through a few of them.\n\n## How an MoE layer works\nThe attention part of the block stays the same. The MLP is replaced by N expert MLPs and a router (a small linear layer).\n\n%%flow Top-2 routing in one MoE layer.\nToken vector -(router scores)-> Pick top 2 of 8 experts -(run)-> *Expert 3, Expert 6 -(weighted sum)-> Layer output\n%%\n\n1. The router computes a score per expert for the token.\n2. Keep the top-k (Switch Transformer: k = 1; Mixtral: k = 2).\n3. Run only those experts and combine their outputs, weighted by the router's softmax scores.\n\nHugging Face's MoE explainer notes that \"experts\" don't specialise in human topics like biology or law; they tend to specialise in token types and patterns, and routing differs per token and per layer.\n\n## Total vs active parameters\nMixtral 8x7B (Jiang et al., 2024) has 8 experts per layer and top-2 routing. Attention and embeddings are shared, so it's about 47B parameters total rather than 56B, and about 13B are used per token.\n\n| Aspect | Dense 13B | Mixtral 8x7B |\n| Parameters in memory | 13B | ~47B |\n| Parameters used per token | 13B | ~13B |\n| Compute per token | ~same | ~same |\n| Quality | Lower | Close to much larger dense models |\n\nSo an MoE model **costs memory like a big model and compute like a small one**. DeepSeek-V3 pushes this further: 671B total, 37B active.\n\n## Training problems\n- **Load imbalance**: the router can collapse onto a few favourite experts. An auxiliary load-balancing loss (Switch Transformers) or bias-based balancing (DeepSeek) spreads tokens out.\n- **Capacity limits**: each expert processes a bounded number of tokens per batch; overflow tokens get dropped or rerouted.\n- **Instability**: router logits can blow up; tricks like router z-loss help.\n\n## Serving MoE\n- All experts must be loaded, so you need enough total GPU memory even though compute is light.\n- **Expert parallelism** places different experts on different GPUs; each layer does an all-to-all exchange to send tokens to their experts and back. Network bandwidth becomes a bottleneck.\n- Small batches waste the advantage: with few tokens, many experts sit idle while weights still occupy memory.\n\n## Route tokens to experts\nIn a mixture-of-experts layer, a small router scores every expert for each token, and only the top k experts run. Click through the tokens below and change k.\n\n%%widget moe Router scores, top-k selection and the active versus total parameter split.\n\nThe economics in one line: an MoE model has the knowledge capacity of its total parameters and roughly the per-token compute of its active parameters.\n\n| Model | Total parameters | Active per token | Experts |\n| Mixtral 8x7B | 46.7B | 12.9B | 8, top-2 |\n| DeepSeek-V3 | 671B | 37B | 256 routed + 1 shared, top-8 |\n\n## What the router learns, and what can go wrong\nThe router is a linear layer: logits = x · W_router, softmax, keep the top k, and use their weights to mix the expert outputs. Left alone, it collapses: a few experts get most tokens early, get better from the extra training, and attract even more tokens. Fixes:\n\n- **Load-balancing loss**: an extra term that penalises uneven routing across a batch (Switch Transformer, Mixtral).\n- **Capacity limits**: each expert takes at most a fixed number of tokens per batch; overflow tokens skip the layer through the residual.\n- **Bias-based balancing**: DeepSeek-V3 adjusts a per-expert bias during training instead of adding a loss term, reported to keep balance with less quality cost.\n\n## Serving consequences\nActive parameters set compute, but total parameters set memory: every expert must be loaded, because the next token may need any of them. Mixtral needs memory for 46.7B weights while computing like a 13B model. In large deployments experts are spread across GPUs (expert parallelism), and tokens are sent across the network to their experts and back each layer, so interconnect speed matters.\n\n! Example: is MoE cheaper for you?\n! At high batch sizes on enough GPUs, an MoE model gives near-dense quality at a fraction of the FLOPs per token.\n! On a single GPU with a small batch, the bottleneck is reading weights from memory, and you still have to hold every expert. A dense model of the same total size would be slower; a dense model of the same active size would fit in a quarter of the memory.\n\n> Takeaway: MoE gives each token a few expert MLPs chosen by a router, so parameter count rises without raising compute per token. You pay in memory, routing complexity and communication.", "s": "Mixtral has 8 expert MLPs per layer but routes each token to only the top 2. Attention and embedding weights are shared, so total parameters are ~47B (not 8x7B=56B), while each token only touches shared weights plus 2 experts, about 13B active parameters. All ~47B must be held in GPU memory, but compute (FLOPs) per token is similar to a 13B dense model."}, {"id": "06.03", "t": "FlashAttention", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/flash-attention", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Dao et al.: FlashAttention (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135", "IO-aware exact attention with tiling."], ["Dao: FlashAttention-2 (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.08691", "Better parallelism and work partitioning, about 2x faster again."], ["Horace He: Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr From First Principles", "https://horace.io/brrr_intro.html", "Compute-bound vs memory-bound, the background you need."]], "q": "FlashAttention does the same maths as standard attention. Where does its speedup come from, and why does it also save memory?", "n": ["On GPUs, attention is limited by memory traffic between slow HBM and fast on-chip SRAM, not by arithmetic.", "Standard attention writes the full T x T score matrix to HBM, reads it back for softmax, writes again, reads again for the V multiply.", "FlashAttention tiles Q, K and V into blocks that fit in SRAM and computes attention block by block, never storing the full matrix.", "Softmax is done incrementally with the online softmax trick: keep a running max and running sum, rescale as new blocks arrive.", "For the backward pass it recomputes attention instead of storing it, trading cheap FLOPs for expensive memory.", "Result: exact attention, memory linear in sequence length, and 2-4x speedups; FlashAttention-2 and -3 add better GPU utilisation."], "ak": "", "a": "## The real bottleneck\nHorace He's \"Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr\" explains the key fact: modern GPUs can do arithmetic far faster than they can move data from their main memory (HBM). An operation that reads and writes a lot of data but does little maths per byte is **memory-bound**, and speeding up the maths doesn't help.\n\nA GPU has two kinds of memory:\n| Memory | Size | Bandwidth |\n| HBM (main GPU memory) | tens of GB | a few TB/s |\n| SRAM (on-chip, per SM) | ~100-200 KB per SM | roughly an order of magnitude faster |\n\n## What standard attention does\n1. Compute S = Q K^T and write the T x T matrix to HBM.\n2. Read S, compute softmax P, write P to HBM.\n3. Read P and V, compute output O, write O.\n\nFor long sequences the T x T matrices are huge, and most of the time goes to these reads and writes, not the maths. Memory use also grows with T^2.\n\n## The FlashAttention idea\nDao et al. made attention **IO-aware**: never write S or P to HBM at all.\n\n%%flow FlashAttention keeps each tile in fast on-chip memory.\nQ block + K, V blocks (HBM) -(load tile)-> *SRAM: scores, partial softmax, partial output -(update running max and sum)-> Next K, V block -(after last block)-> Write O to HBM\n%%\n\n- **Tiling**: split Q, K and V into blocks small enough for SRAM. For each Q block, loop over K/V blocks.\n- **Online softmax**: softmax needs the max and sum over a whole row, which isn't known until all blocks are seen. Keep a running max and running sum, and rescale the partial output when a new block raises the max. The final result is exactly standard softmax.\n- **Recomputation**: in the backward pass, recompute attention scores from Q, K, V instead of storing the T x T matrix.\n\n## Results\n- **Exact**: not an approximation; outputs match standard attention up to floating-point rounding.\n- **Memory**: linear in sequence length instead of quadratic, which made long-context training practical.\n- **Speed**: 2-4x faster attention in the original paper. FlashAttention-2 (2023) roughly doubled that again with better parallelism across thread blocks and less non-matmul work. FlashAttention-3 targets Hopper GPU features (asynchrony, FP8).\n\nIt's built into PyTorch's scaled_dot_product_attention and into serving engines, so you usually get it without asking.\n\n## Tiles instead of a full matrix\nStandard attention writes the full n × n score matrix to GPU main memory (HBM), reads it back for softmax, writes the probabilities, and reads them again to multiply by V. FlashAttention processes queries and keys in tiles small enough to fit in on-chip SRAM and never writes the full matrix. Step through it below.\n\n%%widget flash Tiled attention: which blocks are in fast memory, and how much memory traffic is saved.\n\nThe memory hierarchy is what makes this matter. On an A100, HBM is 40 or 80 GB at about 1.5 to 2 TB/s; on-chip SRAM is about 20 MB in total at roughly 19 TB/s (figures from the FlashAttention paper). Attention does few arithmetic operations per byte moved, so it was limited by HBM traffic, not by math.\n\n## The online softmax trick\nSoftmax needs the maximum and the sum over the whole row, which seems to require the full row first. FlashAttention keeps a running maximum m and a running sum l per query row:\n\n```\nfor each key block j:\n    s      = q · K_j / sqrt(d)\n    m_new  = max(m, max(s))\n    l      = l * exp(m - m_new) + sum(exp(s - m_new))\n    out    = out * exp(m - m_new) + exp(s - m_new) · V_j\n    m      = m_new\nfinal output = out / l\n```\n\nWhen a new block raises the maximum, earlier partial results are rescaled by exp(m_old − m_new). The final answer is exactly the same as standard attention, not an approximation.\n\n## What changed in practice\n- Memory for attention became linear in sequence length, which made 32k to 128k training contexts affordable.\n- Dao et al. reported 2 to 4× faster attention and up to about 3× faster GPT-2 training end to end in the first paper (2022). FlashAttention-2 and -3 improved GPU utilisation further, with version 3 targeting H100 features such as FP8.\n- In the backward pass, the attention matrix is recomputed from Q, K and V instead of stored. Recomputing is cheaper than reading it back from HBM.\n\n> Takeaway: attention is memory-bound, not compute-bound. FlashAttention computes exact attention tile by tile in on-chip SRAM with an online softmax, so the T x T matrix never touches main memory.", "s": "The speedup comes from cutting reads and writes to slow GPU HBM: attention is memory-bound, and standard attention writes and re-reads the full T x T score and softmax matrices. FlashAttention tiles Q, K, V into SRAM-sized blocks, computes scores, an online (running max/sum) softmax and the output block by block, and writes only the final output. Since the T x T matrix is never materialised (and is recomputed in the backward pass instead of stored), memory is linear rather than quadratic in sequence length."}, {"id": "06.04", "t": "Work through a KV-cache allocation", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/paged-attention-pdf", "k": ["Exercise"], "x": [], "r": [["Kwon et al.: PagedAttention (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180", "Section 4: block tables, sharing and copy-on-write."], ["vLLM docs: Automatic prefix caching", "https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/features/automatic_prefix_caching/", "How shared prompt blocks are reused across requests."], ["Hugging Face: KV cache strategies", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/kv_cache", "Cache size and memory options."]], "q": "A GPU has 24 GB free for KV cache. Per-token KV size is 128 KB, block size is 16 tokens. How many blocks exist, and how many 1,000-token requests fit with paging vs with 4,096-token contiguous reservations?", "n": ["Step 1: per-token KV bytes = 2 x layers x kv_heads x head_dim x bytes.", "Step 2: per-block bytes = per-token bytes x block size.", "Step 3: number of blocks = free memory / per-block bytes.", "Step 4: blocks per request = ceil(tokens / block size). Waste is at most one partial block per sequence.", "Step 5: compare with contiguous allocation at the maximum length to see the batch-size gain.", "Shared prefixes: blocks of a common system prompt are counted once and shared by reference."], "ak": "", "a": "## The setup\nThis exercise uses the numbers from lessons 04.04 and 06.01. Model: 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128, fp16. GPU memory left after weights and activations: 24 GB. Block size: 16 tokens.\n\n## Step 1: bytes per token\n```\n2 (K and V) x 32 layers x 8 heads x 128 dims x 2 bytes = 131,072 bytes = 128 KB\n```\n\n## Step 2: bytes per block\n```\n128 KB x 16 tokens = 2 MB per block\n```\n\n## Step 3: how many blocks\n```\n24 GB / 2 MB = 12,288 blocks  (using 1 GB = 1,024 MB)\n```\nThat's 12,288 x 16 = 196,608 tokens of total KV capacity.\n\n## Step 4: paged allocation\nA request with 1,000 tokens (prompt plus output so far) needs ceil(1,000 / 16) = 63 blocks. The last block holds 8 tokens and wastes 8 slots, under 1%.\n\n```\n12,288 blocks / 63 blocks per request = 195 requests\n```\n\n## Step 5: contiguous allocation\nA pre-vLLM server reserves the maximum, 4,096 tokens, for every request:\n```\n4,096 x 128 KB = 512 MB per request\n24 GB / 512 MB = 48 requests\n```\nEach request only uses 1,000 of its 4,096 reserved slots, so about 76% of the memory is wasted.\n\n| Allocation | Requests in memory | Memory actually holding tokens |\n| Contiguous (max 4,096) | 48 | ~24% |\n| Paged (16-token blocks) | 195 | ~99% |\n\nFour times the batch means, roughly, up to four times the decode throughput, as long as compute isn't yet the limit. That's in line with the 2-4x the vLLM paper reports.\n\n## Step 6: shared prefix\nNow suppose every request starts with the same 512-token system prompt. That's 32 blocks, stored once and shared. Each request needs only its own ceil(488 / 16) = 31 blocks, plus the shared 32 counted once:\n```\n(12,288 - 32) / 31 = 395 requests\n```\n\n%%flow How the memory budget flows to batch size.\n24 GB free -(/ 2 MB)-> 12,288 blocks -(/ 63 per request)-> *195 concurrent requests -(shared 512-token prefix)-> ~395\n%%\n\n## Run the numbers for your own setup\nThe calculator below follows the six steps of this lesson. Change the free memory, model shape, block size and request lengths, and compare how many concurrent requests fit under contiguous, paged, and paged-with-shared-prefix allocation.\n\n%%widget pagecalc Concurrent requests that fit in GPU memory under three allocation schemes.\n\n## Sensitivity: which input matters most\nTry these in the calculator:\n\n- **Double the max length** (from 4k to 8k) while actual requests stay at 1k. Contiguous capacity halves; paged capacity doesn't move. Paging decouples capacity from the worst case.\n- **Double the actual tokens per request.** Both schemes lose capacity, because now the memory is really used.\n- **Grow the shared prefix.** With a 2,000-token system prompt and 500-token user turns, sharing the prefix nearly quadruples capacity, because 80% of each request's tokens are stored once.\n- **Change block size** from 16 to 128. Waste in the last block grows, but the effect is small next to the others. Engines choose 16 or 32 mostly for kernel efficiency.\n\n! Example: the capacity plan for a chat product\n! Llama 3 8B in bf16 on an 80 GB GPU: 16 GB weights, about 8 GB for activations and overhead, leaving about 56 GB for KV.\n! At 128 KiB per token, 56 GB holds about 430,000 tokens. With an average live conversation of 3,000 tokens, that is about 140 concurrent conversations with paging.\n! Contiguous allocation with an 8k reservation (1 GiB each) would fit about 50. Same GPU, same model, almost 3× the users.\n\n> Takeaway: per-token KV bytes x block size gives block size in bytes; free memory over that gives the block pool. Paging packs requests by actual length instead of maximum length, and shared prefixes stretch it further.", "s": "Block = 128 KB x 16 = 2 MB, so 24 GB / 2 MB = 12,288 blocks. A 1,000-token request needs ceil(1000/16) = 63 blocks, so paging fits 12,288 / 63 = 195 requests. Contiguous 4,096-token reservations cost 4,096 x 128 KB = 512 MB each, so only 24 GB / 512 MB = 48 fit: about 4x fewer, with ~76% of reserved memory unused."}]}, {"n": "07", "name": "Model tradeoffs", "lessons": [{"id": "07.01", "t": "Quantization", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/quantization", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Maarten Grootendorst: A Visual Guide to Quantization", "https://newsletter.maartengrootendorst.com/p/a-visual-guide-to-quantization", "Number formats, absmax, GPTQ, AWQ and GGUF with diagrams."], ["Frantar et al.: GPTQ (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17323", "Post-training 3-4 bit quantization using second-order information."], ["Lin et al.: AWQ (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00978", "Protect the weights that matter for large activations."]], "q": "A 70B model in fp16 needs how much memory for weights? What about int8 and 4-bit, and what do you risk at 4-bit?", "n": ["Quantization stores weights (and sometimes activations and KV cache) in fewer bits: fp16 to int8, int4 or fp8.", "Memory for weights = parameters x bits / 8. 70B in fp16 is 140 GB; int8 is 70 GB; 4-bit is about 35 GB.", "Decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so fewer bytes per weight also means faster tokens.", "Basic method: scale a block of values by its max (absmax) or min/max range to fit the integer range; store the scale.", "Outliers break naive quantization. GPTQ uses second-order information to correct errors; AWQ protects weights tied to large activations.", "Post-training quantization (PTQ) needs no retraining; quantization-aware training (QAT) trains with simulated low precision. Quality loss is small at 8-bit and grows below 4-bit."], "ak": "", "a": "## Fewer bits per weight\nA model's weights are numbers, usually stored as 16-bit floats (fp16 or bf16). Quantization stores them with fewer bits. Grootendorst's visual guide is the best single introduction.\n\n| Format | Bits | 70B model weights |\n| fp32 | 32 | 280 GB |\n| fp16 / bf16 | 16 | 140 GB |\n| int8 / fp8 | 8 | 70 GB |\n| int4 / 4-bit | 4 | ~35 GB (plus scales) |\n\nTwo benefits:\n- **Fits on fewer GPUs**: a 70B model at 4-bit fits on one 48 GB GPU or two 24 GB ones.\n- **Faster decode**: decode reads every weight per token and is memory-bound, so half the bytes is close to twice the tokens per second, if the kernels are good.\n\n## How a value gets quantized\nThe simplest scheme is **absmax**: for a block of weights, find the largest absolute value, scale so it maps to 127, round each value to the nearest integer.\n\n```\nscale = 127 / max(|w|)\nq = round(w x scale)        # int8\nw_restored = q / scale\n```\n\nThe rounding error is the quality cost. Store one scale per block (say 64 or 128 weights) instead of per tensor, so one large value doesn't wreck the precision of everything else.\n\n## The outlier problem\nLLM activations and some weights have rare, very large outliers. With absmax, a single outlier stretches the scale and every normal value gets squeezed into a few integer levels. Methods to deal with it:\n- **LLM.int8()**: keep outlier dimensions in fp16, quantize the rest.\n- **GPTQ** (Frantar et al.): quantize weights one column at a time and adjust the remaining weights to compensate for the error, using second-order (Hessian) information from a small calibration set.\n- **AWQ** (Lin et al.): find the ~1% of weight channels that multiply large activations and scale them to protect them before quantizing.\n- **GGUF k-quants** (llama.cpp): block-wise mixed-precision formats for CPU and laptop inference.\n\n## PTQ vs QAT\n%%flow Two ways to get a quantized model.\n[Post-training] Trained fp16 model -(calibrate on a small dataset)-> *Quantized model\n[Quantization-aware] Model -(train with simulated low precision)-> Quantized model that expects the noise\n%%\n\nPost-training quantization is fast and needs no training data beyond calibration. QAT is more work but recovers more quality at very low bits.\n\n## What you risk\nAt 8 bits the loss is usually negligible. At 4 bits most chat benchmarks hold up well, but losses show up first in maths, code, long-context recall and less common languages. Below 4 bits quality drops quickly for most models. Always evaluate on your own task.\n\n## Quantize a row of weights\nThe figure quantizes a row of weights to the chosen bit width. Add an outlier, change the block size that shares one scale, and watch the rounding error.\n\n%%widget quant Rounding weights to fewer bits, and how one outlier stretches the scale for its whole block.\n\nThe basic scheme, symmetric absmax quantization:\n\n```\nscale = max(|w|) / (2^(bits-1) - 1)        # 127 for int8, 7 for int4\nq     = round(w / scale)                    # stored as a small integer\nw_hat = q * scale                           # used at inference\n```\n\nWith int4 there are only 15 usable levels. If one weight is 20× larger than the rest, the scale is set by it, and most normal weights round to 0 or ±1. Smaller blocks (32 to 128 weights per scale) contain the damage, at the cost of storing more scales.\n\n## Methods you will see\n| Method | What it does | Typical use |\n| LLM.int8() | Keeps outlier feature dimensions in fp16, the rest in int8 | 8-bit inference; showed outliers appear around 6.7B parameters |\n| GPTQ | Quantizes column by column, adjusting remaining weights to cancel the error, using a small calibration set | 4-bit GPU inference |\n| AWQ | Finds the weights that matter most for activations and protects them with per-channel scaling | 4-bit GPU inference |\n| GGUF k-quants | Block-wise mixed precision formats in llama.cpp | CPU and laptop inference |\n| FP8 (E4M3) | 8-bit floating point, supported in hardware on H100 and newer | Fast serving, sometimes training |\n\n## Measure, don't assume\nPerplexity on general text often barely moves at 4 bits while specific skills drop: long multi-step math, code, low-resource languages and long-context recall tend to suffer first. Run your own eval set on the quantized model before switching, and compare at the task level.\n\n! Example: what 4-bit buys you\n! A 70B model needs about 140 GB in bf16 and about 40 GB at 4 bits with group scales.\n! That moves it from two 80 GB GPUs to one, and since decode is memory-bound, fewer bytes per weight also means faster tokens.\n\n> Takeaway: weight memory = parameters x bits / 8. Quantization shrinks it and speeds up memory-bound decode; outlier-aware methods like GPTQ and AWQ keep 4-bit models usable, but test the tasks you care about.", "s": "Weights = params x bytes: 70B x 2 bytes = 140 GB in fp16; 70B x 1 byte = 70 GB in int8; 70B x 0.5 byte = ~35 GB at 4-bit (a bit more for per-block scales). At 4-bit, rounding error and outliers can hurt quality, mostly on maths, code, long-context and rare languages, so you'd use outlier-aware methods (GPTQ, AWQ) and test on your own evals."}, {"id": "07.02", "t": "Sparse attention", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/sparse-attention", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Child et al.: Generating Long Sequences with Sparse Transformers (2019)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.10509", "Strided and fixed sparse patterns."], ["Beltagy et al.: Longformer (2020)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.05150", "Sliding window plus global tokens."], ["Yuan et al.: Native Sparse Attention (DeepSeek, 2025)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.11089", "Trainable, hardware-aligned sparse attention."]], "q": "With a sliding window of 4,096 tokens, can a token in layer 30 of a 32-layer model use information from 50,000 tokens back? Explain.", "n": ["Full attention scores every pair of tokens: O(n^2). Sparse attention scores only a chosen subset.", "Sliding window (local) attention: each token attends to the last W tokens. Cost O(n x W), and KV cache capped at W.", "Global tokens: a few tokens attend to and are attended by everything (Longformer).", "Strided / dilated patterns: attend to every k-th token to reach further at the same cost (Sparse Transformers).", "Stacking layers widens the receptive field: L layers of window W can pass information about L x W tokens.", "Recent work learns which blocks to attend to (DeepSeek NSA) or mixes local and full-attention layers (Gemma, Mistral)."], "ak": "", "a": "## Most pairs don't matter\nFull attention compares each token with every previous one. In practice, attention weights are concentrated: most of a token's weight goes to nearby tokens and a handful of distant ones. Sparse attention bets on this and skips the rest, cutting compute and memory from quadratic to closer to linear.\n\n## Common patterns\n%%layers Ways to choose which pairs to score.\n*Sliding window | each token sees the last W tokens (Mistral, Gemma local layers)\nDilated / strided | every k-th token further back (Sparse Transformers)\nGlobal tokens | a few tokens see and are seen by all (Longformer, BigBird)\nBlock / learned selection | pick the most relevant blocks per query (DeepSeek NSA)\n%%\n\n**Sliding window**\nEach token attends to its previous W tokens. Cost per token is constant, and the KV cache only needs the last W entries per layer, so memory stops growing with context. Mistral 7B used W = 4,096.\n\n**Strided and dilated**\nChild et al.'s Sparse Transformers combined a local pattern with a strided one (every k-th token), so that in two hops any token can reach any other, at O(n sqrt(n)) cost.\n\n**Global tokens**\nLongformer adds a few global tokens (like a [CLS] or the question tokens) that attend everywhere. Good for documents where some tokens need the whole picture.\n\n**Learned and block sparsity**\nDeepSeek's Native Sparse Attention (2025) compresses distant tokens into block summaries, selects the most relevant blocks for each query, and keeps a local window, all in a form that maps well onto GPU kernels and can be trained end to end.\n\n## Receptive field grows with depth\nInformation moves one window per layer. Token t in layer 1 sees t - W..t. Those tokens in layer 1 already saw W further back, so in layer 2 token t indirectly sees 2W back. After L layers the theoretical reach is about L x W.\n\n! Example: window 4,096, 32 layers\n! Reach at layer 30 is about 30 x 4,096 = 122,880 tokens, so 50,000 back is in range in principle.\n! But the information has to be relayed through about 13 hops, each compressing it, so precise recall of a detail that far back is weak.\n! That's why many models mix in some full-attention layers, for example Gemma's alternating local and global layers.\n\n## Trade-offs\n| Aspect | Full attention | Sliding window |\n| Compute per token | O(n) | O(W) |\n| KV cache | grows with n | fixed at W per layer |\n| Exact long-range recall | Strong | Weak beyond W |\n| Kernel support | Universal | Good (FlashAttention supports windows) |\n\n## Explore the patterns\nRows are queries, columns are keys. Pick a pattern and see which pairs are computed. Stack layers to see how far information can travel.\n\n%%widget sparse Sliding-window, dilated, global-token and block-sparse attention patterns.\n\nHow much each pattern saves for a sequence of n tokens:\n\n| Pattern | Pairs computed | Example |\n| Full causal | about n²/2 | GPT-style models |\n| Sliding window w | about n × w | Mistral 7B (w = 4,096) |\n| Window + global tokens | n × w + n × g | Longformer, BigBird |\n| Interleaved local and global layers | Mix per layer | Gemma 2 and 3 alternate local and global layers |\n\n## Receptive field, worked out\nWith a window of 4,096 and 32 layers, token information can travel 4,096 positions per layer, so in principle the top layer can see 32 × 4,096 = 131,072 tokens back. In practice information degrades with each hop, so models that need reliable long-range recall keep some full-attention layers. That is the reasoning behind interleaving: most layers cheap and local, a few layers global.\n\n## The serving side\nSliding windows bound the KV cache. A model with a 4,096 window only ever needs the last 4,096 tokens' keys and values per layer, so the cache becomes a fixed-size ring buffer however long the conversation grows. For chat applications with long sessions, that matters more than the FLOP savings.\n\n> Takeaway: sparse attention scores only some pairs: a local window, strides, global tokens or learned blocks. Depth stretches reach, but precise long-range recall usually needs some full-attention layers.", "s": "In principle yes: each layer passes information one window back, so after ~30 layers the receptive field is about 30 x 4,096 = ~123k tokens, which covers 50,000. But the information must be relayed indirectly through about 13 window hops, getting compressed at each, so precise recall from that far back is unreliable. Direct attention only reaches 4,096 tokens per layer; that's why models mix in full-attention layers."}, {"id": "07.03", "t": "Teaching a smaller model", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/slm-and-distillation", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Hinton et al.: Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network (2015)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.02531", "Soft targets and temperature."], ["Sanh et al.: DistilBERT (2019)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01108", "40% smaller, 60% faster, 97% of BERT's language understanding."], ["Hsieh et al.: Distilling Step-by-Step (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.02301", "Train small models on LLM rationales; beat a much larger LLM with less data."]], "q": "Why do soft labels from a teacher carry more information than hard labels, and what does temperature do in distillation?", "n": ["Small language models (1-8B parameters) are cheaper and faster to serve, can run on-device, and fine-tune easily.", "Distillation trains a small student to imitate a large teacher instead of learning only from raw data.", "Soft targets: the teacher's full probability distribution says which wrong answers are 'nearly right', which hard labels can't.", "Temperature > 1 softens the teacher's distribution so this information is visible; the loss is KL divergence between teacher and student.", "LLM-era distillation often means sequence-level: generate lots of teacher outputs (including reasoning) and fine-tune the student on them.", "Students rarely beat the teacher overall, but on a narrow task a distilled small model can match or beat a general large one."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why small models\nA smaller model is cheaper per token, has lower latency, fits on one GPU or even a phone, and is easier to fine-tune. For a narrow, high-volume task (classifying tickets, extracting fields, routing queries), a 1-8B model is often enough if it's trained well. Distillation is how you train it well.\n\n## The original idea\nHinton et al. (2015) observed that a trained model's output distribution contains more than its top answer. For an image of a 2, the teacher might give \"2: 0.9, 3: 0.05, 7: 0.04, cat: 0.0001\". The fact that 3 and 7 are plausible and \"cat\" is absurd is **dark knowledge** about how the classes relate. A hard label (\"2\") throws this away.\n\nSo train the student to match the teacher's whole distribution:\n\n```\np_teacher = softmax(z_teacher / T)\np_student = softmax(z_student / T)\nloss = KL(p_teacher || p_student) x T^2  +  alpha x CE(hard label, student)\n```\n\n**Temperature** T > 1 flattens both distributions, making small probabilities large enough to learn from. The T^2 factor keeps gradient sizes comparable.\n\n%%flow Classic logit distillation.\nInput -(teacher, frozen)-> Soft probabilities at T -(KL loss)-> *Student update\nInput -(student)-> Student probabilities at T -(KL loss)-> Student update\n%%\n\nDistilBERT applied this to language: 40% fewer parameters, 60% faster, keeping 97% of BERT's performance on the GLUE benchmark.\n\n## Distillation for LLMs\nWith generative models, two styles are common:\n- **Logit distillation**: match the teacher's next-token distribution at every position. Needs access to the teacher's logits and the same tokenizer.\n- **Sequence-level distillation**: have the teacher generate many responses, then fine-tune the student on them with ordinary supervised training. Works through an API, with any tokenizer.\n\nHsieh et al.'s \"Distilling Step-by-Step\" adds the teacher's reasoning (rationales) as an extra training target. Their 770M T5 model beat a 540B PaLM on some benchmarks using less training data than standard fine-tuning. Reasoning distillation is also how many small \"thinking\" models are made: a large reasoning model's chain-of-thought traces become the student's training data.\n\n## Limits\n- The student inherits the teacher's mistakes and biases.\n- Students lose breadth: a model distilled on support tickets is great at tickets and worse at everything else.\n- Check provider terms: some prohibit using outputs to train competing models.\n\n## See what soft labels carry\nA teacher's output distribution contains more than its top answer. Raise the distillation temperature below and see the teacher's view of which wrong answers are close.\n\n%%widget distill Teacher probabilities for one input at different distillation temperatures.\n\nHinton, Vinyals and Dean (2015) called this \"dark knowledge\". An image of a 2 that looks a bit like a 3 and a bit like a 7 produces tiny probabilities for 3 and 7, invisible at T = 1 but clear at T = 4. Training the student to match those softened probabilities teaches it how classes relate, which hard labels can't.\n\n## The loss\n```\nloss = alpha * T^2 * KL( softmax(teacher_logits / T) || softmax(student_logits / T) )\n     + (1 - alpha) * cross_entropy(student_logits, true_label)\n```\n\nThe T² factor keeps the gradient of the soft term at a similar scale as T changes. Common choices: T between 2 and 5, alpha around 0.5.\n\n## Distillation for LLMs, in three flavours\n| Flavour | Needs | Example |\n| Logit distillation | Teacher's full token distributions | DistilBERT: about 40% smaller, 60% faster, retaining about 97% of BERT's language understanding score |\n| Sequence-level (synthetic data) | Only teacher outputs as text | Fine-tune a small model on a large model's answers; works through APIs |\n| Reasoning traces | Teacher's step-by-step solutions | DeepSeek-R1 distilled reasoning into 1.5B to 70B Qwen and Llama models |\n\nThe synthetic-data flavour is what most teams do in practice, because API models don't return full distributions. Check the teacher provider's terms first; several forbid using outputs to train competing models.\n\n! Example: distilling a classifier from a large model\n! Label 20,000 support tickets with a frontier model, using a careful prompt and a second pass for disagreements. Cost: a few dollars to tens of dollars.\n! Fine-tune a small open model on those labels. Evaluate both on 500 human-labelled tickets.\n! If the student matches the teacher within a point or two, you serve the student at a fraction of the cost. If not, the gap tells you which categories need more examples.\n\n> Takeaway: distillation trains a small student on a large teacher's soft outputs or generated answers. Soft targets carry how-wrong-is-wrong information, and temperature makes it visible; for narrow tasks, the student can match the teacher at a fraction of the cost.", "s": "A hard label only says which answer is right; the teacher's full distribution also says how plausible each wrong answer is (which classes or tokens are similar), so each example gives the student much more signal ('dark knowledge'). Temperature T > 1 divides the logits before softmax, flattening the distribution so those small probabilities become large enough to learn from; the student is trained to match the teacher's softened distribution via KL divergence (scaled by T^2)."}, {"id": "07.04", "t": "Speculative decoding", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/speculative-decoding", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Leviathan et al.: Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.17192", "Draft-then-verify with the same output distribution."], ["Chen et al.: Accelerating LLM Decoding with Speculative Sampling (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.01318", "DeepMind's version, 2-2.5x on Chinchilla 70B."], ["Cai et al.: Medusa (2024)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.10774", "Extra decoding heads instead of a separate draft model."]], "q": "Why can a big model verify 5 drafted tokens in about the same time it takes to generate 1, and how does speculative decoding keep the output distribution unchanged?", "n": ["Decode is memory-bound: each step reads all the weights to produce one token, leaving compute idle.", "Speculative decoding: a small, fast draft model proposes k tokens; the big target model checks all k in one forward pass.", "Checking k tokens in parallel costs about the same as one decode step, because the weights are read once.", "Accept drafted tokens left to right while they pass; at the first rejection, sample a corrected token from the target and start again.", "The acceptance rule (accept with probability min(1, p_target / p_draft)) guarantees the output has exactly the target model's distribution.", "Speedup depends on acceptance rate; 2-3x is common. Variants: Medusa heads, EAGLE, n-gram / prompt lookup drafts."], "ak": "", "a": "## Idle compute in decode\nFrom lesson 01.03: decode generates one token per forward pass, and each pass reads every weight from GPU memory. The GPU's arithmetic units are mostly idle, waiting on memory. If we could get several useful tokens out of one pass, we would use that spare compute for free.\n\n## Draft, then verify\nLeviathan et al. (Google) and Chen et al. (DeepMind) proposed the same idea in 2022-2023:\n1. A small **draft** model (or cheap method) quickly generates k candidate tokens.\n2. The large **target** model runs one forward pass over the prompt plus all k drafts. Like prefill, this computes the target's distribution at every one of those positions in parallel.\n3. Compare left to right. Accept drafts while they pass; at the first rejection, replace that token with one sampled from the target and discard the rest.\n4. Repeat from the new position.\n\n%%seq One speculative step with k = 4.\nDraft model > Draft model: propose t1 t2 t3 t4\nDraft model > Target model: verify all four in one pass\nTarget model > Target model: accept t1, t2; reject t3\nTarget model -> Draft model: t1 t2 + corrected t3', continue\n%%\n\nEach round yields at least one token (the correction) and up to k + 1 when all drafts pass.\n\n## Why verification is cheap\nVerifying k tokens is a forward pass over k positions. Since decode is memory-bound, reading the weights once and doing k times the maths takes about as long as one normal step, for small k.\n\n## Same output, not an approximation\nFor greedy decoding, accept a draft if it equals the target's top token. For sampling, the papers use a rejection rule: accept draft token x with probability min(1, p_target(x) / p_draft(x)); if rejected, sample from the normalised difference max(0, p_target - p_draft). This guarantees the final tokens follow **exactly** the target model's distribution. Quality is unchanged; only speed changes.\n\n## What determines the speedup\n- **Acceptance rate**: how often the draft agrees with the target. Higher on predictable text (code, boilerplate, structured output).\n- **Draft cost**: the draft must be much faster than the target (often 10-50x smaller, same tokenizer).\n- **k**: more drafts help when acceptance is high, waste work when it's low.\nChen et al. reported 2-2.5x on Chinchilla 70B.\n\n## Variants\n| Method | Draft source |\n| Classic | Separate small model from the same family |\n| Medusa | Extra prediction heads on the target model itself |\n| EAGLE | Lightweight head predicting from the target's hidden states |\n| Prompt lookup / n-gram | Copy spans from the prompt (great for editing and RAG) |\n\n## Simulate a draft-and-verify round\nA small draft model proposes k tokens. The large model checks all k in one forward pass, accepts the longest correct prefix, and adds one token of its own. Change the acceptance rate and draft length to see the speedup.\n\n%%widget specdec Speculative decoding rounds, expected tokens per round and speedup.\n\nThe expected number of tokens per round with acceptance rate α and draft length k (Leviathan et al., 2023):\n\n```\nE[tokens] = (1 - α^(k+1)) / (1 - α)\n```\n\nAt α = 0.8 and k = 4 that is (1 − 0.8^5) / 0.2 = 3.36 tokens per target forward pass instead of 1. Subtract the cost of running the draft model k times and you get the real speedup, often 2 to 3× in practice.\n\n## Why the output is unchanged\nFor greedy decoding, the target accepts a draft token only if it is the token the target would have picked. For sampling, the rule accepts draft token x with probability min(1, p_target(x) / p_draft(x)), and on rejection samples from the normalised difference max(0, p_target − p_draft). This is a form of rejection sampling, and it provably produces exactly the target model's distribution. Speculative decoding is a speed technique, not a quality trade.\n\n## Where the speedup comes from, and where it doesn't\n- Decode is memory-bound: each step reads all the weights to produce one token. Checking k tokens in one pass reads the weights once, so the extra tokens are nearly free.\n- At large batch sizes the GPU is already busy, decode becomes more compute-bound, and the free capacity disappears. Speculative decoding helps most for low-latency, low-batch serving.\n- Acceptance is high on predictable text (code, boilerplate, quoting the input) and lower on creative text. Medusa, EAGLE and n-gram prompt lookup are variants that avoid running a separate draft model.\n\n> Takeaway: speculative decoding lets a cheap draft propose tokens and the big model verify them all in one memory-bound pass. A careful acceptance rule keeps the output distribution identical, so you get 2-3x speed for free in quality.", "s": "Decode is memory-bandwidth bound: one step reads all the weights to produce one token, so a forward pass over 5 positions reads the weights once and only adds cheap compute, taking about the same time. Drafts are accepted left to right with probability min(1, p_target/p_draft) (or exact match in greedy mode); at the first rejection a replacement is sampled from the residual distribution max(0, p_target - p_draft). This rejection-sampling rule makes the output exactly distributed as the target model's."}, {"id": "07.05", "t": "Match the optimization to the bottleneck", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/quantization-summary-pdf", "k": ["Exercise"], "x": [], "r": [["Horace He: Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr From First Principles", "https://horace.io/brrr_intro.html", "Compute, memory bandwidth and overhead as the three bottlenecks."], ["Databricks: LLM Inference Performance Engineering: Best Practices", "https://www.databricks.com/blog/llm-inference-performance-engineering-best-practices", "TTFT, TPOT, throughput and how batching and quantization move them."], ["NVIDIA: Mastering LLM Techniques: Inference Optimization", "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/mastering-llm-techniques-inference-optimization/", "Overview of batching, KV cache, parallelism and quantization."]], "q": "Your chatbot has slow first tokens on long documents, but streaming speed is fine. Which bottleneck is it, and which two optimizations would you try first?", "n": ["Name the symptom first: slow time to first token (TTFT), slow time per output token (TPOT), low throughput, or out-of-memory.", "Slow TTFT on long prompts: prefill is compute-bound. Try prompt/prefix caching, shorter context (better retrieval), FlashAttention, chunked prefill.", "Slow TPOT: decode is memory-bandwidth bound. Try weight quantization, speculative decoding, a smaller or distilled model, GQA models.", "Low throughput / cost per token: batch more. Paged attention, continuous batching, KV cache quantization, MoE trade-offs.", "Out of memory: quantize weights and KV cache, paging, tensor parallelism across GPUs.", "Measure before and after on your real traffic; an optimization for one bottleneck can hurt another (e.g. bigger batches raise TPOT)."], "ak": "", "a": "## Start from the symptom\nEvery optimization in modules 4 to 7 targets one bottleneck. Applying them at random wastes effort and sometimes makes things worse. Databricks' inference best-practices post uses these metrics:\n- **TTFT** (time to first token): how long before streaming starts. Mostly prefill.\n- **TPOT** (time per output token): streaming speed. Mostly decode.\n- **Throughput**: tokens per second across all users, which sets cost.\n- **Memory headroom**: whether the model and enough KV cache fit at all.\n\n## The map\n| Symptom | Bottleneck | Try first |\n| Slow TTFT on long prompts | Prefill compute (attention grows with n^2) | Prefix / prompt caching, send less context, FlashAttention, chunked prefill |\n| Slow TPOT | Decode memory bandwidth | Weight quantization, speculative decoding, smaller or distilled model |\n| Low throughput, high cost | Batch too small | Continuous batching, paged attention, KV cache quantization |\n| Out of memory | Weights + KV cache | Quantize weights and KV, GQA/MLA models, tensor parallelism |\n| Long context too slow at all | Quadratic attention | Sliding-window / sparse attention models, retrieval instead of stuffing |\n| Want big-model quality at small-model compute | Parameters vs FLOPs | Mixture-of-experts model |\n\n%%flow Diagnose before optimizing.\nMeasure TTFT, TPOT, throughput, memory -(find worst)-> *Name the bottleneck -(pick from map)-> One change -(re-measure)-> Keep or revert\n%%\n\n## Trade-offs to watch\n- **Bigger batches** raise throughput but raise each user's TPOT.\n- **Quantization** speeds decode and frees memory, but can cost accuracy on maths and code.\n- **Speculative decoding** helps most at small batch sizes; at very large batches the GPU is no longer idle and gains shrink.\n- **MoE** cuts compute per token but needs more memory and inter-GPU communication.\n- **Distillation** is the biggest win for a narrow task, and the most work.\n\n! Example: slow first token on long documents\n! Streaming is fine, so decode isn't the problem. The first token is slow only when documents are long: prefill bound.\n! Try first: (1) prefix caching, if many requests share the same document or system prompt; (2) send less: retrieve the relevant chunks instead of the whole document.\n! Then check the server uses FlashAttention and chunked prefill so one long prompt doesn't stall others.\n\n## Find the right fix from the symptom\nTick the symptoms you see in your deployment. The figure ranks optimizations by how many of your symptoms they address.\n\n%%widget diagnose From observed symptoms to the optimizations that address them.\n\n## Measure first: four numbers\nBefore choosing anything, measure these on real traffic:\n\n1. **Time to first token (TTFT)** at p50 and p99. Dominated by queueing and prefill.\n2. **Inter-token latency** (time per output token). Dominated by decode and batch size.\n3. **Throughput** in tokens per second per GPU at your latency target.\n4. **GPU memory headroom**: how much is weights, how much KV cache, how much free.\n\nThen match: long TTFT with long prompts points at prefill (prefix caching, FlashAttention, chunked prefill). Slow inter-token latency at small batch points at decode memory bandwidth (quantization, speculative decoding). Low throughput with memory full points at the KV cache (paging, GQA, FP8 KV, quantized weights to free space).\n\n! Example: two teams, same model, opposite fixes\n! Team A runs an internal coding assistant: 20 users, long prompts, short answers. Their TTFT is 4 s. Prefix caching of the repository context cuts it to 0.6 s. Speculative decoding would barely help.\n! Team B runs a public chatbot: 2,000 concurrent users, short prompts, long answers, GPUs at full memory. Paged attention plus FP8 KV doubles the batch size and halves cost per token. Prefix caching helps a little; speculative decoding hurts at their batch size.\n! Same model, same hardware. The workload picks the fix.\n\n> Takeaway: measure TTFT, TPOT, throughput and memory, name the bottleneck, then pick the optimization built for it. Prefill problems and decode problems have different fixes.", "s": "Slow first token but fine streaming means prefill is the bottleneck: processing the long prompt is compute-heavy (attention cost grows with length). First fixes: prefix/prompt caching so repeated documents or system prompts aren't re-prefilled, and cutting the context sent (retrieve relevant chunks instead of whole documents). Also ensure FlashAttention and chunked prefill. Quantization and speculative decoding mainly help decode, so they're not the first choice here."}]}, {"n": "08", "name": "Reasoning and verification", "lessons": [{"id": "08.01", "t": "Reasoning models with human feedback", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/reasoning-models-with-human-feedback", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Ouyang et al.: InstructGPT (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155", "The SFT, reward model, PPO pipeline."], ["Hugging Face: Illustrating RLHF", "https://huggingface.co/blog/rlhf", "Diagrams of each RLHF stage."], ["DeepSeek-AI: DeepSeek-R1 (2025)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948", "Reasoning learned through RL with verifiable rewards."], ["Lightman et al.: Let's Verify Step by Step (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.20050", "Process reward models that grade each reasoning step."]], "q": "Compare RLHF with a learned reward model to RL with verifiable rewards (as in DeepSeek-R1). What signal does each optimise, and what can go wrong?", "n": ["RLHF: collect human comparisons of responses, train a reward model on them, then optimise the LLM against the reward model (PPO), with a KL penalty to stay near the original.", "DPO does the same job directly from preference pairs, with no separate reward model or RL loop.", "Reward hacking: the policy finds outputs the reward model scores highly but humans don't actually prefer (long, flattering, confident answers).", "Reasoning models (o1, DeepSeek-R1) are trained with RL on tasks with checkable answers: maths results, passing unit tests. The reward is verifiable, not a learned opinion.", "DeepSeek-R1-Zero showed long chains of thought, self-checking and backtracking emerging from RL alone on a base model.", "Outcome reward models score the final answer; process reward models score each step, which Lightman et al. found gives better supervision for maths."], "ak": "", "a": "## From preferences to a reward\nA pretrained and fine-tuned model can answer, but \"good answer\" is hard to write down as a loss. RLHF turns human judgement into a training signal. The InstructGPT pipeline, drawn step by step in Hugging Face's RLHF explainer:\n\n%%flow The three stages of classic RLHF.\nBase model -(SFT on demonstrations)-> SFT model -(humans rank outputs)-> *Reward model -(PPO + KL penalty)-> Aligned model\n%%\n\n1. **SFT**: fine-tune on human-written ideal responses.\n2. **Reward model**: humans compare pairs of model outputs; a model learns to predict which one they prefer, outputting a scalar score.\n3. **RL**: optimise the LLM to maximise the reward model's score using PPO, with a KL penalty that keeps it close to the SFT model so it doesn't drift into nonsense that happens to score well.\n\n**DPO** (Direct Preference Optimization) reaches a similar result with a simple classification-style loss on the preference pairs, with no reward model and no RL loop, which is why it became popular for open models.\n\n## Reward hacking\nA learned reward model is only an approximation of human preference. Optimise hard against it and the policy finds its blind spots: longer answers, more confident tone, agreeing with the user. The KL penalty, reward model ensembles, and fresh human data limit this but never remove it.\n\n## Verifiable rewards and reasoning models\nFor maths and code there's a better signal: check the answer. DeepSeek-R1's paper describes training with RL where the reward is simply whether the final answer is correct (and in the right format), or whether code passes tests.\n\n| Aspect | RLHF with reward model | RL with verifiable rewards |\n| Signal | Learned model of human preference | Programmatic check (answer match, tests) |\n| Works for | Helpfulness, tone, safety, open-ended tasks | Maths, code, logic, anything checkable |\n| Main risk | Reward hacking the reward model | Gaming the checker; narrow skills |\n| What emerges | Polite, helpful style | Long reasoning, self-verification, backtracking |\n\nDeepSeek-R1-Zero, trained with RL only from a base model, learned on its own to write longer reasoning, re-check its work and say things like \"wait, let me reconsider\". The final R1 adds a small SFT warm-up for readability and a later preference stage for general helpfulness.\n\n## Outcome vs process rewards\nAn **outcome reward** only checks the final answer, so a lucky guess after bad reasoning gets full marks. A **process reward model** scores each step. Lightman et al.'s \"Let's Verify Step by Step\" trained one on 800k step-level human labels and found it selected correct maths solutions much more reliably than an outcome reward model.\n\n## How the reward model is trained\nLabelers see two answers to the same prompt and pick the better one. The reward model r(prompt, answer) is trained so the chosen answer scores higher, using the Bradley-Terry loss:\n\n```\nloss = -log sigmoid( r(prompt, chosen) - r(prompt, rejected) )\n```\n\nIf the reward model gives the chosen answer 2.0 and the rejected one 0.5, the difference is 1.5, sigmoid(1.5) = 0.82, and the loss is 0.20. If it had them backwards (0.5 versus 2.0), sigmoid(−1.5) = 0.18 and the loss is 1.71. Only differences matter, so the reward scale is arbitrary.\n\nThe policy is then trained with RL (usually PPO) to maximise:\n\n```\nreward(prompt, answer) - beta * KL( policy || reference model )\n```\n\nThe KL term is the leash. It keeps the policy near the supervised model it started from, so it can't drift into strange text that happens to score well.\n\n## Watch over-optimisation happen\nThe reward model is a learned approximation of human judgement, trained on limited data. Push the policy hard against it and the policy finds the reward model's blind spots. Gao, Schulman and Hilton (2022) measured this: as optimisation moves the policy further from the start (measured by KL), the proxy reward keeps climbing while the true \"gold\" reward rises, peaks and falls.\n\n%%widget rewardhack Proxy reward keeps rising while true quality peaks and falls as RL pushes harder.\n\nFamiliar real symptoms: answers get longer because length correlated with preference; models agree with the user's stated opinion (sycophancy) because labelers liked being agreed with; confident tone without more accuracy.\n\n## DPO: the same goal without a reward model\nDirect Preference Optimization (Rafailov et al., 2023) showed the RLHF objective can be optimised directly on preference pairs with a classification-style loss, skipping the separate reward model and the RL loop. It is simpler and more stable, and became a common choice for open models. It still learns from the same preference data, so it inherits the same biases.\n\n! Example: why verifiable rewards changed reasoning training\n! For a maths problem with a known answer, the reward is a rule: 1 if the final answer matches, 0 if not. There is nothing to hack except actually getting the answer right (or exploiting a bug in the checker).\n! DeepSeek-R1-Zero was trained this way with GRPO, and its AIME 2024 pass@1 rose from 15.6% to 71.0% over training, with long reasoning and self-checking emerging without being taught.\n\n> Takeaway: RLHF optimises a learned model of human preference and risks reward hacking. Reasoning models add RL on verifiable rewards, where checking the answer is the signal, and long careful reasoning emerges from it.", "s": "RLHF optimises a learned reward model trained on human preference comparisons (via PPO with a KL penalty, or directly via DPO); it handles open-ended qualities like helpfulness but can be reward-hacked (verbosity, sycophancy) because the reward model is only a proxy. RL with verifiable rewards optimises a programmatic check, such as the correct final maths answer or passing tests; the signal is objective and drove emergent long reasoning in R1, but it only covers checkable tasks and can be gamed if the checker is weak (e.g. right answer from wrong reasoning, which process rewards address)."}, {"id": "08.02", "t": "Chain of thought", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/chain-of-thought", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Wei et al.: Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.11903", "Few-shot examples with worked reasoning."], ["Kojima et al.: Large Language Models are Zero-Shot Reasoners (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11916", "'Let's think step by step' alone."], ["Wang et al.: Self-Consistency (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11171", "Sample many reasoning paths and take the majority answer."]], "q": "Why does writing out intermediate steps improve accuracy for a next-token predictor? Give a mechanical explanation, not 'it thinks harder'.", "n": ["Chain of thought (CoT): have the model write intermediate reasoning before the final answer.", "Few-shot CoT (Wei et al.): include worked examples with reasoning in the prompt. Zero-shot CoT (Kojima et al.): add 'Let's think step by step'.", "Mechanism: each generated token gets a full forward pass, so more tokens means more computation, and intermediate results are written to the context where later tokens can attend to them.", "Gains are large for multi-step maths and logic, small for simple lookup tasks, and appeared mainly in larger models.", "Self-consistency: sample several reasoning paths at temperature > 0 and take the majority answer. Often a big accuracy boost.", "Caveats: the written reasoning isn't always faithful to how the model reached its answer, and CoT costs tokens and latency. Reasoning models do CoT internally by default."], "ak": "", "a": "## The technique\nWei et al. (2022) showed that adding a few examples with worked reasoning to a prompt sharply improved large models on maths word problems. Kojima et al. found an even simpler trigger: append \"Let's think step by step.\" to the question.\n\n```\nQ: A cafe had 23 apples. They used 20 for lunch and bought 6 more. How many now?\nA: They started with 23. After lunch: 23 - 20 = 3. After buying: 3 + 6 = 9. The answer is 9.\n```\n\n## Why it works mechanically\nA transformer does a fixed amount of computation per generated token: one forward pass through all layers. If it must output the answer immediately, all the reasoning has to fit inside that one pass. Writing steps out helps in two ways:\n- **More computation**: every intermediate token is another full forward pass.\n- **External working memory**: intermediate results (\"3\") are written into the context, where later tokens can attend to them directly instead of recomputing.\n\n%%flow Direct answer vs chain of thought.\n[Direct] Question -(1 forward pass)-> Answer token\n[Chain of thought] Question -(pass)-> Step 1 -(pass, reads step 1)-> Step 2 -(pass, reads both)-> *Answer\n%%\n\nThat's also why CoT helps most on multi-step problems and barely at all on single-fact recall.\n\n## Self-consistency\nA single reasoning chain can go wrong at one step. Wang et al. sampled many chains at temperature > 0 and took a majority vote on the final answer. On GSM8K maths this added large gains over a single greedy chain. It's an early example of spending more inference compute to get better answers.\n\n## Where it falls short\n- **Cost**: reasoning tokens are output tokens; they add latency and price.\n- **Faithfulness**: research has found the written reasoning can be a rationalisation: models sometimes change the answer because of a hint in the prompt without mentioning the hint. Don't treat CoT as a reliable explanation.\n- **Errors compound**: one wrong step early poisons everything after it, which motivates verification (lesson 08.05) and tree search (08.04).\n\n## CoT in reasoning models\nModels like o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1 and Claude with extended thinking produce long chains of thought by default, trained with RL (lesson 08.01). For these, adding \"think step by step\" adds little; the useful controls are the reasoning budget and a clear statement of the goal and constraints.\n\n## Why writing steps helps, mechanically\nA transformer does a fixed amount of computation per token. A hard multi-step problem may need more computation than one forward pass provides. Writing intermediate steps gives the model more tokens, and so more passes, and each step's result becomes part of the context the next step can read. The reasoning text is working memory.\n\nMeasured effect, from Wei et al. (2022): PaLM 540B on the GSM8K grade-school maths benchmark went from 17.9% with standard prompting to 56.9% with eight chain-of-thought examples. The gain only appeared in large models; small models produced fluent but wrong chains.\n\nZero-shot chain of thought (Kojima et al., 2022) needs no examples, just \"Let's think step by step.\" It raised one model's accuracy on the MultiArith benchmark from 17.7% to 78.7%.\n\n## Self-consistency: vote over paths\nSample several reasoning chains at a temperature around 0.7, take each one's final answer, and return the most common one. On GSM8K, Wang et al. (2023) raised PaLM 540B from 56.5% (one greedy chain) to 74.4% with 40 samples.\n\nThe figure simulates voting. Set how often one sample is right and how scattered the wrong answers are.\n\n%%widget selfconsistency Majority-vote accuracy as you add samples.\n\nVoting works because correct reasoning paths tend to converge on the same answer, while errors scatter across many different wrong answers. When errors agree (a common misconception, an ambiguous question), voting can lock in the wrong answer. Try p = 30% with a single wrong option in the figure.\n\n## Practical notes\n- Ask for the answer in a fixed, parseable form at the end (\"Final answer: 42\"), so votes can be counted.\n- Voting needs answers that can be compared: numbers, labels, short strings. For open-ended writing, use a judge model to pick the best candidate instead.\n- Cost is linear in samples. Five to ten samples get most of the benefit on many tasks.\n- With reasoning models (o1, R1 and successors), the model already produces long internal chains. Adding \"think step by step\" helps less, and per-call reasoning budgets replace manual prompting.\n\n> Takeaway: chain of thought gives the model more forward passes and a scratchpad in its own context. It's strongest on multi-step problems, gets better with self-consistency voting, and isn't a faithful explanation.", "s": "A transformer does a fixed amount of computation per output token. Writing intermediate steps gives the model more forward passes (more total compute) before the answer, and stores partial results in the context where later tokens can attend to them instead of having to compute everything implicitly in one pass. So multi-step problems get decomposed into steps that each fit in one pass."}, {"id": "08.03", "t": "Tool usage in LLMs", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/tool-usage-in-llms", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Schick et al.: Toolformer (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761", "Models that teach themselves to call APIs."], ["Yao et al.: ReAct (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629", "Interleaving reasoning and actions."], ["Anthropic: Tool use with Claude", "https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/tool-use/overview", "How tool definitions, tool calls and tool results flow."]], "q": "Describe the message flow when a model answers 'What's the weather in Paris?' using a get_weather tool. Who executes the tool?", "n": ["Tool use lets a model call functions it can't do itself: search, calculators, databases, APIs, code execution.", "You describe each tool with a name, a description and a JSON schema for arguments.", "The model decides whether to call a tool and outputs a structured tool call; your code executes it and returns the result as a new message.", "The model then reads the result and answers, or calls another tool. That loop is the core of agents.", "ReAct interleaves reasoning ('I need the population first') with actions and observations; Toolformer trained models to insert API calls themselves.", "Design rules: clear names and descriptions, few overlapping tools, validated arguments, errors returned as informative text, and confirmation for actions with side effects."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why tools\nAn LLM can't look up today's weather, run code reliably, query your database or send an email. It can, however, decide that a tool is needed and write the arguments. Tool use connects that decision to real functions.\n\n## The message flow\n%%seq Tool calling: the model asks, your code acts.\nUser > App: What's the weather in Paris?\nApp > Model: user message + tool definitions\nModel -> App: tool_call get_weather(city=\"Paris\")\nApp > Weather API: execute\nWeather API -> App: 14 C, cloudy\nApp > Model: tool_result 14 C, cloudy\nModel -> User: It's 14 C and cloudy in Paris.\n%%\n\nThe model never executes anything. It produces a structured request; **your application** runs the function, then sends the result back as a tool-result message. Anthropic's tool use docs show exactly this exchange.\n\n## Defining a tool\n```\n{\n  \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n  \"description\": \"Current weather for a city. Use when the user asks about weather now.\",\n  \"input_schema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": { \"city\": {\"type\": \"string\"} },\n    \"required\": [\"city\"]\n  }\n}\n```\nThe description is a prompt. It's how the model decides when to use the tool and how to fill the arguments, so write it for a reader who knows nothing about your system.\n\n## Where the idea came from\n- **ReAct** (Yao et al., 2022): prompt the model to alternate Thought, Action, Observation. Reasoning picks the next action; observations ground the reasoning in real data. It reduced hallucination on question answering compared with reasoning alone.\n- **Toolformer** (Schick et al., 2023): a model learned on its own where API calls (calculator, search, translation) would help it predict text, by keeping only the calls that lowered its loss.\n- Today, models are trained directly on function-calling formats, and APIs expose tool calls as structured output.\n\n## Designing good tools\n- Use clear, distinct names and descriptions; overlapping tools confuse selection.\n- Keep argument schemas small and validate them before execution.\n- Return errors as useful text (\"city not found, try a country code\") so the model can recover.\n- Return only what's needed; huge tool outputs fill the context window.\n- Require confirmation for tools with side effects (payments, deletes, sending messages).\n- Treat tool outputs as untrusted: a web page fetched by a tool can contain prompt injection.\n\n## Step through a tool call\nThe model never executes a tool. It emits a structured request, your code decides whether to run it, and the result goes back into the conversation. Pick a scenario and step through the messages.\n\n%%widget toolcall The messages exchanged in one tool call, step by step.\n\n## A tool definition that works\n```\n{\n  \"name\": \"search_orders\",\n  \"description\": \"Find a customer's orders. Use when the user asks about order status, delivery or returns. Returns at most 20 orders, newest first.\",\n  \"input_schema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"customer_id\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"Internal id, for example C-10293. Not the email.\"},\n      \"status\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"any\", \"open\", \"shipped\", \"returned\"], \"default\": \"any\"},\n      \"since\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"ISO date, for example 2026-01-01\"}\n    },\n    \"required\": [\"customer_id\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhat makes it good: the description says when to use it and what comes back; enums replace free text where the set is fixed; the id format is spelled out so the model doesn't pass an email; results are bounded.\n\n## Tool results the model can use\n- **Return only what is needed.** A 40 KB JSON blob burns context and buries the answer. Return the five fields the model needs, not the whole database row.\n- **Make errors actionable.** \"customer_id C-1029 not found; ids have 5 digits after C-\" lets the model fix its call. A stack trace doesn't.\n- **Use stable, human-readable ids** where the model will refer back to them.\n\n## Safety is your code's job\nTreat every tool argument as untrusted input: validate types, enforce limits, and check permissions as the end user, not as an all-powerful service account. Separate read tools from write tools, and require confirmation for anything that sends, deletes, pays or publishes. Text that comes back from a tool (a web page, an email) can contain instructions aimed at the model; that is prompt injection, and the defence is to limit what the model is allowed to do next, not to hope it ignores them.\n\n> Takeaway: the model chooses a tool and writes arguments; your code executes and returns the result; the model continues. Tool descriptions are prompts, and side-effecting tools need guardrails.", "s": "The app sends the user message plus the get_weather tool definition (name, description, JSON schema) to the model. The model returns a structured tool call get_weather(city='Paris') instead of text. The application (not the model) executes the function/API, then sends the result back as a tool-result message. The model reads it and writes the final answer, or calls another tool."}, {"id": "08.04", "t": "Tree of thought", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/tree-of-thought", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Yao et al.: Tree of Thoughts (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601", "Search over reasoning steps with self-evaluation."], ["Snell et al.: Scaling LLM Test-Time Compute Optimally (2024)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.03314", "When more inference compute beats a bigger model."], ["Besta et al.: Graph of Thoughts (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.09687", "Generalising chains and trees to graphs of thoughts."]], "q": "Tree of Thoughts solved 74% of Game of 24 puzzles vs 4% for chain of thought with GPT-4. What does ToT do differently that explains the gap?", "n": ["Chain of thought commits to one path; an early mistake can't be undone.", "Tree of Thoughts (ToT) treats reasoning as search: generate several candidate next steps ('thoughts'), evaluate them, expand the promising ones, and backtrack from dead ends.", "Three pieces: a thought generator, a state evaluator (the LLM rates states as sure / maybe / impossible), and a search algorithm (BFS or DFS).", "On Game of 24, GPT-4 with ToT solved 74% vs 4% with chain-of-thought prompting.", "Costs many more model calls; best for problems with clear intermediate states and checkable progress.", "Same theme as test-time compute scaling: search, voting and verification at inference can substitute for a bigger model on hard problems."], "ak": "", "a": "## Beyond one chain\nA chain of thought is a single path. If step 2 is wrong, steps 3 onward build on the error, and the model rarely goes back. Humans solving puzzles try an approach, notice it's going nowhere, and back up. Tree of Thoughts (Yao et al., 2023) gives the model the same ability.\n\n## How ToT works\nThe problem is broken into steps. Each partial solution is a node in a tree.\n\n%%flow Tree of Thoughts: expand, evaluate, keep the best, repeat.\nProblem -(generate k thoughts)-> Candidate states -(LLM rates: sure / maybe / impossible)-> *Keep best b -(expand again or backtrack)-> Solution\n%%\n\n1. **Thought generator**: ask the model for several possible next steps from the current state.\n2. **State evaluator**: ask the model to judge each state, for example \"can 10, 13, 13 still reach 24? sure / maybe / impossible\", or to vote between states.\n3. **Search**: breadth-first search keeping the best b states per level, or depth-first with backtracking when a state is judged impossible.\n\nThe model plays both roles, proposer and critic, orchestrated by ordinary code.\n\n## The results\n| Task (GPT-4) | Chain of thought | Tree of Thoughts |\n| Game of 24 | 4% | 74% (b = 5) |\n| Creative writing coherence (human preference) | Lower | Preferred |\n| Mini crosswords (words correct) | ~16% | ~60% |\n\nGame of 24 (combine four numbers with + - x / to make 24) has clear intermediate states and dead ends that are easy to judge, which is exactly where search shines. Chain of thought fails because a bad first operation leaves an unsolvable state with no way back.\n\n## The price\nToT uses many more model calls per problem: every generated and evaluated thought is a call. It's worth it for hard problems where progress can be checked, not for everyday questions.\n\n## Test-time compute\nToT is one instance of a broader idea: spend more compute at inference to get better answers. Options include self-consistency voting (08.02), best-of-n with a verifier, tree or beam search with a process reward model, and the long internal reasoning of o1/R1-style models. Snell et al. (2024) found that, for some problems, optimally allocated test-time compute let a small model beat one 14x larger. Graph of Thoughts extends trees to graphs where partial solutions can be merged.\n\n## Run the search yourself\nThe figure is a working breadth-first Tree of Thoughts on the Game of 24. Each level combines two of the remaining numbers with + − × ÷. An evaluator rates every new state as possible or impossible, and the best b states are kept. The evaluator here is an exact solver with an adjustable error rate, standing in for an LLM's \"sure / maybe / impossible\" judgement.\n\n%%widget tot Breadth-first Tree of Thoughts on Game of 24 with a noisy evaluator.\n\nThings to try:\n\n- Default numbers 4, 9, 10, 13, breadth 5, error 15%: usually solved, for example (13 − 9) × (10 − 4) = 24.\n- Breadth 1 with 20% error: close to a single chain of thought. One misjudged state and the search fails.\n- Raise breadth to 10: almost always solved even with a noisy evaluator, at roughly twice the calls.\n- Error 0%: the evaluator is perfect, so breadth 1 is enough. A good evaluator is worth more than a wide search.\n\n## What the counts mean\nThe first level alone generates up to 36 candidate states from four numbers (6 pairs × up to 6 operations, minus duplicates). Every candidate needs a model call to propose and another to evaluate in the original method. Yao et al. reported ToT on Game of 24 using far more tokens per problem than chain of thought: success went from 4% to 74%, and cost went up by an order of magnitude or more. That trade only makes sense when a wrong answer is expensive and states can be judged.\n\n## The general pattern\nTree of Thoughts has three swappable parts, and you can build your own versions:\n\n| Part | Simple version | Stronger version |\n| Proposer | Ask the model for k next steps | Sample at higher temperature for diversity |\n| Evaluator | Ask the model to rate a state | A trained verifier, unit tests, a solver, or a rule |\n| Search | BFS keeping b states | Beam search, best-first, or Monte Carlo tree search |\n\nIn code generation, the evaluator can be the test suite: generate several patches, run the tests, keep the ones that pass, and refine. That is ToT with a perfect evaluator, and it is one of the most reliable patterns in practice.\n\n> Takeaway: Tree of Thoughts turns reasoning into search: propose several next steps, have the model judge them, keep the best and backtrack from dead ends. It costs more calls and pays off on problems with checkable intermediate states.", "s": "Chain of thought follows one path and can't recover if an early arithmetic choice leaves an unsolvable state. ToT generates several candidate next steps at each stage, has the model evaluate each partial state (sure/maybe/impossible to reach 24), keeps the best few (BFS with breadth 5) and prunes or backtracks from dead ends. Exploring and evaluating alternatives is what lifts success from 4% to 74%, at the cost of many more model calls."}, {"id": "08.05", "t": "Diagnose four reasoning failures", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/reasoning-models-summary", "k": ["Exercise"], "x": [], "r": [["Lightman et al.: Let's Verify Step by Step (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.20050", "Step-level verification of reasoning."], ["Huang et al.: Large Language Models Cannot Self-Correct Reasoning Yet (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01798", "Self-correction without external feedback often hurts."], ["Anthropic: Reasoning models don't always say what they think", "https://www.anthropic.com/research/reasoning-models-dont-say-think", "Measuring chain-of-thought faithfulness."]], "q": "A reasoning model gets a maths answer wrong, then when asked 'are you sure?' changes a correct answer to a wrong one on another problem. Which failure is the second, and what's a better way to verify?", "n": ["Failure 1, wrong step early: one arithmetic or logic slip propagates. Fix: step-level verification, tools (calculator, code), self-consistency.", "Failure 2, unfaithful reasoning: the written steps don't reflect what drove the answer (e.g. a hint in the prompt). Fix: don't rely on CoT as an explanation; check outputs independently.", "Failure 3, self-correction that makes things worse: asked to 'check your answer' without new information, models often flip correct answers. Fix: verify with external signals (tests, tools, a separate verifier).", "Failure 4, overthinking or underthinking: too few tokens on hard problems, endless loops or second-guessing on easy ones. Fix: set reasoning budgets per task, cap tokens, detect loops.", "Verification is easier than generation for many tasks: check with unit tests, a solver, a calculator, or a trained verifier.", "Always look at full traces for failed cases before changing prompts or models."], "ak": "", "a": "## How to use this lesson\nReasoning models fail in a few recognisable ways. When an output is wrong, read the whole trace and decide which of these four it is; each has a different fix.\n\n## 1. An early wrong step\nThe model makes one arithmetic or logic error, then reasons carefully and confidently from the wrong value.\n- **Signs**: the trace is coherent except for one step; the final answer is consistent with the mistake.\n- **Fixes**: give it a calculator or code execution for arithmetic; sample several chains and vote (self-consistency); score steps with a verifier. Lightman et al. found step-level (process) supervision catches these much better than checking only the final answer.\n\n## 2. Unfaithful reasoning\nThe visible reasoning doesn't reflect what actually determined the answer. Anthropic's study inserted hints into prompts; models often used the hint to change their answer but mentioned it in their reasoning only a minority of the time.\n- **Signs**: the answer changes with irrelevant prompt details while the reasoning text doesn't mention them.\n- **Fixes**: don't treat the chain of thought as an audit log. Evaluate outputs directly, and test with perturbed prompts.\n\n## 3. Self-correction that backfires\nAsk \"are you sure? check again\" with no new information and models often change correct answers to wrong ones. Huang et al. found that intrinsic self-correction, without external feedback, frequently lowered accuracy on reasoning benchmarks.\n- **Signs**: accuracy drops after a \"review your answer\" step; answers flip under mild pushback.\n- **Fixes**: verify with an **external signal**: run the code, check against a solver, use a separate verifier model, or retrieve a source. Only revise when the check fails.\n\n## 4. Thinking too little or too much\nOn hard problems, a small reasoning budget cuts the chain before it reaches the answer. On easy problems, long budgets can lead to loops, repeated second-guessing and higher cost with no gain.\n- **Signs**: truncated traces at max tokens; traces that circle (\"wait... actually... wait\").\n- **Fixes**: set reasoning effort per task type, cap tokens, route easy queries to a cheaper non-reasoning model, detect repetition.\n\n## The diagnosis table\n| Failure | Typical sign | First fix |\n| Early wrong step | One bad step, rest consistent | Tools for arithmetic, voting, step verifier |\n| Unfaithful reasoning | Answer moves with hidden cues | Judge outputs, not explanations |\n| Self-correction backfire | Correct answers flip on \"are you sure?\" | External verification only |\n| Wrong reasoning budget | Truncation or loops | Per-task budgets, routing |\n\n%%flow A safer verification loop.\nModel answer -(external check: tests, solver, tool)-> *Pass? -(yes)-> Return\nModel answer -(check fails)-> Feedback with the error -(revise)-> New answer\n%%\n\n## Verifiers set the ceiling\nMany fixes in this lesson come down to \"generate several candidates and check them\". How much that helps depends on the checker. The figure computes exactly what best-of-n gives you for a given generator and verifier.\n\n%%widget verify Accuracy of best-of-n selection with an imperfect verifier.\n\nRead the chart from both ends:\n\n- The dashed line (pass@n) is what a perfect verifier would get: the chance that at least one candidate is right.\n- Your verifier's line sits below it. With a high false-accept rate it flattens quickly: more samples just give the verifier more wrong answers to accept.\n\nThat is why research on process reward models (Lightman et al., 2023, \"Let's Verify Step by Step\") matters: a verifier that checks each step catches errors an outcome check misses. Their process-supervised reward model, used to pick among many solutions, solved 78% of a representative subset of the MATH test set, ahead of outcome supervision.\n\n## A worked diagnosis\n! Example: a support agent that refunds the wrong amount\n! Symptom: on 6% of refund tickets the agent refunds the full order instead of one item.\n! Read the traces. In every failure the agent's first step reads \"the customer wants a refund for order 5521\" and never mentions the item. The rest of the chain is consistent with that wrong first step. This is failure 1, an early wrong step.\n! Fix options, in order of cost: (1) make the plan explicit, asking the agent to write \"items to refund:\" before calling any tool; (2) add a check before the refund tool runs that the amount matches the listed items; (3) sample two plans and escalate to a human if they disagree.\n! Option 2 alone took failures to near zero, because the check is a rule, not another model call.\n\n## Two research results to keep in mind\n- **Reasoning can be unfaithful.** Turpin et al. (2023) biased few-shot prompts so the answer was always \"(A)\". Models followed the bias and wrote plausible explanations that never mentioned it. A sensible-looking chain is not proof of why the model answered.\n- **Self-correction without new information often fails.** Huang et al. (2023) found that asking a model to review and fix its own reasoning, with no external feedback, often made results worse: it changed right answers to wrong ones about as often as the reverse. Self-review works when it brings in new signal: a test result, a tool output, a retrieved document.\n\n> Takeaway: classify the failure before fixing it. Most reasoning fixes come from external checks (tools, tests, verifiers), not from asking the model to try harder.", "s": "The second case is self-correction backfiring: asked to re-check without any new information, the model flips a correct answer because pushback alone shifts its output (intrinsic self-correction often lowers accuracy). Better: verify with an external signal, e.g. recompute with a calculator or code, check against a solver or tests, or use a separate verifier/process reward model, and only revise when that check fails, passing the concrete error back."}]}, {"n": "09", "name": "MCP and agents", "lessons": [{"id": "09.01", "t": "Context engineering", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/context-engineering", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Anthropic: Effective context engineering for AI agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents", "Context as a finite resource; compaction, notes, sub-agents."], ["Chroma: Context Rot", "https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot", "Measured degradation as input length grows."], ["Liu et al.: Lost in the Middle (2023)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172", "Position of information in long contexts matters."]], "q": "An agent's quality drops after 40 tool calls in a long session. Name three context engineering techniques you'd apply and what each fixes.", "n": ["Context engineering is choosing everything that goes into the context window on each call: instructions, examples, retrieved data, tool definitions and results, conversation history.", "Context is a limited resource: longer contexts cost more, are slower, and quality degrades as length grows ('context rot').", "Aim for the smallest set of high-signal tokens that gets the job done.", "Techniques: clear and well-structured system prompts, few canonical examples, minimal non-overlapping tools, retrieval just in time instead of everything up front.", "For long-running agents: compaction (summarise old history), structured notes stored outside the context, and sub-agents that return condensed results.", "Trim tool outputs, put key information at the start or end, and remove stale results."], "ak": "", "a": "## From prompts to context\nPrompt engineering focused on wording one instruction. Agents changed the problem: a model now runs for many turns, calling tools, and each call's context is a mix of system prompt, tool definitions, history, tool results and retrieved documents. Anthropic's post on context engineering defines the job as curating the optimal set of tokens for each inference, and treats context as a finite resource with diminishing returns.\n\n## Why more context isn't better\n- **Cost and latency**: every input token is prefilled and billed, on every call.\n- **Context rot**: Chroma's study tested many models and found performance degrading as input length grew, even on simple tasks, well before the context limit.\n- **Position effects**: \"Lost in the Middle\" showed information in the middle of a long context is used less reliably.\n- **Attention budget**: the model has to spread attention across everything; irrelevant tokens dilute what matters.\n\n## What goes into a call\n%%layers The parts of an agent's context, roughly in order.\nSystem prompt | role, goals, constraints, at the right altitude: specific but not brittle\nTool definitions | few, distinct, well-described\nExamples | a handful of canonical cases, not every edge case\n*Retrieved / just-in-time data | fetched when needed, by path or query\nHistory and tool results | trimmed, summarised, stale results removed\n%%\n\n## Techniques\n**Write a clear system prompt.** Anthropic suggests the right altitude: neither hard-coded if-else logic nor vague guidance. Use sections (background, instructions, tool guidance, output format).\n\n**Load just in time.** Instead of pasting every document, give the agent identifiers (file paths, URLs, queries) and tools to fetch what it needs, the way a person keeps references rather than memorising everything.\n\n**Compaction.** When the context nears its limit, summarise the conversation so far, keep decisions, open issues and key details, drop raw tool output, and continue in a fresh context with the summary.\n\n**Structured notes.** The agent writes progress to a file or memory store (a to-do list, a NOTES.md) and reads it back later, so state survives compaction.\n\n**Sub-agents.** Give a focused sub-task to a separate agent with its own clean context; it returns a short summary instead of thousands of tokens of exploration.\n\n! Example: quality drops after 40 tool calls\n! Clear old tool results: raw outputs from 30 calls ago are noise. Fixes context rot and cost.\n! Compact: summarise the session into decisions, current state and next steps, then continue. Fixes the history filling the window.\n! Externalise state: keep a task list in a notes file the agent updates. Fixes losing track of goals after compaction.\n\n## Build a context budget\nEvery call sends the full context: system prompt, tool definitions, history, retrieved documents and room for the answer. In an agent, that is resent on every step. Set your own numbers below.\n\n%%widget context What fills the context window, and what it costs per call and per agent run.\n\nThings that usually surprise people:\n\n- **Tool schemas add up.** 50 tools at a few hundred tokens each is over 15,000 tokens before the user has said anything.\n- **History grows without limit.** A 100-turn conversation can be the largest part of every call.\n- **Agent runs multiply everything.** A 20-step run with 60k tokens of context sends 1.2 million input tokens. Prompt caching (04.04) is what makes this affordable.\n\n## Why more context can hurt\nLiu et al. (2023, \"Lost in the Middle\") placed the one relevant document among up to 30 retrieved documents and moved it around. Accuracy was highest when it was first or last and dropped markedly when it was in the middle, in some settings below the model's accuracy with no documents at all. Newer models are better at long contexts, but the rule still holds: irrelevant text is not free. It costs money and dilutes attention.\n\n## Techniques in more detail\n| Technique | How | When |\n| Compaction | Summarise old turns into a short note, keep recent turns verbatim | Long chats and long agent runs |\n| Structured notes | The agent writes progress to a file or memory tool and reads it back | Multi-hour tasks, work that spans sessions |\n| Just-in-time retrieval | Keep ids or paths in context, fetch content only when needed | Large codebases, document sets |\n| Tool search | Load tool definitions on demand instead of all up front | Dozens of tools or more |\n| Sub-agents | A helper explores with its own context and returns a short summary | Broad searches that would flood the main context |\n\n! Example: trimming a coding agent's context\n! Before: system prompt 3k, 40 tools 14k, full file contents of 25 files 90k, history 30k. 137k tokens per step, slow and expensive, and the agent often edits the wrong file.\n! After: tools loaded on demand (3k typical), file paths and a grep tool instead of full contents (8k), history compacted every 20 turns (10k). About 24k tokens per step.\n! Cost per step drops by over 80%, and edits land in the right file more often because the relevant code is no longer buried.\n\n> Takeaway: context engineering is curating the smallest set of high-signal tokens for each call. Load data just in time, trim and compact history, keep state in notes, and push exploration into sub-agents.", "s": "1) Clear/trim old tool results: raw outputs from many calls ago add noise and cost, causing context rot. 2) Compaction: summarise history into decisions, current state and next steps and continue in a fresh context, so the window isn't dominated by stale turns. 3) Structured external notes (task list/progress file) the agent reads and updates, so goals and state survive compaction. Also: sub-agents for exploratory sub-tasks that return short summaries, and just-in-time retrieval instead of loading everything."}, {"id": "09.02", "t": "The agent loop", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/ai-agents", "k": [], "x": [], "r": [["Anthropic: Building effective agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents", "Workflows vs agents, and five composable patterns."], ["Lilian Weng: LLM Powered Autonomous Agents", "https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-06-23-agent/", "Planning, memory and tool use."], ["Yao et al.: ReAct (2022)", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629", "Reason, act, observe as a loop."]], "q": "When should you build a fixed workflow instead of an autonomous agent? Give two criteria and an example of each.", "n": ["An agent is an LLM using tools in a loop: decide, act, observe the result, repeat until done.", "Anthropic distinguishes workflows (LLM calls in a path fixed by code) from agents (the model directs its own steps and tool use).", "Workflow patterns: prompt chaining, routing, parallelisation, orchestrator-workers, evaluator-optimizer.", "Use workflows when the steps are known in advance; agents when the number and order of steps can't be predicted.", "Agents need stopping conditions (task done, max steps, budget), error recovery, and checkpoints for human review on risky actions.", "Start with the simplest thing that works; add autonomy only when it measurably helps, because agents cost more and their errors compound."], "ak": "", "a": "## What an agent is\nLilian Weng's overview describes an agent as an LLM brain plus planning, memory and tool use. Anthropic's \"Building effective agents\" gives the simplest working definition: an LLM using tools in a loop, deciding its own next step based on feedback from the environment.\n\n%%flow The agent loop.\nGoal -(model decides)-> *Tool call -(execute)-> Observation -(append to context)-> Model decides again -(until done or limit)-> Final answer\n%%\n\nEach turn the model sees the goal, the history and the latest tool results, then either calls another tool or finishes. ReAct (lesson 08.03) is the same loop with explicit reasoning between actions.\n\n## Workflows vs agents\nAnthropic splits agentic systems into two kinds:\n- **Workflows**: LLM calls and tools connected by a path you write in code.\n- **Agents**: the model decides the path itself, dynamically.\n\nCommon workflow patterns:\n| Pattern | What it does | Example |\n| Prompt chaining | Step 1's output feeds step 2, with checks between | Outline, check outline, write draft |\n| Routing | Classify input, send to a specialised prompt or model | Refund vs technical vs sales queries |\n| Parallelisation | Run independent subtasks at once, or vote | Several guardrail checks in parallel |\n| Orchestrator-workers | One LLM splits work and delegates dynamically | Multi-file code change |\n| Evaluator-optimizer | One LLM drafts, another critiques, repeat | Translation with review |\n\n## When to use which\n- **Steps known in advance** -> workflow. Generating a weekly report from fixed sources is always fetch, summarise, format.\n- **Steps unknown, depend on what's found** -> agent. Fixing a bug in an unfamiliar codebase: you don't know which files to read until you've read some.\n- **Errors are cheap to detect** (tests, compilers) -> agents work well, because the loop can self-correct from real feedback.\n- **Errors are costly or silent** -> keep humans in the loop or stay with workflows.\n\n## Making the loop safe\n- **Stopping conditions**: task complete, max iterations, token or cost budget.\n- **Ground truth each step**: tool results, test outputs, not the model's own claims.\n- **Checkpoints**: pause for human approval before irreversible actions.\n- **Sandboxing**: run code and file operations in an isolated environment.\n- **Good tools**: Anthropic stresses designing the agent-computer interface as carefully as a human UI: clear tool docs, examples, formats that are hard to misuse.\n\n## Why simple first\nAgents trade cost and latency for flexibility, and small error rates compound over many steps: 95% per-step reliability over 20 steps is about 36% end to end. Start with a single call plus retrieval, move to a workflow, and only then to an agent when the task really needs it.\n\n## Why long tasks fail\nIf each step of an agent succeeds with probability p, a task of n independent steps succeeds with p^n. At 95% per step, 20 steps succeed 36% of the time. The figure adds the two things that change this: detecting errors and retrying.\n\n%%widget reliability Task success versus number of steps, with and without error detection and retries.\n\nThe lesson is about detection, not retries. A retry only helps if the loop notices the step failed. Undetected errors (a wrong file edited, a plausible but wrong number) can't be retried, so they compound. Set detection to 0% and retries do nothing.\n\nWays to detect errors in an agent loop:\n\n- **Check tool results**: non-zero exit codes, empty search results, HTTP errors. Feed them back to the model as plain text.\n- **Run tests or validators** after every code change, not just at the end.\n- **Assert invariants** in code: the refund is not larger than the order, the file still parses.\n- **Ask for evidence**: before finishing, the agent must cite the test output or record that shows the task is done.\n\n## The loop in code\n```\nmessages = [system, user_task]\nfor step in range(MAX_STEPS):                  # hard cap on steps\n    reply = model(messages, tools=TOOLS)\n    if reply.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":\n        return reply.text                       # finished\n    for call in reply.tool_calls:\n        if needs_approval(call): wait_for_human(call)\n        result = run_tool(call)                 # validated, sandboxed, time-limited\n        messages += [reply, tool_result(call.id, result)]\n    if spent_tokens() > BUDGET: return escalate(\"budget exceeded\")\nreturn escalate(\"step limit reached\")\n```\n\nEvery production loop has the same guards: a step cap, a token or cost budget, approval for risky actions, and a way to hand off to a human.\n\n## Workflow first\nAnthropic's guide \"Building effective agents\" (December 2024) separates workflows, where code fixes the sequence of LLM calls (prompt chaining, routing, parallel calls, orchestrator-workers, evaluator-optimizer), from agents, where the model chooses its own steps. Start with the simplest version that meets the eval. A three-step chain that always runs in the same order is easier to test, cheaper and more predictable than an agent. Use an agent when the number and order of steps truly can't be known in advance.\n\n> Takeaway: an agent is a model calling tools in a loop until it decides it's done. Prefer fixed workflows when the steps are known; use agents when they aren't, with limits, real feedback and checkpoints.", "s": "Use a workflow when the steps are known and fixed in advance (e.g. a weekly report: fetch metrics, summarise, format), and when errors are costly or hard to detect, so predictability matters. Use an agent when the number and order of steps depend on what's discovered (e.g. debugging an unfamiliar codebase) and when results can be checked by real feedback such as tests, so the loop can self-correct."}, {"id": "09.03", "t": "Model Context Protocol", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/model-context-protocol", "k": [], "x": ["MCP"], "r": [["Model Context Protocol: Introduction", "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/introduction", "Official overview: hosts, clients, servers."], ["MCP specification", "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18", "Protocol details: JSON-RPC, primitives, transports, authorization."], ["Anthropic: Introducing the Model Context Protocol", "https://www.anthropic.com/news/model-context-protocol", "Why it was created (November 2024)."]], "q": "Explain the M x N integration problem MCP solves, and name MCP's three server-side primitives with an example of each.", "n": ["MCP is an open protocol for connecting AI applications to tools and data, introduced by Anthropic in November 2024.", "Without it, every app needs a custom integration for every data source: M apps x N tools. With MCP it becomes M + N.", "Architecture: a host (the AI app) runs one MCP client per server; servers expose capabilities over JSON-RPC.", "Server primitives: tools (actions the model can call), resources (data the app can read into context), prompts (reusable templates the user can pick).", "Transports: stdio for local servers, streamable HTTP for remote ones; remote servers use OAuth for authorization.", "Security: tool descriptions and outputs are untrusted input (prompt injection, tool poisoning); keep humans in the loop for sensitive actions and grant least privilege."], "ak": "", "a": "## The integration problem\nEvery AI application wants access to the same things: files, GitHub, Slack, databases, calendars. Before MCP, each app wrote its own connector for each service. With M apps and N services that's M x N integrations, each with its own quirks. Anthropic introduced the Model Context Protocol in November 2024 as an open standard so that a service is wrapped once as an MCP server and works with every MCP-capable app: M + N.\n\n## Architecture\n%%flow One host, several servers, one client per server.\n*Host (AI app) -(MCP client)-> GitHub server\nHost (AI app) -(MCP client)-> Database server\nHost (AI app) -(MCP client)-> Local files server\n%%\n\n- **Host**: the AI application the user interacts with (a chat app, an IDE, an agent).\n- **Client**: a connection inside the host, one per server.\n- **Server**: a program that exposes capabilities, local or remote.\n\nMessages are JSON-RPC 2.0. Local servers usually run as a subprocess over **stdio**; remote servers use **streamable HTTP**, with OAuth for authorization.\n\n## What servers expose\n| Primitive | Controlled by | What it is | Example |\n| Tools | Model | Functions the model can call | create_issue, run_query |\n| Resources | Application | Data the app can read into context | a file, a table schema |\n| Prompts | User | Reusable templates the user selects | \"review this PR\" |\n\nClients can also offer capabilities to servers, for example **sampling** (the server asks the host's model to generate text) and **elicitation** (the server asks the user for input).\n\n## A tool call through MCP\n%%seq Discovering and calling a tool.\nHost > Server: initialize (capabilities)\nHost > Server: tools/list\nServer -> Host: [create_issue: schema...]\nHost > Server: tools/call create_issue {...}\nServer -> Host: result: issue #42 created\n%%\n\nThe host puts the tool list in the model's context (lesson 08.03); when the model emits a tool call, the host routes it to the right server through its client.\n\n## Security\nMCP makes it easy to connect powerful tools, which raises the stakes:\n- **Prompt injection**: a resource or tool result (an email, a web page, an issue comment) can contain instructions aimed at the model.\n- **Tool poisoning**: a malicious server's tool descriptions can carry hidden instructions.\n- **Over-broad permissions**: a server with full account access can do damage if the model is tricked.\nMitigations: install servers you trust, grant least privilege, show users what will be called, require confirmation for writes, and treat everything that comes back as data, not instructions.\n\n## The integration count\nWithout a shared protocol, every AI application writes its own connector for every tool: N × M integrations. With one protocol, each application implements a client once and each tool implements a server once: N + M.\n\n%%widget mcpcount Integrations needed with custom connectors versus a shared protocol.\n\n## What travels on the wire\nMCP messages are JSON-RPC 2.0. After an initialisation handshake where client and server agree on a protocol version and capabilities, a tool call looks like this:\n\n```\n-> {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 1, \"method\": \"tools/list\"}\n<- {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 1, \"result\": {\"tools\": [\n     {\"name\": \"get_issue\", \"description\": \"Fetch one GitHub issue\",\n      \"inputSchema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"number\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}}, \"required\": [\"number\"]}}]}}\n\n-> {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 2, \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n    \"params\": {\"name\": \"get_issue\", \"arguments\": {\"number\": 812}}}\n<- {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 2, \"result\": {\"content\": [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"#812 Login fails on Safari ...\"}], \"isError\": false}}\n```\n\nThe host application turns the tools/list result into tool definitions for the model, and when the model asks for get_issue, the client sends tools/call and passes the content back as the tool result.\n\nTwo transports are defined: **stdio** for local servers launched as a subprocess, and **Streamable HTTP** for remote servers, with OAuth for authorisation.\n\n## Beyond tools\n| Primitive | Controlled by | Example |\n| Tools | The model decides when to call | create_ticket, run_query |\n| Resources | The application decides what to attach | A file, a database schema, a log |\n| Prompts | The user picks from a menu | \"Summarise this pull request\" template |\n| Sampling (client feature) | Server asks the host's model for a completion | A server that needs an LLM without its own API key |\n\n## Security in practice\n- **Only install servers you trust.** A server's tool descriptions go straight into the model's context and can contain hidden instructions (\"tool poisoning\").\n- **Least privilege.** A server with a token that can delete repositories gives that power to whatever text the model reads.\n- **Watch for the lethal combination**: access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, and a way to send data out. Any agent with all three can be steered by injected text into leaking data. Remove at least one.\n\n> Takeaway: MCP is a standard JSON-RPC protocol between AI apps (hosts and clients) and servers that expose tools, resources and prompts. It turns M x N integrations into M + N, and makes tool security a first-class concern.", "s": "Without a standard, each of M AI apps needs a custom integration for each of N services (M x N). MCP defines one protocol, so each service ships one server and each app one client implementation (M + N). Server primitives: tools (model-invoked actions, e.g. create_issue on GitHub), resources (app-controlled data read into context, e.g. a file or DB schema), and prompts (user-selected templates, e.g. a 'summarise this PR' workflow)."}, {"id": "09.04", "t": "Build an agent plan you can check", "f": "https://fanout.sh/ai-engineering/lessons/summary-pdf--ai-agents-and-mcp", "k": ["Exercise"], "x": [], "r": [["Anthropic: Building effective agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents", "Simple, composable patterns and when to add autonomy."], ["Anthropic: Writing effective tools for agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents", "Designing and evaluating tools with the agent."], ["Hamel Husain: Your AI Product Needs Evals", "https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/", "Building an evaluation loop for LLM products."]], "q": "Write a checkable plan for an agent that triages new GitHub issues: list its tools, stopping conditions, human checkpoints and how you'd evaluate it.", "n": ["A checkable plan states the goal, inputs, tools, steps, stop conditions, approvals and success criteria before any code.", "Tools: list each with its permission level (read vs write); keep writes behind confirmation.", "Stop conditions: success, max steps, budget, or a specific error that needs a human.", "Checkpoints: where a human reviews, especially before irreversible or external actions.", "Evaluation: a set of real past cases with expected outcomes; measure task success, steps, cost, and failure types from traces.", "Iterate from traces: read failed runs, classify the failure (context, tool, reasoning, budget), fix one thing, re-run the eval set."], "ak": "", "a": "## Why plan first\nAgents fail in ways that are hard to see: a wrong tool call 12 steps in, a loop that burns budget, an action that should have needed approval. Writing a plan you can check, before building, forces each of these decisions into the open. It also gives you the spec for your evaluation.\n\n## The plan template\n| Section | Question it answers |\n| Goal | What does done look like, in one sentence? |\n| Inputs | What does the agent receive, and from where? |\n| Tools | Which tools, read or write, and with what permissions? |\n| Steps | Expected path for a typical case (the agent may deviate) |\n| Stop conditions | When does it finish, give up, or escalate? |\n| Checkpoints | Which actions need a human first? |\n| Context plan | What's in the system prompt, what's fetched just in time? |\n| Evaluation | Which cases, which metrics, what pass rate? |\n\n## Worked example: GitHub issue triage\n! Example: triage new issues\n! Goal: every new issue gets a type label, a priority, a likely owner and, if it's a duplicate, a link to the original, within 10 minutes.\n! Inputs: the new issue's title, body and author, via an MCP GitHub server.\n! Tools (read): get_issue, search_issues, list_labels, read_file (for CODEOWNERS). Tools (write): add_labels, post_comment, assign.\n! Stop: labels applied and comment posted; or 15 tool calls; or confidence low, in which case label needs-triage and stop.\n! Checkpoints: closing as duplicate requires a maintainer's approval; the agent only suggests it.\n\n%%flow The triage agent's expected path.\nNew issue -(read)-> Issue text -(search similar)-> Possible duplicates -(classify)-> *Labels + priority -(CODEOWNERS)-> Owner -(write, low risk)-> Labels + comment\n%%\n\n**Context plan**\nSystem prompt: label definitions, priority rules, examples of three well-triaged issues. Fetched just in time: similar issues, CODEOWNERS. Tool results trimmed to titles and URLs.\n\n**Evaluation**\n- Take 100 past issues that maintainers already triaged; their labels are the expected output.\n- Metrics: label accuracy, priority within one level, duplicate precision (false duplicates are expensive), median tool calls and cost per issue.\n- Pass bar before launch, for example 90% type-label accuracy and zero wrong auto-closures (the agent can't close, by design).\n\n## Iterating from traces\nHamel Husain's advice for any LLM product applies: look at your data. Read failed traces and sort each failure:\n1. **Context**: it lacked information (missing label definitions).\n2. **Tool**: wrong tool, bad arguments, or an unhelpful tool output.\n3. **Reasoning**: right information, wrong conclusion.\n4. **Budget**: ran out of steps.\nFix the most common category, re-run the eval set, and compare. Anthropic's tool-writing guide recommends the same loop for tools: let the agent use them on realistic tasks, read where it struggles, and rewrite descriptions and outputs.\n\n## How many test cases is enough?\nA plan you can check needs an eval, and an eval needs enough cases to tell real changes from noise. Set your pass rate and number of cases below.\n\n%%widget evalcalc Margin of error for an eval pass rate, and cases needed to detect a change.\n\nRules of thumb that fall out of the maths:\n\n- 50 cases at an 80% pass rate gives roughly ±11 points of uncertainty. Good for catching big regressions, useless for comparing two similar prompts.\n- 400 cases gives about ±4 points.\n- To reliably detect a 5-point change between two variants at an 80% pass rate, you need around 1,000 cases per variant. Most teams don't have that, so they compare variants on the same cases (paired comparison) and read the cases that flipped.\n\n## What goes in the eval set\n1. **Real inputs**, sampled from logs or written by people who know the users. Synthetic cases are fine for coverage but under-represent how messy real input is.\n2. **The hard cases first**: every production failure becomes a test case.\n3. **Grading that matches the task**: exact match for labels, unit tests for code, a rubric scored by a judge model for open text. Check the judge against 50 or so human grades before trusting it.\n4. **Trajectory checks for agents**: not just \"was the final answer right\" but \"did it call the refund tool at most once\" and \"did it ask before deleting\".\n\n! Example: a triage agent's plan with checks attached\n! Goal: label new GitHub issues with type and area, and ask for missing reproduction steps.\n! Steps and checks: (1) read the issue, check that it has a title and body; (2) classify type, checked against 300 human-labelled issues, target 90%; (3) classify area, same set, target 85%; (4) if bug and no reproduction steps, draft a comment, checked by a judge rubric on 50 cases; (5) apply labels, allowed only from a fixed list, enforced in code.\n! Each step has a number, so when the agent gets worse you know which step to fix.\n\n> Takeaway: before building an agent, write down its goal, tools with permissions, stop conditions, checkpoints, context plan and evaluation set. Then improve it by reading traces and fixing the most common failure type.", "s": "Goal: label type and priority, suggest an owner and flag duplicates for each new issue. Tools: read (get_issue, search_issues, list_labels, read CODEOWNERS) and low-risk writes (add_labels, post_comment, assign); no close permission. Stop: labels and comment done, or a max of ~15 tool calls/budget, or low confidence -> label needs-triage. Checkpoints: closing duplicates or anything external needs maintainer approval. Evaluation: replay ~100 past triaged issues, measure label accuracy, priority accuracy, duplicate precision, steps and cost per issue; read failed traces and classify as context, tool, reasoning or budget failures before changing anything."}]}];
